<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE chapter SYSTEM "http://phoenix.int.thomsonreuters.com/dtd/pax.dtd">
<chapter date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
    <metadata field="right.running.head">
      <value>Union Structures</value>
    </metadata>
  </metadata.block>
  <front date.updated="0">
    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
      <label date.updated="0">Chapter</label>
      <designator date.updated="0">5</designator>
      <name date.updated="0">Union Structures</name>
    </outline.name.block>
  </front>
  <chapter.body date.updated="0">
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.B Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Finances</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">I</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Introduction</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:1</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Generally</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The structure of trade unions in Canada has been primarily shaped by their principal responsibility: the bargaining and administration of collective agreements. A typical union will have a central headquarters, a number of local branches and, depending on its geographic and membership size, an intermediate level of regional offices. The local will usually have the primary task of negotiating a collective agreement with its employer and, once it has been ratified and signed, administering the agreement during its contractual life. The union&apos;s headquarters will house the national executive, provide the administrative support for the organization, and offer technical support such as research, educational, legal and other services for bargaining and collective agreement administration. If the union is large enough, it might provide some of these services in a decentralized form through provincial or regional offices, or through industry divisions. In addition, the typical union will likely be affiliated both with a national union federation (such as the Canadian Labour Congress) as well as with regional and local pan-union councils that will pursue social policy and legislative goals important to the labour movement.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Since the end of the Second World War and the emergence of the contemporary system of labour law and industrial relations in Canada, unions have grown to become rather complex organizations. In the late 1940s, Canadian unions were usually small and poorly financed, employing only a few full-time staff, lacking almost any research, technical and administrative capacity, and heavily dependent upon voluntary assistance from their membership to function. Today, the larger unions in Canada have at least 100,000 members and several hundred locals each, as well as one or more layers of organization between the national office and the local. Collectively, they employ thousands of full-time staff to assist in the bargaining for better collective agreements and working conditions for their membership. The annual expenditures of each of the larger Canadian unions run into the tens of millions of dollars.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">However, while unions and the labour movement in Canada have grown enormously since the 1940s, it is important to keep in mind that, even today, they are miniscule in size and wealth when compared to Canadian corporations. For example, each one of the top 147 Canadian corporations in 1990 had more Canadian assets than all of the unions in Canada combined. See M. Sanger, &ldquo;The Myth of Big Labour,&rdquo; in <ital>The Facts</ital> (Ottawa, CUPE, December, 1992).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Their bargaining decisions have an influential effect on our economy, and their activism on social and political issues has decisively shaped contemporary Canadian values.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Canadian labour law speaks<trace.deleted/>  to the structure of unions in <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>three</trace> principal areas.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Labour legislation in Canada is largely silent about the internal organization of trade unions. This appears to have been a deliberate choice of law makers. When labour legislation does turn its attention to a trade union, its primary focus is on the union as an agent of bargaining unit employees, rather than on its structure and the interrelationship of its members. See <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RWDSU, Locals 414, 422, 440, 461 &amp; 1000 and New Dominion Stores (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="223" pre.rptr="19" ref.id="Id8b945908cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RWDSU, Locals 414, 422, 440, 461 &amp; 1000 and New Dominion Stores (Re)">RWDSU, Locals 414, 422, 440, 461 &amp; 1000 and New Dominion Stores (Re) (1993), 19 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 223 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, where the Ontario Labour Relations Board, on p. 234, stated that:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">The <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital> is primarily concerned about institutional collective bargaining relationships &mdash; the trade union in its role as <ital>statutory bargaining agent.</ital> The statute does not purport to regulate internal union affairs, nor does it prescribe any general code of &ldquo;democratic practice&rdquo; &hellip; Indeed, the statute is exceedingly (and we think, intentionally) sparse in respect of such matters, leaving them to be determined, for the most part, in accordance with the union&apos;s constitution.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> First, in <trace.deleted/>
                <trace>disputes</trace> between a parent union and its local, legal issues may arise either over the ownership of property and assets<trace.deleted/>. The issue of property and asset ownership usually arises when the membership of a local is severing its relationship with the parent, and the jurisdiction to determine proper title lies with the courts. Regarding trusteeships, there has only <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-6" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;11;30" nep=";;7" folio="5-5" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="4" sp=";;5" ?>been limited legislative regulation enacted in two jurisdictions in Canada, again leaving principal jurisdiction to the courts as the adjudicator of disputes between a local and its parent.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>third</trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>way</trace> Canadian labour law <trace>has shaped union structures  and internal union relationship </trace>has been <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>in through the regulation of </trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>mergers, transfers of jurisdiction over locals and other types of structural reorganizations. <trace>Here, </trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>much</trace> of the modern case law has emerged from labour relations boards, who do not regulate union mergers and reorganizations <ital>per se</ital>, but whose statutory authority to declare whether bargaining rights should be granted to a successor union is usually determinative of the success of a merger or reorganization. Labour boards in union successorship proceedings have been increasingly mindful both that unions are not run by lawyers, and that they have become complex organizations in structure and in decision-making. Accordingly, modern labour board rulings on union mergers and reorganizations have replaced the procedural rigidity that had influenced their earlier scrutiny of union constitutions with a more flexible approach that recognizes, in the words of one board decision, &ldquo;the realities of modern labour relations&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,007" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8b96ca08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada (Re)">RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada (Re) (1993), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;16,007 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 14,041.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In contrast, judicial rulings on mergers and reorganizations had, during the twentieth century, been largely guided by an antiquated legal approach towards trade unions that has missed most of the considerable organizational evolution of the labour movement in the post-war era. The common law did not catch up with these developments until the beginning of the twenty-first century. Instead, it continued to analogize a trade union to a Victorian gentlemen&apos;s club. Through this prism, a union is treated the same as an unincorporated association, whose members have voluntarily come together to pursue a set of objectives, based upon a number of rules that the members have agreed upon to govern themselves by. In this view, a trade union, like a gentlemen&apos;s club or other voluntary associations, has no legal existence separate and apart from its individual members, who are bound to each other by a complex web of contracts consisting of the terms of the union&apos;s governing documents. Hence, any change to the members&apos; rules deemed to be &ldquo;fundamental&rdquo; &mdash; such as a merger &mdash; required either explicit authority in the constitution or the <ital>unanimous</ital> consent of the members. As a consequence, a number of union mergers and reorganizations in the 1960s and 1970s failed the rigid scrutiny of the courts and labour boards.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">This common law theory made a poor fit with the complex structure of the modern trade union. Academics criticized the theory,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                            <ref ref.id="Icff6a2408cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" short="y">H.W. Arthurs, D.D. Carter, J. Fudge, H.J. Glasbeek and G. Trudeau, Labour Law and Industrial Relations in Canada, 4th ed. (Toronto, Butterworths, 1993), at p. 184</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> labour relations boards raised doubts about its applicability and usefulness,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para; 220-063" pre.rptr="98" ref.id="Id8b993b08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers (Re)">Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers (Re) (1998), 98 C.L.L.C. &para; 220-063, 42 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 1, 42 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 37 (O.L.R.B.), 2000 C.L.L.C. &para;220-018 (Ont. S.C.J. (Div. Ct.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Coleman and OTEU, Local 378 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1" pre.rptr="28" ref.id="Id8b993b18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Coleman and OTEU, Local 378 (Re)">Coleman and OTEU, Local 378 (Re) (1995) 28 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 1 (B.C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="National Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers Union of Canada and Melnor Manufacturing Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="360" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1989&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8b993b28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="National Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers Union of Canada and Melnor Manufacturing Ltd. (Re)">National Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers Union of Canada and Melnor Manufacturing Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1989&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Apr. 360</ref>
                          </cite>; <trace>
                            <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,038" pre.rptr="88" ref.id="Id8b993b38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)">IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re) (1988), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; Alta. L.R.B.R. 182, 88 C.L.L.C. &para;16,038 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                            </cite>
                          </trace>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="542" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1984&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8b993b48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)">UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1984&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 542</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and the courts finally pronounced it dead and dispatched.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ahenakew" p2="MacKay" post.rptr="314" pre.rptr="241" ref.id="Id8b9bac08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ahenakew v. MacKay">Ahenakew v. MacKay (2004), 241 D.L.R. (4th) 314, 187 O.A.C. 162 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Berry" p2="Pulley" post.rptr="493" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2002&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8b9bac18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Berry v. Pulley">Berry v. Pulley, &lsqb;2002&rsqb; 2 S.C.R. 493</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>Berry v. Pulley</ital>, the Supreme Court of Canada recognized unions as possessing a unique legal status separate from other unincorporated associations by virtue of their statutory powers and their pivotal role in industrial relations.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Berry" p2="Pulley" post.rptr="493" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2002&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8b9bac28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Berry v. Pulley">Berry v. Pulley, &lsqb;2002&rsqb; 2 S.C.R. 493</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Unlike voluntary associations, unions have the legislative authority to bargain exclusively on behalf of their members&apos; terms and conditions of employment. Unlike voluntary associations, unions have the statutory power to require non-members to pay membership fees, and they also have the statutory responsibility to represent these non-members. Unlike voluntary associations, they have only limited freedom over the admission and control of their members. And, almost certainly, the size, the structure and responsibilities of a contemporary union greatly outmatch any unincorporated association.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Consequently, the law on union mergers and successorships has been ushered into the modern age. Antiquated rules regarding the requirement for unanimity and a perfect adherence to the constitutional process no longer stand as impediments to union mergers and successorships. Unions are now accepted in the common law as mature, responsible and complex institutions, having outgrown the fetters that the law on voluntary associations had imposed.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-8" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2024;12;31" nep=";;9" folio="5-7" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;7" ?>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.D Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">II</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">The Parent-Local Relationship<footnote date.updated="0">
              <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
              <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">For additional in-depth analysis of this subject, please see the article &ldquo;Is a union&apos;s status affected by its contractual relationship with its members?&rdquo; in the Issues in Focus section.</para.text>
                </para>
              </footnote.body>
            </footnote>
          </name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.D Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:2</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Judicial Approaches</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The legal relationship between a parent union and its locals revolves predominantly around the constitution, by-laws and charter of the union. In the absence of legislation regulating the internal structure of Canadian unions,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Where legislation has established the parent union as the sole possessor of bargaining rights, the parent may appoint a local to act as its agent to negotiate a local collective agreement, but the parent does not thereby surrender its bargaining rights: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Re Kirwan" p1.pp="Re" post.rptr="557" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2001&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8b9bac38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Re Kirwan">Re Kirwan, &lsqb;2001&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 557</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the responsibility has fallen to the courts to adjudicate parent-local disputes. In its interpretative role, the courts have consistently held that the constitution and related documents of a union form a contractual relationship between the parent and the local, and that disputes between the two &mdash; whether involving property rights, the boundaries of authority or some other internal issue of rights and liabilities &mdash; are to be resolved by applying the principles of contract law to the language of these governing documents.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20241205">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Brown" p2="Hanley" post.rptr="1112" pre.rptr="2018" ref.id="I62e1ce30a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="Brown v. Hanley">Brown v. Hanley, 2018 ONSC 1112 (Ont. S.C.J.), affirmed 2019 ONCA 395 (Ont. C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Kinnear" p2="Hanley" post.rptr="3003" pre.rptr="2017" ref.id="Id8b9e1d08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Kinnear v. Hanley">Kinnear v. Hanley, 2017 CarswellOnt 3003, 2017 ONSC 1165</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="Deveau" post.rptr="44" pre.rptr="19" ref.id="Id8b9e1d18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. Deveau">CUPE v. Deveau (1976), 19 N.S.R. (2d) 44 (S.C.), affd 19 N.S.R. (2d) 24 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Raymond" p2="Doherty" post.rptr="99" pre.rptr="49" ref.id="Id8b9e1d28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Raymond v. Doherty">Raymond v. Doherty (1965), 49 D.L.R. (2d) 99, &lsqb;1965&rsqb; 1 O.R. 593 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Sellor&apos;s" p2="Woodruff" post.rptr="646" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1925&rsqb; 4" ref.id="Id8b9e1d38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Sellor&apos;s v. Woodruff">Sellor&apos;s v. Woodruff, &lsqb;1925&rsqb; 4 D.L.R. 646, 57 O.L.R. 582 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Morrison" p2="Ingles" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8b9e1d48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Morrison v. Ingles">Morrison v. Ingles, &lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 50 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">While the courts once were ready to assume a wide jurisdiction over intra-union disputes involving parents and locals,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Local 1571, ILA" p2="ILA" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1951&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8ba08e08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Local 1571, ILA v. ILA">Local 1571, ILA v. ILA, &lsqb;1951&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 50, 26 M.P.R. 129 (N.B.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Dawkins" p2="Antrobus" post.rptr="615" pre.rptr="17" ref.id="Id8ba08e18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Dawkins v. Antrobus">Dawkins v. Antrobus (1881), 17 Ch. D. 615 (C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> they have in recent times acknowledged a more restrained role. In <ital>Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn.</ital>, the British Columbia Supreme Court ruled in 2014 that: &ldquo;The courts have a limited role with respect to disputes involving the internal affairs&rdquo; of unions and voluntary associations, and accepted that it is not to act as a court of appeal for such internal decisions. The Court went on to say that it should only intervene if the internal decision was made without jurisdiction, it was made against the plain rules of the constitution, or it was made in bad faith or contrary to the rules of natural justice.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145" p2="AFM" post.rptr="2661" pre.rptr="2014" ref.id="Id8ba08e28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145 v. AFM">Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145 v. AFM, 2014 CarswellBC 2661, 2014 BCSC 1713, at paras. 10&ndash;13, reversed on other grounds 2015 CarswellBC 1128, 2015 BCCA 171</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145" p2="AFM" post.rptr="3154" pre.rptr="2015" ref.id="Id8ba08e38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145 v. AFM">Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145 v. AFM, 2015 CarswellBC 3154, 2015 BCSC 1989</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">As a study in the institutional distribution of power, unions are a paradox. Most commonly, a union&apos;s organizational authority is highly concentrated in the hands of the parent. Here rests the union&apos;s executive, quasi-judicial <trace>functions </trace>and much of its financial powers, along with most of the inherent and residual rights under the constitution and bylaws. Yet, in most unions, the local has considerable autonomy in the sphere of collective bargaining, which is the organization&apos;s primary purpose. It is the local that is often endowed with the bargaining unit certification from the labour relations board, and, likewise, it is the local that usually has the authority to ratify or reject a tentative collective agreement with the employer.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">However, since internal union disputes between parents and locals are adjudicated on the language of the union&apos;s governing documents, and as these documents frequently concentrate organizational authority at the centre, the courts in Canada have regularly upheld the predominant constitutional authority of the parent. This judicial pattern has been criticized by some labour law observers as frustrating democratic principles central to collective bargaining in favour of an unimaginative reliance on contract principles better suited to the rules of unincorporated clubs.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">D. Carter, G. England, B. Etherington &amp; G. Trudeau, <ital>Labour Law in Canada</ital> (5th ed.) (Toronto: Butterworths, 2002).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Yet, while this pattern of Canadian case law may not necessarily be an accurate reflection of the real distribution of power and responsibility within a union, it is usually a faithful application by the judiciary of the explicit location of institutional authority in a union&apos;s constitution. Indeed, it would appear to be much the better policy &mdash; particularly in light of the historically difficult relationship between unions and the courts<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See generally <cite finder="legacy" type="periodical">
                            <ref normalpub="lr" ref.id="Icff6c9508cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" reftext="T.S. Kuttner, &ldquo;Constitution as Covenant: Labour Law, Labour Boards, and the Courts from the Old to the New Dispensation&rdquo;">T.S. Kuttner, &ldquo;Constitution as Covenant: Labour Law, Labour Boards, and the Courts from the Old to the New Dispensation&rdquo; (1988), 13 Queen&apos;s L. J. (No. 2) 32</ref>
                        </cite>; J.M. Weiler, &ldquo;The Role of Law in Labour Relations&rdquo; in <ital>Labour Law and Urban Law in Canada</ital>, I. Bernier and A. Lajoie, eds. (Toronto, University of Toronto Press, 1985); Lord Wedderburn, <ital>The Worker and the Law</ital>, 3rd ed. (London, Penguin, 1986); J.A.G. Griffith, <ital>The Politics of the Judiciary</ital>, 3rd ed. (London, Fontana, 1985); J.B. Atleson, <ital>Values and Assumptions in American Labour Law</ital> (Amherst, University of Massachusetts Press, 1983); C.L. Tomlins, <ital>The State and the Unions: Labour Relations, Law, and the Organized Labor Movement in America, 1880&ndash;1960</ital> (New York, Cambridge University Press, 1985).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> &mdash; to restrict the judiciary to giving effect to the specific language of a union constitution, rather <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-10" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2024;12;31" nep=";;13" folio="5-9" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;9"?>than allowing it a broad discretion to determine the character of union government in its own fashion.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The courts have also been criticized for introducing the misleading &ldquo;breath of life&rdquo; doctrine into their analysis of parent-local relationships.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                            <ref ref.id="Icff6f0608cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" short="y">A.W.R. Carrothers, E.E. Palmer, and W.B. Rayner, Collective Bargaining Law in Canada, 2nd ed. (Toronto, Butterworths, 1986), at pp. 737&ndash;9</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="legacy" type="periodical">
                            <ref normalpub="lr" ref.id="Icff6f0618cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" reftext="E.F. Whitmore, &ldquo;Case and Comment&rdquo;">E.F. Whitmore, &ldquo;Case and Comment&rdquo; (1952), 30 Can. Bar Rev. 1058</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> This doctrine holds that since a parent union embodies the legal capacity to create a local &mdash; that is, to give it the original &ldquo;breath&rdquo; to start life &mdash; then, should the parent subsequently terminate its relationship with its creation, the local, starved of its legal sustenance for life, would die.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">For examples of the courts applying the doctrine, see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="R." p2="Labour Relations Board, Ex parte Gorton-Pew (New Brunswick) Ltd." post.rptr="621" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1952&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8ba57008cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="R. v. Labour Relations Board, Ex parte Gorton-Pew (New Brunswick) Ltd.">R. v. Labour Relations Board, Ex parte Gorton-Pew (New Brunswick) Ltd., &lsqb;1952&rsqb; 2 D.L.R. 621, 102 C.C.C. 387 (N.B.C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Jubilee Lodge No. 6" p2="Carmen&apos;s Council, Section &ldquo;A&rdquo;" post.rptr="318" pre.rptr="56" ref.id="Id8ba57018cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Jubilee Lodge No. 6 v. Carmen&apos;s Council, Section &ldquo;A&rdquo;">Jubilee Lodge No. 6 v. Carmen&apos;s Council, Section &ldquo;A&rdquo; (1920), 56 D.L.R. 318, &lsqb;1920&rsqb; 3 W.W.R. 781 (Man. K.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn." p2="McPherson" post.rptr="672" pre.rptr="8" ref.id="Id8ba57028cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn. v. McPherson">Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn. v. McPherson (1912), 8 D.L.R. 672, 46 N.S.R. 417 (S.C.), affd 16 D.L.R. 853n, 50 S.C.R. 157, leave to appeal to P.C. refused Aug. 4, 1914</ref>
                          </cite>. In <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman and Barrett" p2="Bruce" post.rptr="146" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1950&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8ba57038cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman and Barrett v. Bruce">Lakeman and Barrett v. Bruce, &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 146, &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 1209 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>, Farris C.J.S.C., on p.149, gave a classic recitation of the doctrine:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">This union, therefore, came into existence only when it was so granted its charter by the national body. The national body alone gave the breath of life to and created the union and so when the charter was revoked, the body giving the breath of life to the union, took the same away and then the union no longer existed. It was dead or defunct.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The critics rightly argue that the doctrine ignores the primacy of the union&apos;s governing documents. That is, the &ldquo;breath of life&rdquo; doctrine only has meaning if the particular terms of the union&apos;s constitution and allied documents actually specify that a severance of the parent-local relationship destroys the local&apos;s existence. But if the governing documents specify something else, or are silent, then the doctrine has no place or meaning. The local may have been spawned by the parent, but its fate upon the dissolution of the relationship, far from being automatic, is entirely dependent upon the particular contractual terms between the two.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">For a discussion on the spectrum of constitutional arrangements possible between a parent and a local, see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU" p2="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU" post.rptr="161" pre.rptr="64" ref.id="Id8ba7e108cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU">IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU (2000), 64 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept./Oct. 891</ref>
                          </cite> at paras. 89&ndash;92. Also see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="28" pre.rptr="171" ref.id="Id8ba7e118cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re)">Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re) (2008), 171 L.A.C. (4th) 28 (B.C. Arb. Bd., Larson), applying board review award (2007), 167 L.A.C. (4th) 404 (B.C.L.R.B.), which set aside preliminary award 160 L.A.C. (4th) 314 (B.C. Arb. Bd., Larson)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU" p2="" post.rptr="871" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2004&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ba7e128cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU">IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU, &lsqb;2004&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept./Oct. 871</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.i.E Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Constitution&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:3</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Separate Personalities of Parent and Local</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In modern Canadian trade union law, a vexing question that had been without a definitive answer was the precise legal nature of the relationship between a parent union and its affiliated locals.<trace>0.5</trace>In modern Canadian trade union law, a vexing question that had been without a definitive answer was the precise legal nature of the relationship between a parent union and its affiliated locals.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">0.5 On these questions, see also &dblsect;&dblsect;1:8 &amp; 1:19.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Are a parent union and its local two separate and distinct personalities in law? Conversely, do they share the same personality? Or do they have a legal status that is separate for some purposes and unitary for others? While the governing constitutional documents and labour statutes will determine the relationship in any particular circumstance, the dominant trend has been to accept union locals as separate entities in law.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="20211028">
                        <para.text date.updated="20241205">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Penegal Trim &amp; Supply Ltd. and LIUNA (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="3834" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1998&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ba7e138cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Penegal Trim &amp; Supply Ltd. and LIUNA (Re)">Penegal Trim &amp; Supply Ltd. and LIUNA (Re), &lsqb;1998&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 3834</ref>
                          </cite>, where the Ontario Board stated, in para. 81, that &ldquo;the &lsquo;parent union&rsquo; and the &lsquo;local union&rsquo; are separate and distinct organizations for legal and labour relations purposes. They are two DIFFERENT trade unions. The statute also makes the same distinction; and, in recent years has subjected that parent-local relationship to specific regulation&rdquo;. Also see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Construction Council of Ontario" p2="Toronto (City)" post.rptr="18371" pre.rptr="2023" ref.id="I62e5c5d0a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="IBEW, Construction Council of Ontario v. Toronto (City)">IBEW, Construction Council of Ontario v. Toronto (City), 2023 CarswellOnt 18371 (Ont. L.R.B. (C.G.))</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Gamble" p2="UA, Local 663" post.rptr="1043" pre.rptr="2021" ref.id="I5ddd0b40370e11ec80e0c0b599422da1" title="Gamble v. UA, Local 663">Gamble v. UA, Local 663, 2021 CarswellOnt 1043 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="28" pre.rptr="171" ref.id="Id8ba7e148cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re)">Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re) (2008), 171 L.A.C. (4th) 28 (B.C. Arb. Bd., Larson), applying board review award (2007), 167 L.A.C. (4th) 404 (B.C.L.R.B.), which set aside preliminary award 160 L.A.C. (4th) 314 (B.C. Arb. Bd., Larson)</ref>
                          </cite>, and <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="ILA, Local 273" p2="Maritime Employers&apos; Assn." post.rptr="289" pre.rptr="89" ref.id="Id8baa5208cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="ILA, Local 273 v. Maritime Employers&apos; Assn.">ILA, Local 273 v. Maritime Employers&apos; Assn. (1978), 89 D.L.R. (3d) 289 (S.C.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>. These rulings suggest that <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CJA, Local 1338" p2="Bradley" post.rptr="104" pre.rptr="174" ref.id="Id8baa5218cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CJA, Local 1338 v. Bradley">CJA, Local 1338 v. Bradley (1999), 174 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 104 (P.E.I.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>, is no longer good law.<trace.deleted/>This more flexible and realistic approach is preferable in law and industrial relations practice, allowing as it does for the myriad range of activities assumed separately and in unison by a parent and its affiliates. A functional analogy to illustrate the dominant/subordinate legal relationship between a parent and a local would be the relationship between a province and a municipality: the province creates municipalities by statute, the municipality has to govern within the boundaries of the provincial grant of power, and the province has the authority to dissolve the municipality<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>. The municipality cannot &ldquo;go it alone&rdquo; and does not exist without the provincial grant.</trace>
                          <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>W</trace>ithin these confines, <trace>however,</trace> the municipality has a legal personality that is distinct and separate for a number of purposes from the province that created it. </para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Northwest Territories Court of Appeal, in <ital>Fullowka v. Royal Oak Ventures Inc.</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Fullowka" p2="Royal Oak Ventures Inc." post.rptr="411" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2008&rsqb; 7" ref.id="Id8baa5228cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Fullowka v. Royal Oak Ventures Inc.">Fullowka v. Royal Oak Ventures Inc., &lsqb;2008&rsqb; 7 W.W.R. 411 (N.W.T.C.A.), affd 315 D.L.R. (4th) 577 (S.C.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> has recognized that, for the purpose of suing unions, union locals can be separate entities from their parent unions. It listed four policy reasons for this conclusion:</para.text>
                <list list.style="block" date.updated="0">
                  <list.item date.updated="0">
                    <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <designator date.updated="0">(a)</designator>
                    </name.block>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">the evolution of unions as separate suable entities is based on a statutory foundation, and the statutes generally grant bargaining certificates to the locals, not the national unions.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </list.item>
                  <list.item date.updated="0">
                    <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <designator date.updated="0">(b)</designator>
                    </name.block>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">local unions generally have their own executives and decision-making structures, and in accordance with the principles in <ital>Berry v. Pulley</ital> they should be recognized as separate entities.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </list.item>
                  <list.item date.updated="0">
                    <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <designator date.updated="0">(c)</designator>
                    </name.block>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">locals can enter into contracts, the most significant of course being collective agreements. Those agreements should be enforceable only by and against the local, not the national union or other affiliated locals.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </list.item>
                  <list.item date.updated="0">
                    <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <designator date.updated="0">(d)</designator>
                    </name.block>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">if locals and national unions are not separate entities, it would seem to follow that they have no separate rights to hold property. Not only would local and national entities be responsible for each other&apos;s obligations, it would appear that each local would be responsible for all the obligations of the other locals. This is a commercially unreasonable result.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                  <ref p1="Fullowka" p2="Royal Oak Ventures Inc." post.rptr="411" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2008&rsqb; 7" ref.id="Id8bacc308cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Fullowka v. Royal Oak Ventures Inc.">Fullowka v. Royal Oak Ventures Inc., &lsqb;2008&rsqb; 7 W.W.R. 411 (N.W.T.C.A.), affd 315 D.L.R. (4th) 577 (S.C.C.)</ref>
                              </cite>, at para. 140.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </list.item>
                </list>
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">The Supreme Court of Canada, in upholding the decision of the Northwest Territories Court of Appeal in <ital>Fullowka</ital>, ruled that the parent union and its union local in these circumstances were separate legal entities on the basis that the local held the status of exclusive bargaining agent for its members, it enjoyed a high degree of autonomy under the prevailing union constitution, and the subsequent merger agreement between its parent union and the CAW permitted the local to retain its assets and its autonomous status.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Fullowka" p2="Royal Oak Ventures Inc." post.rptr="411" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2008&rsqb; 7" ref.id="Id8bacc318cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Fullowka v. Royal Oak Ventures Inc.">Fullowka v. Royal Oak Ventures Inc., &lsqb;2008&rsqb; 7 W.W.R. 411 (N.W.T.C.A.), affd 315 D.L.R. (4th) 577 (S.C.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at paras. 119&ndash;133. At para. 126, the Supreme Court of Canada stated: &ldquo;&lsqb;N&rsqb;othing in the constitution or the merger agreement supports the view that the local and the national unions are simply branches of the same entity. Each has its own management structure, areas of responsibility and assets and liabilities which are treated as such consistently in both documents.&rdquo;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                  <trace>In the adjudication of disputes over successorship, trusteeship, and the allocation of assets, boards and courts still tend to deny that locals have existence and personality wholly independent of their parent, given their shared constitutional foundation.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">4.2 See &sect;&sect;5:6, 5:9, 5:33&ndash;5:36.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>4.2 Nonetheless, in line with <ital>Fullowka</ital>, </trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>there is strong tendency to grant</trace> a parent and its local <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>separate legal</trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>standing , and distinct identities in relations with third parties , including in the allocation of</trace> certification<trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>and</trace> collective bargaining,<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">4.3 See &sect;&sect;1:19 &amp; 5:10.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                  <trace>4.3</trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>challenge<trace>s</trace> to the imposition of a trusteeship,<trace>4.4<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">4.4 See below, &sect;5:10, &ldquo;Union Structures and Certification&rdquo;</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                  </trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>the <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>fulfilment of</trace> regulatory duties<trace>,</trace>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4.50</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">In <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Piechotta" p2="United Food and Commercial Workers Canada Union, Local 401" post.rptr="904" pre.rptr="2025" ref.id="Id1a81cf0fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="Piechotta v. United Food and Commercial Workers Canada Union, Local 401">Piechotta v. United Food and Commercial Workers Canada Union, Local 401, 2025 CarswellAlta 904, 2025 ABKB 241</ref>
                        </cite>, the court held that the parent union was a separate legal entity, and could not be held liable for alleged violations of Alberta&apos;s <ital>Occupational Health and Safety Act</ital> committed by the local, despite the parent&apos;s power under the constitution to impose trusteeship on the local.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                  <trace.deleted/>finding liability in a civil suit,<trace.deleted/>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">For an additional example of circumstances where the union parent and its local were assigned separate legal personalities by a court, see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="ATU" p2="ICTU" post.rptr="662" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1997&rsqb; 5" ref.id="Id8bacc328cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="ATU v. ICTU">ATU v. ICTU, &lsqb;1997&rsqb; 5 W.W.R. 662 (Alta. Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> defending against an allegation that the duty of fair representation has been breached,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5.50</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Rattray" p2="Unifor National" post.rptr="25" pre.rptr="2020" ref.id="I62e7e8b0a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="Rattray v. Unifor National">Rattray v. Unifor National, 2020 CarswellSask 25 (Sask. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>
                          <trace>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="Narayanan" p2="CUPE" post.rptr="10" pre.rptr="2026" title="Narayanan v. CUPE">Narayanan v. CUPE, 2026 SKLRB 10, 2026 CarswellSask 45 (Sask. L.R.B.)</ref>
                            </cite>
                          </trace>
                          <trace>.</trace>
                        </para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> or <trace.deleted/>against an employer grievance<trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>.</trace>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="28" pre.rptr="171" ref.id="Id8bacc338cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re)">Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re) (2008), 171 L.A.C. (4th) 28 (B.C. Arb. Bd., Larson), applying board review award (2007), 167 L.A.C. (4th) 404 (B.C.L.R.B.), which set aside preliminary award 160 L.A.C. (4th) 314 (B.C. Arb. Bd., Larson)</ref>
                          </cite>. However, for an example of a union parent and its local being found to constitute a single legal entity in the context of an employer grievance respecting damages arising from tortious activity by the local, see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canada Post Corp. and CUPW (CPC-0500002) (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="262" pre.rptr="160" ref.id="Id8bacc348cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canada Post Corp. and CUPW (CPC-0500002) (Re)">Canada Post Corp. and CUPW (CPC-0500002) (Re) (2007), 160 L.A.C. (4th) 262 (Can. Lab., Jolliffe)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> and yet have a merged and singular legal personality for other purposes, such as internal union governance. This more flexible and realistic approach is preferable in law and industrial relations practice, allowing as it does for the myriad range of activities assumed separately and in unison by a parent and its affiliates. A functional analogy to illustrate the dominant/subordinate legal relationship between a parent and a local would be the relationship between a province and a municipality: the province creates municipalities by statute, the municipality has to govern within the boundaries of the provincial grant of power, and the province has the authority to dissolve the municipality; but, within these confines, the municipality has a legal personality that is distinct and separate for a number of purposes from the province that created it.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.trace="20221125" date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The modern functional approach to<trace.deleted/> the <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>relations between the status and personality</trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>of</trace> a union parent and <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>those of its (s)</trace> local was illustrated in <ital>Robson and University of New Brunswick, Re</ital>.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Robson and University of New Brunswick, Re" post.rptr="212" pre.rptr="2022" ref.id="I04ea0660696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="Robson and University of New Brunswick, Re">Robson and University of New Brunswick, Re, 2022 CarswellNB 212 (N.B. Labour &amp; Employment Bd.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The issue before the New Brunswick Labour and Employment Board was whether a union member could bring a complaint against the parent union as well as her local union, on the grounds that the parent union&apos;s servicing representative had assisted the local union in the negotiations for the purported discriminatory provision in the collective agreement. The parent union sought to be removed as a named respondent in the legal action, citing a provision in the union&apos;s national constitution that the actions of a union local could not bind the parent union, unless specifically authorized by the national president. The New Brunswick Board dismissed the complaint against the parent union, ruling that when national servicing representatives are assisting a union local in collective bargaining or in any other duties relating to the local, they <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>do so as</trace> representatives of the local union, not the parent. It noted that national representatives, in these circumstances, have only advisory roles, not decision-making roles. <ital>Robson</ital> followed earlier decisions by the Nova Scotia Supreme Court,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Romard" p2="C.U.P.E., Local 3264" post.rptr="268" pre.rptr="2000" ref.id="I04ea5480696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="Romard v. C.U.P.E., Local 3264">Romard v. C.U.P.E., Local 3264, 2000 CarswellNS 268 (N.S. S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the New Brunswick Court of Queen&apos;s Bench<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="LeBlanc" p2="CUPE, Local 558" post.rptr="85" pre.rptr="2016" ref.id="I04eaa2a0696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="LeBlanc v. CUPE, Local 558">LeBlanc v. CUPE, Local 558, 2016 CarswellNB 85, 2016 NBCA 11</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and the Newfoundland and Labrador Labour Board,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ryan" p2="St. John&apos;s (City)" post.rptr="207" pre.rptr="2007" ref.id="I04eaf0c0696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="Ryan v. St. John&apos;s (City)">Ryan v. St. John&apos;s (City), 2007 CarswellNfld 207 (N.L. Bd. of Inquiry)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> which had ruled that a parent union and its union locals pos<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-14" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2024;12;31" nep=";;15" folio="5-13" hyphenated="y" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;13" ?>sessed separate legal personalities for the purposes of liability arising from the actions of national representatives.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">This approach is not unanimous. See <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref date="2011" p1="Canada Post Corp." p2="C.U.P.W." post.rptr="4" pre.rptr="204" ref.id="I04eb17d0696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="Canada Post Corp. v. C.U.P.W.">Canada Post Corp. v. C.U.P.W. (2011), 204 L.A.C. (4th) 4, 2011 CarswellNat 877 (Can. Arb.), application for judicial review refused 2012 CarswellOnt 10164, 2012 ONSC 4110 (Ont. Div. Ct.), additional reasons 2012 CarswellOnt 13090, 2012 ONSC 5566 (Ont. Div. Ct.)</ref>
                          </cite>, where Arbitrator Michel Picher ruled that a parent union could not escape financial liability for wildcat strikes led by several of its locals. He stated: &ldquo;&lsqb;Unions&rsqb; must be taken to accept that they are responsible for the actions of their respective officers, be it at the national, regional or local level.&rdquo; Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref date="1993" p1="O&apos;Sullivan" p2="Amcon Management Ltd." post.rptr="417" pre.rptr="19" ref.id="I04eb3ee0696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="O&apos;Sullivan v. Amcon Management Ltd.">O&apos;Sullivan v. Amcon Management Ltd. (1993), 19 C.H.R.R. 417 (Ont. Bd. of Inquiry-H.R.)</ref>
                        </cite>, in the circumstances of a human rights complaint.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In <ital>Toronto District School Board v. CUPE Local 4400</ital>, an arbitrator rejected an argument that grievors who had previously signed minutes of settlement ending their employment could claim top-up payments pursuant to an agreement between the Crown and the parent union remedying a constitutional claim. Because the latter agreement had been implemented by retroactively amending the terms of the collective agreement between the local and the employer, the arbitrator held that the top up was covered by the waiver of all claims against the employer that the grievors had consented to.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">12 <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Toronto District School Board" p2="CUPE Local 4400" pre.rptr="2026" post.rptr="28912" title="Toronto District School Board v. CUPE Local 4400">Toronto District School Board v. CUPE Local 4400, 2026 CanLII 28912, 2026 CarswellOnt 4834 (Ont. Arb., Gedalof)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.D Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:4</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Judicial Deference to Central Authority</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">An important feature in the constitutions of Canadian unions is the delineation of the relationship between the parent and the local. Typically, the constitutional distribution of power gives the parent union an extremely broad authority, including the right: to charter as a local any group of employees wishing to join the union,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Canadian Union of Public Employees (CUPE), Constitution (<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>2025</trace>), articles 3.2, <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>12</trace>; Public Service Alliance of Canada (PSAC), Constitution (<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>2024</trace>), sections 8&ndash;11; United Steelworkers of America (USWA), Constitution (<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>2025</trace>), article 7(1), (2); Unifor Constitution (<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>2025</trace>), articles 5.B.<trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>7</trace>, 15.A.2.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to require the local to abide by the governing documents of the union,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace>CUPE (2025), article 12.3</trace>
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC</trace>; <trace>(2024),   sections 9.1., 11.1</trace>
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA (2025), articles 7(4)&ndash;(5)</trace>; <trace.deleted/>; Unifor Constitution (<trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>202 5</trace>), articles 15.A.3, <trace.deleted/>15.I.3 and 16.4.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to settle internal jurisdictional questions between locals,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 3.5; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, sections 9(2), 27; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 4(25); <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>Unifor Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 7.C.3.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to manage and control the central finances of the union,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>8</trace>.3, <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>9</trace>; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, section 24; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 4(13), 14; <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>Unifor Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 15.I.4, <trace.deleted/>15.H.7 and 15.H.9.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to act as an internal appeal body on intra-union disputes,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, appendix B.XI; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, sections 7(2)(f), 25(4); <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 4(25), 13; <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>Unifor Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 18.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to investigate, suspend, place in trusteeship and/or revoke the charter of a local,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 3.3, 7.7, 7.8; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, sections 8(6), 10(2), 25(8); <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 7(3), (9); <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>Unifor Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 15.H.9, 15.M and 18.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to remove a local union officer or representative from office for good cause,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 7.7, 7.8; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, section 25; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 9(2), (3); <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>Unifor Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 15.H.9, 15. M and 18.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and to assume possession, either as owner or as trustee, of the assets and property of a local upon its dissolution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 13.2, appendix B.1.3; <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 9(9).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Locals in Canadian unions, generally, are given only a restricted range of constitutional powers, including the right: to elect their own local officers,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="1">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, appendix B.II; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, sections 9(4)(a), 9(5)(f), 11(4)(9a), 11(5)(f); <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 7(8); Unifor Constitution (2017), article 15.B.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to select and send delegates to the national convention,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="1">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 6; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, sections 9(4)(b), 19; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 6(2), (10); <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>Unifor Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 6.B.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to introduce resolutions and constitutional amendments at the national convention,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="1">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 6.11; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, sections 9(13), 11(4)(b); <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 6(12); <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>Unifor Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 6.C.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to appeal decisions of the national executive made against the local to the national convention,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 7.7; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, section 11.7; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, articles 4(25), 9(5); <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>Unifor Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 6.D.2-6.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> a limited authority to develop their own by-laws,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="1">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>12</trace>.3; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, sections 9(1), 11(1); <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 7(5); <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>Unifor Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 15.A.3-5.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and to conduct internal trials on disciplinary matters arising out of local affairs.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE, Constitution (2025)</trace>, appendix B.XI; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC, Constitution (2024)</trace>, section 25; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USWA, Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 13; <trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>Unifor Constitution (2025)</trace>, article 18.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">When Canadian courts have had the opportunity to read a union constitution in a parent-local dispute over property rights or boundaries of authority, they have on most occasions upheld the dominant authority of central power. This has included circumstances not only where the constitution had made an explicit reference to the division of internal authority,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn. (Canadian Federation of Musicians, Local 145)" p2="AFM" post.rptr="2142" pre.rptr="2016" ref.id="Id8bb1a508cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn. (Canadian Federation of Musicians, Local 145) v. AFM">Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn. (Canadian Federation of Musicians, Local 145) v. AFM, 2016 CarswellBC 2142, 2016 BCSC 1427</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145" p2="AFM" post.rptr="1128" pre.rptr="2015" ref.id="Id8bb41608cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145 v. AFM">Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145 v. AFM, 2015 CarswellBC 1128, 2015 BCCA 171</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338" p2="Bradley" post.rptr="11" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1999&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bb41618cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338 v. Bradley">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338 v. Bradley, &lsqb;1999&rsqb; P.E.I.J. No. 11 (S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Stamos" p2="Belanger" post.rptr="&para;14,043" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8bb41628cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Stamos v. Belanger">Stamos v. Belanger (1994), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;14,043, 57 C.P.R. (3d) 390 (Ont. Gen. Div.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="Deveau" post.rptr="44" pre.rptr="19" ref.id="Id8bb41638cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. Deveau">CUPE v. Deveau (1976), 19 N.S.R. (2d) 44 (S.C.), affd 19 N.S.R. (2d) 24 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McMillin" p2="Yandell" post.rptr="398" pre.rptr="22" ref.id="Id8bb41648cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McMillin v. Yandell">McMillin v. Yandell (1971), 22 D.L.R. (3d) 398, &lsqb;1972&rsqb; 1 O.R. 146 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Raymond" p2="Doherty" post.rptr="610" pre.rptr="34" ref.id="Id8bb41658cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Raymond v. Doherty">Raymond v. Doherty (1962), 34 D.L.R. (2d) 610, &lsqb;1962&rsqb; O.R. 929 (H.C.J.), affd 49 D.L.R. (2d) 99, &lsqb;1965&rsqb; 1 O.R. 593 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="International Nickel Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="381" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1950&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8bb41668cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="International Nickel Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">International Nickel Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 1 D.L.R. 381, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; O.R. 765 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman and Barrett" p2="Bruce" post.rptr="146" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1950&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bb41678cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman and Barrett v. Bruce">Lakeman and Barrett v. Bruce, &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 146, &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 1209 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman" p2="Bruce" post.rptr="527" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bb68708cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman v. Bruce">Lakeman v. Bruce, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 527, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 1 W.W.R. 886 (B.C.C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn." p2="McPherson" post.rptr="672" pre.rptr="8" ref.id="Id8bb68718cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn. v. McPherson">Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn. v. McPherson (1912), 8 D.L.R. 672, 46 N.S.R. 417 (S.C.), affd 16 D.L.R. 853n, 50 S.C.R. 157, leave to appeal to P.C. refused Aug. 4, 1914</ref>
                          </cite>. For a contemporary example of a labour relations board upholding the dominant authority of the parent, see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Hillside Townhouses Ltd. and Hospitality, Commercial and Service Employees Union, Local, 73 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1075" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1995&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bb68728cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Hillside Townhouses Ltd. and Hospitality, Commercial and Service Employees Union, Local, 73 (Re)">Hillside Townhouses Ltd. and Hospitality, Commercial and Service Employees Union, Local, 73 (Re), &lsqb;1995&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. August 1075</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> but also where the constitution was silent.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Tiercon Corp." p2="Workers United Ontario Council" post.rptr="4953" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2010&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bb68738cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council">Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council, &lsqb;2010&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4953</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Burnley Nelson Rossendale and District Textile Workers&apos; Union" p2="Amalgamated Textile Workers&apos; Union" post.rptr="298" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1986&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bb68748cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Burnley Nelson Rossendale and District Textile Workers&apos; Union v. Amalgamated Textile Workers&apos; Union">Burnley Nelson Rossendale and District Textile Workers&apos; Union v. Amalgamated Textile Workers&apos; Union, &lsqb;1986&rsqb; I.R.L.R. 298 (Q.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Jubilee Lodge No. 6" p2="Carmen&apos;s Council, Section &ldquo;A&rdquo;" post.rptr="318" pre.rptr="56" ref.id="Id8bb68758cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Jubilee Lodge No. 6 v. Carmen&apos;s Council, Section &ldquo;A&rdquo;">Jubilee Lodge No. 6 v. Carmen&apos;s Council, Section &ldquo;A&rdquo; (1920), 56 D.L.R. 318, &lsqb;1920&rsqb; 3 W.W.R. 781 (Man. K.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>. But see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW, Local 1252" p2="ECWU, Local 44" post.rptr="385" pre.rptr="80" ref.id="Id8bb68768cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW, Local 1252 v. ECWU, Local 44">UFCW, Local 1252 v. ECWU, Local 44 (1987), 80 N.S.R. (2d) 385, 200 A.P.R. 385 (S.C.), revd 81 N.S.R. (2d) 198, 203 A.P.R. 198 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Morrison" p2="Ingles" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8bb68778cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Morrison v. Ingles">Morrison v. Ingles, &lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 50 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> An early example of this judicial predisposition towards central author<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-16" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2024;12;31" nep=";;17" folio="5-15" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;15" ?>ity in the industrial relations arena is found in <ital>Lakeman v. Bruce.</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">17</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman" p2="Bruce." post.rptr="527" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bb8f808cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman v. Bruce.">Lakeman v. Bruce., &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 527, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 1 W.W.R. 886 (B.C.C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> A parent union and its local disagreed on the ownership of the union assets controlled by the local, after dissident members of the local joined another union. In upholding the parent&apos;s property claim to the assets of the local, the British Columbia Court of Appeal cited not only the union&apos;s constitution but also the general responsibility of the parent to protect the broader interests of the union:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">If it appears the Local may be using its funds for purposes which are not &ldquo;legitimate&rdquo;, there is a great deal to be said for the contention that the National Union has a two-fold supervisory interest, first to determine whether the purpose is or is not legitimate, and secondly to take adequate steps to stop it, if the purpose is not legitimate. It would scarcely be possible for a national organization to carry on effectively, without such interest and authority &hellip; The interest of the National Union lies in seeing that its Locals conduct themselves in a manner to advance and not to retard its aims and object.</para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
                <para.text date.updated="0">The court added:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">That must mean that Locals shall carry on in accordance with the constitution, and avoid conduct harmful to the welfare of the National Union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">18</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="Lakeman" p2="Bruce." post.rptr="527" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bbb6908cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman v. Bruce.">Lakeman v. Bruce., &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 527, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 1 W.W.R. 886 (B.C.C.A.)</ref>
                            </cite>, at p. 533.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">This judicial deference towards central authority in intra-union disputes has produced a small body of useful guidelines for analyzing these conflicts. Although each case has turned on the particular wording of the constitution before the court, most unions in Canada share very similar constitutional provisions to govern parent-local relations. Among other precepts, the courts have imposed a fiduciary duty on the local executive to safeguard the assets under the local&apos;s control that are legally the property of the parent,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Leduc" p2="International Brotherhood Electrical Workers" post.rptr="19179" pre.rptr="2019" ref.id="Id8bbb6918cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers">Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers, 2019 CarswellOnt 19179, 2019 ONSC 6507</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Tiercon Corp." p2="Workers United Ontario Council" post.rptr="4953" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2010&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bbb6928cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council">Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council, &lsqb;2010&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4953</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CJA" p2="British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters" post.rptr="177" pre.rptr="100" ref.id="Id8bbb6938cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CJA v. British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters">CJA v. British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters (2000), 100 A.C.W.S. (3d) 177 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338" p2="Bradley" post.rptr="11" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1999&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bbb6948cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338 v. Bradley">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338 v. Bradley, &lsqb;1999&rsqb; P.E.I.J. No. 11 (S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="Deveau" post.rptr="44" pre.rptr="19" ref.id="Id8bbb6958cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. Deveau">CUPE v. Deveau (1976), 19 N.S.R. (2d) 44 (S.C.), affd 19 N.S.R. (2d) 24 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McMillin" p2="Yandell" post.rptr="398" pre.rptr="22" ref.id="Id8bbb6968cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McMillin v. Yandell">McMillin v. Yandell (1971), 22 D.L.R. (3d) 398, &lsqb;1972&rsqb; 1 O.R. 146 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Raymond" p2="Doherty" post.rptr="99" pre.rptr="49" ref.id="Id8bbdda08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Raymond v. Doherty">Raymond v. Doherty (1965), 49 D.L.R. (2d) 99, &lsqb;1965&rsqb; 1 O.R. 593 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman and Barrett" p2="Bruce" post.rptr="146" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1950&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bbdda18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman and Barrett v. Bruce">Lakeman and Barrett v. Bruce, &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 146, &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 1209 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman" p2="Bruce" post.rptr="527" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bbdda28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman v. Bruce">Lakeman v. Bruce, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 527, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 1 W.W.R. 886 (B.C.C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn." p2="McPherson" post.rptr="672" pre.rptr="8" ref.id="Id8bbdda38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn. v. McPherson">Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn. v. McPherson (1912), 8 D.L.R. 672, 46 N.S.R. 417 (S.C.), affd 16 D.L.R. 853n, 50 S.C.R. 157, leave to appeal to P.C. refused Aug. 4, 1914</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> including the delivery of membership lists and the ongoing receipt of union dues from employers.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">20</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW, Local 1252" p2="Cashin" post.rptr="620" pre.rptr="217" ref.id="Id8bbdda48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin">UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin (2002), 217 D.L.R. (4th) 620, 216 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 41, 84 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161 (Nfld. C.A.), affg 149 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 112 (Nfld. S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Flowing from this, defecting or involuntarily merged locals are not permitted to obstruct the parent&apos;s right to recover the union&apos;s assets by dividing up the property among the local&apos;s members under the guise of a membership rebate or a cost-of-living allowance.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">21</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Leduc" p2="International Brotherhood Electrical Workers" post.rptr="19179" pre.rptr="2019" ref.id="Id8bbdda58cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers">Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers, 2019 CarswellOnt 19179, 2019 ONSC 6507</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="Deveau" post.rptr="44" pre.rptr="19" ref.id="Id8bbdda68cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. Deveau">CUPE v. Deveau (1976), 19 N.S.R. (2d) 44 (S.C.), affd 19 N.S.R. (2d) 24 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn." p2="McPherson" post.rptr="672" pre.rptr="8" ref.id="Id8bc04b08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn. v. McPherson">Grand Council Provincial Workmen&apos;s Assn. v. McPherson (1912), 8 D.L.R. 672, 46 N.S.R. 417 (S.C.), affd 16 D.L.R. 853n, 50 S.C.R. 157, leave to appeal to P.C. refused Aug. 4, 1914</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Neither is the local executive permitted to place union funds that it controls in a trust fund as a means of putting these funds beyond the reach of the parent.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">22</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="LCUC" p2="CUPW" post.rptr="450" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1994&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8bc04b18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="LCUC v. CUPW">LCUC v. CUPW, &lsqb;1994&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 450, 14 Alta. L.R. (3d) 372, 146 A.R. 184 (Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, Andrekson J. stated that:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">In my view it is settled law that the money of a Local belongs to the Union as a collective entity and not to either the Local as a branch of that entity or to individual members of that entity.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                        <para.text date.updated="0">The Saskatchewan Labour Relations Board, in <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE, Local 600-5" p2="CUPE, Sask Div." post.rptr="179" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1997&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bc04b28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE, Local 600-5 v. CUPE, Sask Div.">CUPE, Local 600-5 v. CUPE, Sask Div., &lsqb;1997&rsqb; Sask. L.R.B.R. 179</ref>
                        </cite>, has stated at p. 187:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">We do not think there is any general principle which suggests that the assets of a trade union or of a local union &ldquo;belong&rdquo; to the individual members of the organization in a proprietary sense. The funds which are accumulated over time represent contributions of previous members, as well as those presently there, and must be seen as funds belonging to the organization, not to individuals.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> As well, dissidents attempting to lead a local&apos;s membership out of the incumbent union have been prevented <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-18" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2024;12;31" nep=";;19" folio="5-17" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;17" ?>from using the funds and resources of the local to finance the defection to, or start up of, another union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">23</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="LeBlanc" p2="CUPE, Local 558" post.rptr="84" pre.rptr="2016" ref.id="Id8bc2bc08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="LeBlanc v. CUPE, Local 558">LeBlanc v. CUPE, Local 558, 2016 CarswellNB 84, 2016 NBCA 11</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW, Local 1252" p2="Cashin" post.rptr="620" pre.rptr="217" ref.id="Id8bc2bc18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin">UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin (2002), 217 D.L.R. (4th) 620, 216 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 41, 84 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161 (Nfld. C.A.), affg 149 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 112 (Nfld. S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338" p2="Bradley" post.rptr="11" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1999&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bc2bc28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338 v. Bradley">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338 v. Bradley, &lsqb;1999&rsqb; P.E.I.J. No. 11 (S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McMillin" p2="Yandell" post.rptr="398" pre.rptr="22" ref.id="Id8bc2bc38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McMillin v. Yandell">McMillin v. Yandell (1971), 22 D.L.R. (3d) 398, &lsqb;1972&rsqb; 1 O.R. 146 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman and Barrett" p2="Bruce" post.rptr="146" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1950&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bc2bc48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman and Barrett v. Bruce">Lakeman and Barrett v. Bruce, &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 146, &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 1209 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman" p2="Bruce" post.rptr="527" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bc52d08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman v. Bruce">Lakeman v. Bruce, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 527, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 1 W.W.R. 886 (B.C.C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural and Ornamental Ironworkers" p2="" post.rptr="1487" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1982&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bc52d18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural and Ornamental Ironworkers">Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural and Ornamental Ironworkers, &lsqb;1982&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1487</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Further, a parent may succeed, by way of the federal <ital>Trade-marks Act</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">24</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Trade-marks Act</ital>, R.S.C. 1985, c. T-13.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> in preventing union dissidents from using any of the names, trade marks or insignias of the union that would be likely to cause confusion or misrepresentation.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">25</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Stamos" p2="Belanger" post.rptr="&para; 14,043" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8bc52d28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Stamos v. Belanger">Stamos v. Belanger (1994), 94 C.L.L.C. &para; 14,043, 57 C.P.R. (3d) 390 (Ont. Gen. Div.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters and CJA (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="B43/2008" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2008&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bc52d38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters and CJA (Re)">British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters and CJA (Re), &lsqb;2008&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B43/2008</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Even though a majority of a local&apos;s membership may have quit the parent union for another, the local may still have status within the union if the governing constitution provides, as many in Canada do, for a continued life as long as it maintains a minimum number of members. In such situations, the local&apos;s continuing status provides the parent with an additional argument to prevent dissidents from claiming a right to the union assets currently in the local&apos;s hands.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">26</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="LeBlanc" p2="CUPE, Local 558" post.rptr="84" pre.rptr="2016" ref.id="Id8bc52d48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="LeBlanc v. CUPE, Local 558">LeBlanc v. CUPE, Local 558, 2016 CarswellNB 84, 2016 NBCA 11</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338" p2="Bradley" post.rptr="11" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1999&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bc52d58cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338 v. Bradley">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338 v. Bradley, &lsqb;1999&rsqb; P.E.I.J. No. 11 (S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="International Nickel Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="381" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1950&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8bc52d68cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="International Nickel Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">International Nickel Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 1 D.L.R. 381, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; O.R. 765 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman and Barrett" p2="Bruce" post.rptr="146" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1950&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bc79e08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman and Barrett v. Bruce">Lakeman and Barrett v. Bruce, &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 146, &lsqb;1950&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 1209 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman" p2="Bruce" post.rptr="527" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bc79e18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman v. Bruce">Lakeman v. Bruce, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 527, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 1 W.W.R. 886 (B.C.C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>. A typical &ldquo;continuation of local status&rdquo; provision is found in the current Constitution of the Canadian Union of Public Employees, at Appendix B.1.2:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">No Local Union may be dissolved while there are twelve members in good standing therein who desire to continue its existence. The funds of the Local Union cannot be divided among individual members and can only be utilized for valid Local Union purposes &hellip;</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In addition, the courts have rejected the argument that the union merger and amalgamation provisions found in all Canadian labour relations statutes override a union constitution so as to permit a defecting local to sequester assets that would otherwise revert to the parent.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">27</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McMillin" p2="Yandell" post.rptr="398" pre.rptr="22" ref.id="Id8bc79e28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McMillin v. Yandell">McMillin v. Yandell (1971), 22 D.L.R. (3d) 398, &lsqb;1972&rsqb; 1 O.R. 146 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.trace="20241205" date.updated="20241205">
                <para.text date.updated="0">While Canadian law on trade unions is overwhelming in support of the principle that the relationship between a parent union and its locals is hierarchical and based on subordination (subject as always to the wording of the union constitution), the New Brunswick Court of Appeal entertained the argument, without deciding, that the relationship could be one of affiliation.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">28</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="C.U.P.E." p2="Hachey" post.rptr="41" pre.rptr="2011" ref.id="I62f27000a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="C.U.P.E. v. Hachey">C.U.P.E. v. Hachey, 2011 NBCA 41 (N.B. C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.D Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:5</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Procedural Rights in Parent-Local Disputes</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In parent-local disputes, the courts have generally insisted that procedural rights provided by the constitution be respected by both the parent and the local. Procedural steps such as ensuring that the proper internal authority has made the decision, that constitutional time requirements are respected, and the principles of fairness and due process are observed are common rights enforced by the courts.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Local 1571, ILA" p2="ILA" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1951&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bca0f08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Local 1571, ILA v. ILA">Local 1571, ILA v. ILA, &lsqb;1951&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 50, 26 M.P.R. 129 (N.B.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Morrison" p2="Ingles" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8bca0f18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Morrison v. Ingles">Morrison v. Ingles, &lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 50 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In other words, fairness must be ensured where an internal hearing is required or provided; where it is not, the procedural process in the constitution will be strictly construed, although this will usually amount to a lesser obligation upon the union than the strictures of natural justice.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners, Local 1338" p2="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America" post.rptr="140" pre.rptr="148" ref.id="Id8bca0f28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners, Local 1338 v. United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners, Local 1338 v. United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America (1997), 148 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 140 (P.E.I.S.C.), overturned on other grounds 148 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 152 (P.E.I.C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Thus, while the courts have acknowledged the right of dissatisfied members to leave a union, and the right of a parent to protect the broader interests of the union, they have subjected these rights to the dispute-resolution steps established by the parties.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Early decisions by the courts had cautioned parent unions that a general-power provision in a constitution would not necessarily excuse the disregard of procedural rights. In <ital>Smith v. <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-20" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2024;12;31" nep=";;21" folio="5-19" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;19" ?>Cardwell</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Smith" p2="Cardwell" post.rptr="78" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1946&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8bca0f38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Smith v. Cardwell">Smith v. Cardwell, &lsqb;1946&rsqb; 1 W.W.R. 78 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> a parent attempted to resolve a dispute with one of its locals by removing the local officers and ordering new elections. The pretext for intervention arose over the failure of the local executive to submit required monthly reports. The British Columbia Supreme Court ruled that, in the absence of a fair hearing, the parent&apos;s decisions were precipitous, and could not be saved by vague general-power provisions in the constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Smith" p2="Cardwell" post.rptr="78" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1946&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8bcc8008cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Smith v. Cardwell">Smith v. Cardwell, &lsqb;1946&rsqb; 1 W.W.R. 78 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 84, MacFarlane J. wrote that:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">So far as the clause itself is concerned, I do not think that this clause means that the executive of the federation may do what they please irrespective of the provisions of the constitution, and then come to the Court and say that they have decided what their powers are and that the convention has approved them, and that therefore the Court has nothing to say about it. It certainly does not mean that rights not given by the constitution of the federation which has been adopted by the locals may be overridden simply by the process of the convention declaring that they shall be so.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Similarly, in <ital>Morrison v. Ingles</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Morrison" p2="Ingles" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8bcc8018cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Morrison v. Ingles">Morrison v. Ingles, &lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 50 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> an earlier panel of the same court held that the parent had no inherent right, in the absence of a specific constitutional power, to revoke the charter of a local. The British Columbia court went on to rule that even where the power of a parent to revoke a local&apos;s charter did exist, it would intervene in the decision if the local was not accorded natural justice during the internal hearing process. Although many Canadian unions today have specifically granted the parent an extensive authority to intervene in the affairs of a local, including the power to revoke a charter, the local&apos;s right to fair procedures where the constitution provides for a hearing in such circumstances has survived.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners, Local 1338" p2="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America" post.rptr="140" pre.rptr="148" ref.id="Id8bcc8028cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners, Local 1338 v. United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners, Local 1338 v. United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America (1997), 148 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 140 (P.E.I.S.C.), overturned on other grounds 148 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 152 (P.E.I.C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The British Columbia Labour Relations Board has ruled that, where the dissolution and reconstitution of a local by a parent union was a disciplinary act, the union was statutorily required to exercise natural justice. In <ital>Pereira, Cordoni, Nicol, Masunda and Higgs v. Union of Psychiatric Nurses</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Pereira, Cordoni, Nicol, Masunda and Higgs" p2="Union of Psychiatric Nurses" post.rptr="&para;220-038" pre.rptr="97" ref.id="Id8bcc8038cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Pereira, Cordoni, Nicol, Masunda and Higgs v. Union of Psychiatric Nurses">Pereira, Cordoni, Nicol, Masunda and Higgs v. Union of Psychiatric Nurses (1997), 97 C.L.L.C. &para;220-038 (B.C.L.R.B.), clarified &lsqb;1997&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. 142/97</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Board stated that it respected the political autonomy of unions and agreed that a parent union has the right to restructure its subordinate bodies as it sees fit. However, where the restructuring was, in essence, the implementation of a penalty against the local and its officers, then the actions of the parent union must conform to s. 10 of the <ital>Labour Relations Code.</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">R.S.B.C. 1996, c. 244.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> That is, the parent union must provide the local with notice, and with a proper hearing before an unbiased internal tribunal, even though in this case the governing constitution had allowed the parent to dissolve a local &ldquo;in its absolute discretion&rdquo;:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">Natural justice is to be applied to matters with respect to the Union&apos;s Constitution. In order to dissolve a Local, the Union must, by its Constitution, determine that a Local has been in contravention of the Union&apos;s policies and principles.</para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                  <omit.para align="c" date.updated="0">&hellip;</omit.para>
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">The inclusion of the words &ldquo;absolute discretion&rdquo; imply that due process is not required. To accept this interpretation would be to accept that a union can &ldquo;contract out&rdquo; of the statutory requirement that the principles of natural justice be followed in matters with respect to its Constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="Pereira, Cordoni, Nicol, Masunda and Higgs" p2="Union of Psychiatric Nurses" post.rptr="&para;220-038" pre.rptr="97" ref.id="Id8bcef108cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Pereira, Cordoni, Nicol, Masunda and Higgs v. Union of Psychiatric Nurses">Pereira, Cordoni, Nicol, Masunda and Higgs v. Union of Psychiatric Nurses (1997), 97 C.L.L.C. &para;220-038 (B.C.L.R.B.), clarified &lsqb;1997&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. 142/97</ref>
                            </cite>, at paras. 165 and 167.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
                <para.text date.updated="0">As well, where a parent union has suspended the rights of a local, or deposed its executive, the local has a right to retain counsel to represent it during any internal appeal procedures. The British Columbia Board has said that a denial of counsel representation in such serious circumstances is a breach of natural justice.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IATSE, Local 348" p2="IATSE, Local 348" post.rptr="79" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2001&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bcef118cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IATSE, Local 348">IATSE, Local 348, &lsqb;2001&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 79</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In British Columbia, the statutory requirements for natural justice also apply when a union local is protesting against the procedures used by the parent union in a forced merger. This is true even in circumstances where the parent is not a &ldquo;trade union&rdquo;, as defined by the <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, but where the two parties nevertheless share the same constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CJA, Local 1998, Re" p2="" post.rptr="437" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bd16208cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CJA, Local 1998, Re">CJA, Local 1998, Re, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 437, 1999 CarswellBC 3499</ref>
                          </cite>. However, also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters and CJA (McCarron) (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="114" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2005&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bd16218cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters and CJA (McCarron) (Re)">British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters and CJA (McCarron) (Re), &lsqb;2005&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 114</ref>
                        </cite>, where the British Columbia Board decided that an international parent union and its officers could be a &ldquo;person&rdquo; within the meaning of s. 5 of the <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, R.S.B.C. 1996, c. 244, which prohibits the intimidation or coercion of anyone who exercises a right under the Code.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The British Columbia Labour Relations Board went on to rule that a statutory breach of natural justice would include a situation where the parent union failed to provide proper notice of a <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-22" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2024;12;31" nep=";;23" folio="5-21" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;21" ?>forced merger to an affected local, and then failed to cure the original breach when it neither provided an opportunity to cross-examine on issues of material fact during an internal appeal, nor did it provide reasons for its appeal decision.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local No. 1998" p2="" post.rptr="77" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bd16228cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local No. 1998">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local No. 1998, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 77</ref>
                          </cite>. But also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UA, Local 170 and UA, Local 324 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="161" pre.rptr="112" ref.id="Id8bd16238cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UA, Local 170 and UA, Local 324 (Re)">UA, Local 170 and UA, Local 324 (Re) (2005), 112 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161 (B.C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, where the British Columbia Labour Relations Board held that the high standards of natural justice were met in a case where the parent union restructured local jurisdiction only after it provided the affected union locals with notice, a genuine opportunity to be heard, reasons for its decision, and an appeal process that was capable of curing any initial procedural defects.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20241205">A particular feature in the British Columbia <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital> defines a trade union as a local or provincial organization, or a provincial branch of a national or international union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20241205">
                        <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, R.S.B.C. 1996, c. 244, s. 1(1).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> This definition conspicuously excludes national and international unions headquartered elsewhere from its jurisdiction.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Construction Labour Relations Assn. (British Columbia)" p2="P.A.T." post.rptr="530" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1974&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8bd16248cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Construction Labour Relations Assn. (British Columbia) v. P.A.T.">Construction Labour Relations Assn. (British Columbia) v. P.A.T., &lsqb;1974&rsqb; 1 Can. L.R.B.R. 530, &lsqb;1974&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 115/74 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> As a consequence, national and international organizations with head offices outside of British Columbia are not subject to the <ital>Code</ital>, and complaints filed against them for purportedly breaching the statutory natural justice provisions when revoking the charter of a British Columbia local have been dismissed on the procedural grounds that the Labour Relations Board has no jurisdiction to review the parent union&apos;s decision.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20241205">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Harris and Unifor, Re" p2="" post.rptr="262" pre.rptr="5" ref.id="Id8bd3d308cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Harris and Unifor, Re">Harris and Unifor, Re (2017), 5 C.L.R.B.R. (3d) 262, 2017 CarswellBC 2158 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Brooker and Unifor, Re" p2="" post.rptr="315" pre.rptr="3" ref.id="Id8bd3d318cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Brooker and Unifor, Re">Brooker and Unifor, Re (2017), 3 C.L.R.B.R. (3d) 315, 2017 CarswellBC 1616 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; and <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Construction Labour Relations Assn. (British Columbia)" p2="P.A.T." post.rptr="530" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1974&rsqb; 1" ref.id="I62f492e0a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="Construction Labour Relations Assn. (British Columbia) v. P.A.T.">Construction Labour Relations Assn. (British Columbia) v. P.A.T., &lsqb;1974&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 115/74, &lsqb;1974&rsqb; 1 Can. L.R.B.R. 530 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, the BC Board may have jurisdiction over an international union&apos;s actions where the local, national and/or international union share the same constitution, regardless of whether the internal decision-maker was ultimately the parent body.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15.50</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Seder and UA, Local 516, Re" post.rptr="88" pre.rptr="2022" ref.id="I62f4b9f0a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="Seder and UA, Local 516, Re">Seder and UA, Local 516, Re, 2022 BCLRB 88 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="C.J.A., Local 1998, Re" post.rptr="3499" pre.rptr="1999" ref.id="I62f4b9f1a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="C.J.A., Local 1998, Re">C.J.A., Local 1998, Re, 1999 CarswellBC 3499 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20241205">The British Columbia <ital>Human Rights Code</ital> has a broader definition of trade unions, which includes parent unions headquartered outside of the province.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Human Rights Code</ital>, R.S.B.C. 1996, c. 210, S.1.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>Othman</ital>, the BC Human Rights Tribunal ruled that a human rights complaint should not include a national union with its head offices in Ontario as a named defendant, because the local union was the certified bargaining agent for the member and therefore the proper focus of the complaint.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">17</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Othman" p2="Fairmont Hotels" post.rptr="4500" pre.rptr="2006" ref.id="Id8bd3d328cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Othman v. Fairmont Hotels">Othman v. Fairmont Hotels, 2006 CarswellBC 4500, 2006 BCHRT 537 (B.C. Human Rights Trib.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> And in <ital>Holmes</ital>, the Tribunal held that a human rights complaint against a BC union local could proceed, where the decision in question had been made by the parent union headquartered in the United States (and which had not been named in the complaint), but implemented by the union local.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">18</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Holmes" p2="Iron Workers, Local 97" post.rptr="2750" pre.rptr="2017" ref.id="Id8bd64408cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Holmes v. Iron Workers, Local 97">Holmes v. Iron Workers, Local 97, 2017 CarswellBC 2750, 2017 BCHRT 216 (B.C. Human Rights Trib.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="20211028">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The procedural sword, however, cuts two ways. While the parent is obliged to honour the internal constitutional procedures when invoking its authority over a local, likewise, locals and dissatisfied members wishing to challenge their status or to leave the union are also required to follow the union&apos;s procedures to affect their goals.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lakeman" p2="Bruce" post.rptr="527" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bd64418cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lakeman v. Bruce">Lakeman v. Bruce, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 527 at p. 533, &lsqb;1949&rsqb; 1 W.W.R. 886 (B.C.C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>Janzen v. Page</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">20</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Janzen" p2="Page" post.rptr="62" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1955&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bd64428cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Janzen v. Page">Janzen v. Page, &lsqb;1955&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 62, 15 W.W.R. 276 (B.C.C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> an attempt by ousted officers of a local to challenge the parent&apos;s decision by relying on resolutions passed at a support meeting of the membership failed. The British Columbia Court of Appeal stated that the resolutions were not passed at a properly constituted meeting of the local and, in any event, they were a repudiation of the superior constitutional authority of the parent, and were thus null and void. On similar grounds, the Nova Scotia Supreme Court in <ital>Deveau</ital> held that the defecting members&apos; attempt to place the union assets beyond the reach of the parent was defective because, contrary to the local&apos;s by-laws, the requisite notice of motion had not been presented at the prior membership meeting and, as well, the funds had been distributed for non-union purposes.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">21</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="Deveau" post.rptr="44" pre.rptr="19" ref.id="Id8bd64438cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. Deveau">CUPE v. Deveau (1976), 19 N.S.R. (2d) 44 at pp. 59&ndash;60 (S.C.), affd 19 N.S.R. (2d) 24 (C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, Local 772 v. United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">22</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, Local 772" p2="United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada" post.rptr="33" pre.rptr="2013" ref.id="Id8bd8b508cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, Local 772 v. United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada">United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, Local 772 v. United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, 2013 NBCA 33, 2013 CarswellNB 213</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the New Brunswick Court of Appeal ruled that a parent union&apos;s decision to consolidate several provincial locals <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-24" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2024;12;31" nep=";;25" folio="5-23" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;23" ?>was justified on the facts, and the dissident union local had been shown the requisite procedural fairness and had failed to utilize the available remedies under the governing constitution.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The content of the procedural rights to be honoured by parents and locals alike has been considered on several occasions by the courts. While a parent is required to ensure that a substantial intervention into the affairs of a local is in accordance with natural justice where a hearing is required or provided, the courts, keeping in mind that a union is a voluntary organization of laypersons, have not demanded a high or onerous standard. Good faith by the decision-making body is expected.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">23</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America" p2="British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters" post.rptr="132" pre.rptr="1" ref.id="Id8bd8b518cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America v. British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America v. British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters (2003), 1 Admin. L.R. (4th) 132, 121 A.C.W.S. (3d) 38 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Local 1571, ILA" p2="ILA" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1951&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bd8b528cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Local 1571, ILA v. ILA">Local 1571, ILA v. ILA, &lsqb;1951&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 50, 26 M.P.R. 129 (N.B.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Morrison" p2="Ingles" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8bd8b538cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Morrison v. Ingles">Morrison v. Ingles, &lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 50 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Notice of the accusations against the local must be provided in sufficient detail, and the local or the dissident local officers must be given a fair opportunity to meet the charges.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">24</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Morrison" p2="Ingles" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8bdb2608cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Morrison v. Ingles">Morrison v. Ingles, &lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 50 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, the fact that the parent&apos;s executive officers have arrived at a tentative opinion on the issue in dispute prior to the hearing may not be sufficient to taint the process on the grounds of bias.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">25</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Local 1571, ILA" p2="ILA" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1951&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bdb2618cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Local 1571, ILA v. ILA">Local 1571, ILA v. ILA, &lsqb;1951&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 50, 26 M.P.R. 129 (N.B.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>. At p. 60, the New Brunswick Supreme Court favourably quoted from an early English decision &mdash; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Maclean" p2="Workers&apos; Union" post.rptr="602" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1929&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8bdb2628cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Maclean v. Workers&apos; Union">Maclean v. Workers&apos; Union, &lsqb;1929&rsqb; 1 Ch. 602</ref>
                        </cite> at pp. 625&ndash;6 &mdash; on the latitude to be given with regard to the issue of bias in a domestic tribunal:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">&ldquo;&hellip; when members of a club expel another member under their rules, generally speaking, they all have a small pecuniary interest in the matter. Moreover the members of a domestic tribunal have other interests in the decision which may well render them something less than impartial. They have, for example, the interests of their association or body to protect, and may properly regard these interests as a matter of great importance. In many cases the tribunal is necessarily entrusted with the duty of appearing to act as prosecutors as well as that of judges; for there is no one else to prosecute.&rdquo;</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                        <para.text date.updated="0">But see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Morrison" p2="Ingles" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8bdb2638cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Morrison v. Ingles">Morrison v. Ingles, &lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 50 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Where the constitution requires a complaining local to exhaust internal appeals before seeking a judicial remedy, a breach of natural justice in the course of the process will not normally permit the complainant to have a speedier access to the courts.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">26</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Local 1571, ILA" p2="ILA" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1951&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8bdb2648cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Local 1571, ILA v. ILA">Local 1571, ILA v. ILA, &lsqb;1951&rsqb; 3 D.L.R. 50 at pp. 62&ndash;3, 26 M.P.R. 129 (N.B.S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>. But see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Morrison" p2="Ingles" post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8bdb2658cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Morrison v. Ingles">Morrison v. Ingles, &lsqb;1920&rsqb; 2 W.W.R. 50 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 67.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> And where the local union&apos;s position would require a departure from the standard procedures in the governing constitution, the parent union&apos;s authority has been upheld.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">27</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPW, Local 626" p2="CUPW" post.rptr="1952" pre.rptr="2008" ref.id="Id8bdd9708cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPW, Local 626 v. CUPW">CUPW, Local 626 v. CUPW, 2008 CarswellOnt 1952 (Ont. S.C.J.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.D Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:6</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Secession Attempts</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Although constitutional perfection is not required for a subordinate union body to successfully separate from a parent union, a failure to substantially comply with the governing constitution will invariably doom a unilateral secession attempt. Moreover, if the governing constitution is silent, explicitly and implicitly, on the capacity of a local or internal union body to unilaterally secede from the parent, then a disaffiliation application under the successor sections of the labour legislation will likely fail. In <ital>Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Tiercon Corp." p2="Workers United Ontario Council" post.rptr="4953" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2010&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bdd9718cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council">Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council, &lsqb;2010&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4953</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Ontario Labour Relations Board ruled that the absence of any express or implied secession provision in the governing constitution meant that a provincial joint council could not unilaterally separate from the international parent union. In this case, the constitution of the parent union did not expressly provide for secession, and the Board read other constitutional provisions &mdash; which penalized officers who fostered secession &mdash; to mean that disaffiliation was impermissible under the governing documents.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Court of Justice has stated that, in union secession matters, it will demand strict adherence to the union constitution. In <ital>Stamos v. Belanger</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Stamos" p2="Belanger" post.rptr="&para;14,043" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8bdd9728cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Stamos v. Belanger">Stamos v. Belanger (1994), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;14,043, 57 C.P.R. (3d) 390 (Ont. Ct. (Gen. Div.))</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> a union local attempted to withdraw from the international parent and take with it the property and assets in its possession. The union meeting where the dissidents claimed to have received a majority vote in favour of secession had failed to provide adequate notice to the membership of the withdrawal resolution. Adams J. ruled that, in such matters, the courts would require &ldquo;the most rigorous notice and voting procedures set out in the constitution&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Stamos" p2="Belanger" post.rptr="&para; 14,043" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8be00808cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Stamos v. Belanger">Stamos v. Belanger (1994), 94 C.L.L.C. &para; 14,043, 57 C.P.R. (3d) 390 (Ont. Ct. (Gen. Div.))</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 12,275.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Should the union constitution be silent on such requirements, a high procedural standard would be judicially implied. Nor, Adams J. went on to state, could a local attempting to withdraw <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-26" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2024;12;31" nep=";;29" folio="5-25" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;25"?>rely on historically lax procedures at local meetings to justify its procedural defects and so sanctify its attempt to breakaway; a local&apos;s attempt to secede is a fundamentally different matter than the regular flow of business normally conducted at a local meeting, and will be judged on a different and more exacting standard.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.D Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:7</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Good Faith</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">When exercising constitutional powers in a conflict between a parent union and one of its subordinate bodies, each union body must utilize good faith in applying its discretionary authority under the constitution. The good faith requirement in union constitutional issues has been given a broad meaning by the courts to exclude not only acts of bad faith, but also actions that are extraneous or collateral to the governing documents of the union. For example, in <ital>United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America v. British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America" p2="British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters" post.rptr="132" pre.rptr="1" ref.id="Id8be00818cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America v. British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America v. British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters (2003), 1 Admin. L.R. (4th) 132, &lsqb;2003&rsqb; B.C.J. No. 629 (S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> a parent union demanded, against a backdrop of tension between the two union bodies over the parent&apos;s decision to restructure the council, that one of its provincial councils surrender membership records that were in its possession. Under the union constitution, the parent union had the authority to request these documents in order to obtain an accurate inventory of union assets. Notwithstanding this, the British Columbia Supreme Court held that the parent union&apos;s request for the membership documents was intended to provide the parent with a political advantage in the ongoing intra-union conflict. Since this motivation went beyond the administrative purpose of the constitutional provision, the parent union&apos;s request was struck down by the Supreme Court.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.i.B Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Constitution&mdash;Conflict with legislation</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:8</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Statutory Regulation</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Given that the courts have the primary jurisdiction over the regulation of internal union affairs, labour relations boards have regularly demurred from assuming the authority to decide legal complaints dealing with troubled parent-local relations, even when the issues in conflict have been presented as unfair labour practices or as a breach of the duty of fair representation. In <ital>Gower and UTU, Re</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Gower and UTU, Re" p2="Gower and UTU, Re" post.rptr="4391" pre.rptr="2012" ref.id="Id8be27908cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Gower and UTU, Re">Gower and UTU, Re, 2012 CarswellNat 4391, 2012 CIRB 622</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Canada Industrial Relations Board dismissed a series of complaints filed by Canadian officers against their former international parent union which had been displaced by another union whom the complainants supported. The complainants sought a series of interim and final orders from the Board for access to offices and to financial statements, and for damages arising from a breach of the duty of fair representation triggered by the removal of the officers from their union positions by the former parent union. The Board ruled that these complaints dealt with internal union affairs beyond its statutory jurisdiction, and no labour relations purpose would be served by ruling on these matters.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">On occasion, specific legislation will govern the relationship between the parent union and its affiliates. The teachers&apos; unions in Ontario are an example, where the <ital>Teaching Profession Act</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Teaching Profession Act</ital>, R.S.O. 1990, c. T.2.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the <ital>Education Act</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Education Act</ital>, R.S.O. 1990, c. E.2.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and the Ontario <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, S.O. 1995, c. 1, Sch. A.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> all regulate the relationship between the Ontario Teachers&apos; Federation and its affiliates. When a dispute arose between the Federation and one of its affiliates over union policy, the affiliate withheld its dues owed to the parent. The Ontario Court of Appeal, in <ital>Ontario Teachers&apos; Federation v. OSSTF</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ontario Teachers&apos; Federation" p2="OSSTF" post.rptr="67" pre.rptr="161" ref.id="Id8be27918cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ontario Teachers&apos; Federation v. OSSTF">Ontario Teachers&apos; Federation v. OSSTF (2002), 161 O.A.C. 67, supplementary reasons 116 A.C.W.S. (3d) 845 (C.A.), leave to appeal to S.C.C. refused 313 N.R. 397n</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> ruled that the Federation&apos;s by-laws, enacted pursuant to the legislation, were valid, and the Federation was entitled to require the dissenting affiliate to pay the withheld dues in total. Furthermore, the affiliate was not entitled to withhold a portion of the dues equivalent to the amount of money regarding the issue in dispute: &ldquo;If the collective decision has been validly made, it is not, under the usual rules of union democracy, open to an individual to deny or defy the decision.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ontario Teachers&apos; Federation" p2="OSSTF" post.rptr="67" pre.rptr="161" ref.id="Id8be4ea08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ontario Teachers&apos; Federation v. OSSTF">Ontario Teachers&apos; Federation v. OSSTF (2002), 161 O.A.C. 67, supplementary reasons 116 A.C.W.S. (3d) 845 (C.A.), leave to appeal to S.C.C. refused 313 N.R. 397n</ref>
                        </cite>, at para. 45.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.B.3 Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Finances&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:9</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Union Assets</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A trust fund established by a union to finance emergency needs of its members and as a strike reserve is not revocable where the right to revoke was not reserved at the time the trust fund was created, and where there is dissension about the fund&apos;s future. In <ital>Dalhousie Staff Assn. Contingency Fund (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Dalhousie Staff Assn. Contingency Fund (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="102" pre.rptr="175" ref.id="Id8be4ea18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Dalhousie Staff Assn. Contingency Fund (Re)">Dalhousie Staff Assn. Contingency Fund (Re) (1999), 175 N.S.R. (2d) 102, 27 E.T.R. (2d) 310 (S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Nova Scotia Supreme Court held that, in the face of the missing revocation right, the moneys in the trust fund could only be distributed to the union members if there was unanimity among the beneficiaries. However, because it was not clear that all of the fund&apos;s beneficiaries could be ascertained, nor was there unanimity, the court refused to vary the trust to permit a distribution.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Where a local&apos;s by-laws provide for the distribution of its assets among its members upon the dissolution of the local, and there is no challenge to the propriety of the action, the distribution may be validated through an application to the courts under class proceedings legislation.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Elliot" p2="Currie" post.rptr="233" pre.rptr="12" ref.id="Id8be4ea28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Elliot v. Currie">Elliot v. Currie (2001), 12 C.P.C. (5th) 233 (Ont. S.C.J.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Union assets include the funds from the trusts and building corporations operated by both parent unions and their local unions. These trusts and corporations <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>were</trace> created <trace>historically </trace>to enable unions to hold property and to administer funds for valid collective union purposes. Should a local union seek to defect from the parent union, or should the local union be directed by the parent to involuntarily merge with another local, these union assets are deemed in law to be the creature of the union local and not independent of them.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McCauley" p2="Fitzsimmons" post.rptr="293" pre.rptr="112" ref.id="Id8be4ea38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McCauley v. Fitzsimmons">McCauley v. Fitzsimmons (1998), 112 O.A.C. 293, 1998 CarswellOnt 3589 (C.A.), leave to appeal refused (1999), 124 O.A.C. 199 (note) (S.C.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Thus, should the officers of the union local seek to alienate these union assets from the parent upon a defection or an involuntary merger with another union local, these assets&mdash;subject to the wording of the union constitution&mdash;would ordinarily be deemed to belong to the larger union. As the Ontario Superior Court of Justice stated in <ital>Leduc</ital>:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; the trusts and building corporations cannot be separated from the Former Locals and the IBEW. Their assets come from dues collected by the Former Locals pursuant to collective agreements negotiated by them as IBEW locals, with assistance from &lsqb;the&rsqb; International. Those funds and assets were to be used by the Former Locals for collective union purposes, in accordance with the IBEW Constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="Leduc" p2="International Brotherhood Electrical Workers" post.rptr="19179" pre.rptr="2019" ref.id="Id8be4ea48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers">Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers, 2019 CarswellOnt 19179, 2019 ONSC 6507</ref>
                            </cite>, at paras. 58&ndash;68.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
                <para.text date.trace="20221125" date.updated="20241205">In circumstances involving membership welfare and benefit programs that are administered by a union, the trustees of the union benefit plan are required to apply the provisions of the union constitution where it may conflict with the benefit plan provisions. In <ital>Union of British Columbia Performers v. Morton</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.trace="20241205" date.updated="20241205">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Union of British Columbia Performers" p2="Morton" post.rptr="57" pre.rptr="2023" ref.id="I62f81550a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="Union of British Columbia Performers v. Morton">Union of British Columbia Performers v. Morton, 2023 BCCA 57 (B.C. C.A.), reversing Sanders v. Union of B.C. Performers, 2021 CarswellBC 2907, 2021 BCSC 1820 (B.C. S.C.), leave to appeal refused Robert Morton, Brad Loree, Brenda Crichlow, et al. v. Union of British Columbia Performers, et al., 2023 CarswellBC 2945 (S.C.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> a union member wished to transfer his union-administered health and welfare benefits from a trust run by a British Columbia union local to a separate trust administered by the national parent union. The trustees refused to allow the member&apos;s application, stating that allowing such a request was not in the interests of the trust beneficiaries because it would negatively impact the trust&apos;s ability to maintain current levels of coverage. The British Columbia Court of Appeal ruled in favour of the member, stating that the provisions in the union constitution must be applied and the subordinate trust agreement must be amended to bring it in line with the constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Also see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lewis" p2="Union of B.C. Performers" post.rptr="160" pre.rptr="1996" ref.id="I04f48db1696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="Lewis v. Union of B.C. Performers">Lewis v. Union of B.C. Performers, 1996 CarswellBC 160 (B.C. C.A.), leave to appeal refused (1996), 83 B.C.A.C. 238 (note) (S.C.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; and <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref date="1981" p1="Rhodes" p2="Electrical Workers Welfare &amp; Pension Plans, Local 213" post.rptr="369" pre.rptr="27" ref.id="I04f4b4c0696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="Rhodes v. Electrical Workers Welfare &amp; Pension Plans, Local 213">Rhodes v. Electrical Workers Welfare &amp; Pension Plans, Local 213 (1981), 27 B.C.L.R. 369, 1981 CarswellBC 80 (B.C. S.C.), affirmed (1982), 36 B.C.L.R. 233, 1982 CarswellBC 93 (B.C. C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The trial and appeal court judgments in <ital>CUPE v. Deveau</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="Deveau" pre.rptr="19" post.rptr="44" title="CUPE v. Deveau" date="1976">CUPE v. Deveau (1976), 19 N.S.R. (2d) 44 (N.S. S.C.), affirmed 19 N.S.R. (2d) 24 (N.S. C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>. For a useful discussion or the decisions in <ital>Deveau</ital>, see B.G. Hansen, &ldquo;Comments&rdquo; (1978), 56 Can. Bar Rev. 80. <ital>Deveau</ital> is a prime example of the courts upholding the language of the constitution where the provisions clearly favour the parent, as they do in most such circumstances in Canada. For an example of a judicial ruling where the language of the constitution favoured the local, see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="News Group Newspapers Ltd." p2="Society of Graphical and Allied Trades 1982" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1986&rsqb;" post.rptr="227" title="News Group Newspapers Ltd. v. Society of Graphical and Allied Trades 1982">News Group Newspapers Ltd. v. Society of Graphical and Allied Trades 1982, &lsqb;1986&rsqb; I.R.L.R. 227 (C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> provide a valuable review of many of the legal issues that arise in parent-local disputes over the disposition of assets. In this case, a Nova Scotia municipal workers&apos; local of the Canadian Union of Public Employees (CUPE), broke away in 1974 and formed an independent union. Some time before leaving CUPE, the local had ceased forwarding to the parent its <ital>per capita</ital> dues obligations. The local voted to divide up the funds under its control -- consisting of the unremitted dues plus the assets in the possession of the local, amounting to about &dollar;30,000 -- among the membership as a &ldquo;cost-of-living&rdquo; bonus. CUPE&apos;s claim for the recovery of the distributed assets was successful both at trial and on appeal.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The third issue raised in <ital>Deveau</ital> was the extent of the fiduciary relationship between the local executive and the parent union. The courts&apos; analyses started with the CUPE constitution, which placed a specific duty on the officers of the local to forward all of the union&apos;s assets under their control to the parent. It is apparent that a local executive bears a fiduciary obligation both towards the members of its local, and towards the parent union in line with the contractual obligations specified in the constitution. The <ital>Deveau</ital> judgments are unclear on the breadth of this duty, although Canadian case law exists which suggests that a broad scope is to be given to the local executive&apos;s fiduciary duty to the parent.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Prince Edward Island Supreme Court has affirmed that a breakaway union has no claim on the property of the local.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338" p2="Bradley" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1999&rsqb;" post.rptr="11" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338 v. Bradley">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1338 v. Bradley, &lsqb;1999&rsqb; P.E.I.J. No. 11 (P.E.I. T.D.)</ref>
                          </cite>. For a contemporary discussion of the fiduciary duty in a trade union context, see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Guzzo" p2="Randazzo" pre.rptr="2015" post.rptr="18762" title="Guzzo v. Randazzo">Guzzo v. Randazzo, 2015 CarswellOnt 18762, 2015 ONSC 6936 (Ont. S.C.J.), additional reasons 2016 CarswellOnt 5965, 2016 ONSC 2588 (Ont. S.C.J.), affirmed 2017 ONSC 1845, 2017 CarswellOnt 9950 (Ont. Div. Ct.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> And in similar circumstances to <ital>Deveau</ital>, the New Brunswick Court of Appeal ruled that a membership which abandons the parent union and creates a new independent union, and which acquires the existing bargaining rights along the way, nevertheless does not gain the right of ownership over the local&apos;s funds or to transfer them to the new union.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="LeBlanc" p2="CUPE, Local 558" pre.rptr="2016" post.rptr="84" title="LeBlanc v. CUPE, Local 558">LeBlanc v. CUPE, Local 558, 2016 CarswellNB 84, 2016 NBCA 11 (N.B. C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>. The New Brunswick Court of Appeal found that the transfer of local union funds by the exiting local executive to the new union entity was not a valid purpose of the local, as per the requirements of the governing union constitution, because these funds could not be used to &apos;destroy&apos; the local.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In analogous circumstances, the officers and trustees of union locals that were involuntarily merged with other locals through the direction of the parent union breached their fiduciary duties when they attempted to distribute the funds from the old locals to the former membership rather than transfer these funds to the new amalgamated local. This would amount to unjust enrichment.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Leduc" p2="International Brotherhood Electrical Workers" pre.rptr="2019" post.rptr="19179" title="Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers">Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers, 2019 CarswellOnt 19179, 2019 ONSC 6507 (Ont. S.C.J.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at paras. 94-103.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> However, the courts will also scrutinize the relationship of a union officer to the union, in order to determine whether the officer&apos;s power and influence was sufficient to find the existence of a fiduciary relationship. In <ital>Randazzo</ital>, the Ontario Divisional Court held that a dissident local union officer who led her members to another union did not have broad enough powers within the local &mdash; no cheque signing authority, no power to employ or dismiss employees, and no authority to establish a budget without executive approval &mdash; to be in a fiduciary relationship with her former union.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.D Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:10</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Union Structures and Certification</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.trace="20260211" date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Under labour relations statutes, the certification of bargaining representatives is only granted to prescribed classes of organizations. The key requirement is that an applicant <trace>must</trace> meet the statutory definition a &ldquo;union,&rdquo; in accordance with relevant jurisprudence.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">0.50</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref">
                            <ref ref.id="Id1ade950fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" secrange="y" secref="1:14 to 1:16">&dblsect;1:14 to 1:16</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In unions with complex or hierarchical structures, this can create two kinds of challenges for certification<trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>: one turning on the eligibility of the local, the other turning on the eligibility of the parent.   As for the first,</trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>where</trace> a local of a parent union applies for certification, the board will only grant certification if <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>it</trace> is satisfied that the local itself is a viable, properly established union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">0.60</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref">
                            <ref ref.id="Id1ade951fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" secref="1:19">&sect;1:19</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> To be certified, a local must be more than a proxy for the parent. Even if a local has been chartered by its parent, if it lacks duly appointed or elected officers, a board may find that it fails to qualify as a union and thus cannot be granted certification.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">0.70</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Azteca Builders Ltd." post.rptr="5" pre.rptr="2025" ref.id="Id1ade952fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="Azteca Builders Ltd.">Azteca Builders Ltd., 2025 BCLRB 5, 2025 CarswellBC 40, reconsideration refused 2025 BCLRB 86</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">Where a union<trace.deleted/> loca<trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>l</trace> lack<trace>s</trace> sufficient autonomy and agency to qualify as <trace>a </trace>union<trace.deleted/>, in most jurisdictions there is nothing barring the parent from applying for certification.<trace> The parent may even apply to hold bargaining rights in the name of a local, despite that local having no separate legal existence.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">71</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">0.71 <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="International Union of Operating Engineers, Local Union No. 955" p2="Steel River SICIM Pipeline Ltd." pre.rptr="2025" post.rptr="111" title="International Union of Operating Engineers, Local Union No. 955 v. Steel River SICIM Pipeline Ltd.">International Union of Operating Engineers, Local Union No. 955 v. Steel River SICIM Pipeline Ltd., 2025 ALRB 111, 2025 CarswellAlta 2098 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>0.71 Even where the local is intended to have relative autonomy in bargaining, the parent may limit legal uncertainty and minimize inconvenience by applying for formal certification itself and then functionally delegating the resulting powers to a local. As the Canadian Industrial Relations Board has noted, &ldquo;in certification orders, it is not uncommon to name a national union as the certified bargaining agent while locals may be responsible for negotiating and/or administering the relevant collective agreement.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">23</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">
                            <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="Air Canada and CUPE, Re" p2="" post.rptr="1197" pre.rptr="2025" title="Air Canada and CUPE, Re">Air Canada and CUPE, Re, 2025 CIRB 1197, 2025 CarswellNat 4894, 2025 CarswellNat 4893 (C.I.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite> at para. 98.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>0.75 </trace>However, where a parent union is a national organization, that option may be foreclosed in some provinces by a statutory requirement that a union be a &ldquo;local or provincial organization&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">0.80</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace>On &ldquo;local character&rdquo; requirements, </trace>
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>see generally</trace>
                          <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref">
                            <ref ref.id="Id1ae1060fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" secref="1:23">&sect;1:23</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The same goes for a &ldquo;multi-provincial local&rdquo; if it cannot be demonstrated that those &ldquo;responsible for governing the union&apos;s affairs and assuming responsibility for its actions&rdquo; are based within the province.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">0.90</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Azteca Builders Ltd." post.rptr="5" pre.rptr="2025" ref.id="Id1ae1061fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="Azteca Builders Ltd.">Azteca Builders Ltd., 2025 BCLRB 5, 2025 CarswellBC 40, reconsideration refused 2025 BCLRB 86</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> While the &ldquo;provincial character&rdquo; requirement has generally been interpreted in a flexible manner to be compatible with a wide variety of parent-local relationship arrangements. Nonetheless, while a parent union will be given status as a legal entity under such legislation, it will not be entitled to formal trade union status if it lacks this character. </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">It follows that a parent union cannot become a party to a collective agreement in these circumstances.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="28" pre.rptr="171" ref.id="Id8be75b08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re)">Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re), grievance award (2008), 171 L.A.C. (4th) 28 (Larson), applying board review award (2007), 167 L.A.C. (4th) 404 (Saunders), which set aside preliminary award 160 L.A.C. (4th) 314 (Larson)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In different circumstances, the Newfoundland and Labrador Labour Relations Board provided a more restrictive interpretation of the statutory provision. In <ital>Public Service Alliance of Canada, Local 05/91100 v. Canadian Corps of Commissionaires</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Public Service Alliance of Canada, Local 05/91100" p2="Canadian Corps of Commissionaires" post.rptr="&para;220-019" pre.rptr="2004" ref.id="Id8be75b18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Public Service Alliance of Canada, Local 05/91100 v. Canadian Corps of Commissionaires">Public Service Alliance of Canada, Local 05/91100 v. Canadian Corps of Commissionaires, 2004 C.L.L.C. &para;220-019, 101 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 279 (Nfld. &amp; Lab. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> it ruled that a union local without the autonomous power under the national constitution to conduct collective bargaining, the right to strike, or to control its own grievance and arbitration process did not meet the test of provincial or local control, and denied certification.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A local or provincial council is entitled to apply to a labour relations board to seek to be certified as the bargaining agent in the place of its estranged parent, as long as the application is otherwise timely. In <ital>Ontario Power Generation and LIUNA (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ontario Power Generation and LIUNA (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="131" pre.rptr="116" ref.id="Id8be75b28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ontario Power Generation and LIUNA (Re)">Ontario Power Generation and LIUNA (Re) (2005), 116 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 131 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Ontario Labour Relations Board was unpersuaded by arguments that the local union was the same legal and practical entity as its parent<trace.deleted/> and therefore incapable of displacing itself as bargaining agent for the affected employees.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">Could the challenges of certification in multi-tiered unions be overcome by having a parent and local apply jointly for certification? In most cases, no. Canadian labour law generally allows only one trade union to hold bargaining rights for an industrial bargaining unit.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3.50</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Nova Scotia offers a notable exception. Under s. 23(6) of the Trade Union Act, R.S.N.S. 1989, c. 475, two or more trade unions may make a joint application for certification and, for the purposes of the application and the certification, the two unions are treated as a single entity.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The bargaining rights cannot be jointly held by the parent union and the local; only one can be the &ldquo;trade union&rdquo; for certification purposes.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414,422,440,448,461,483,488,1000 &amp; 1688 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1350" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1993&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8be9cc08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414,422,440,448,461,483,488,1000 &amp; 1688 (Re)">Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414,422,440,448,461,483,488,1000 &amp; 1688 (Re), &lsqb;1993&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1350</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Thus, while a labour relations board may certify a trade union by reference in the certification order to the &ldquo;Workers&apos; Union and its Local 310&rdquo;, this does not necessarily result in bargaining rights flowing to the local union when, at a later date, it is attempting to divorce from the parent union. A labour relations board will examine all of the relevant evidence, including the union&apos;s constitution, political structure and the bargaining history, in order to determine the ultimate assignment of the bargaining rights in the wake of a parent-local divorce.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Tiercon Corp." p2="Workers United Ontario Council" post.rptr="396" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8be9cc18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council">Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 396</ref>
                          </cite>
                          <trace>; see generally </trace>
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>&dblsect;5:29&ndash;5:45, &ldquo;Union Successor Rights: Mergers, Amalgamations and Transfers of Jurisdiction&rdquo;</trace>
                        </para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">While certification rights formally held by a parent may be functionally exercised by a local (or vice versa in the case of a trusteeship), labour boards have been very clear that bargaining rights cannot be formally transferred within a union structure without the approval of the labour board. It is not uncommon for employers and unions to voluntarily amend their relationship, including the scope of the bargaining unit, through a &ldquo;recognition clause&rdquo; in a collective agreement or otherwise. In some jurisdictions, such voluntary agreements between the employer and the union has been found to displace the original certification.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">6 See for example <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Nova Scotia Government and General Employees Union" p2="Metro Community Living Support Services Ltd" pre.rptr="2015" post.rptr="87" title="Nova Scotia Government and General Employees Union v. Metro Community Living Support Services Ltd">Nova Scotia Government and General Employees Union v. Metro Community Living Support Services Ltd, 2015 NSLB 87, 2015 CarswellNS 585 (N.S. L.B.), reversed 2016 NSSC 123, 2016 CarswellNS 386 (N.S. S.C.), affirmed 2017 NSCA 15, 2017 CarswellNS 70 (N.S. C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>, in which the Labour Board held that, by dint of provisions in the Nova Scotia<ital> Trade Union Act</ital> that deem a voluntary recognition agreement to be a certification, a board&apos;s original certification is &ldquo;spent&rdquo; once the parties file a collective agreement that includes a recognition clause with a bargaining unit different from that described in the original certification.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Canadian Industrial Relations Board has made very clear, however, that the identity of the bargaining agent cannot be altered without seeking a board order to amend the certification order, regardless of changes to internal union structure, the consent of all union parties, and voluntary recognition by the employer.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">7 <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Air Canada and CUPE, Re" post.rptr="1197" pre.rptr="2025" title="Air Canada and CUPE, Re">Air Canada and CUPE, Re, 2025 CIRB 1197, 2025 CarswellNat 4894, 2025 CarswellNat 4893 (C.I.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at paras. 84&ndash;98.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In <ital>UBCJA, Local 683 and J.D. Irving Ltd.,</ital> the New Brunswick Labour and Employment held that the actions and statements of a parent or affiliated union could not be attributed to a local applying for certification, for the purposes of determining whether the applicant engaged in discrimination of the kind that would bar it from eligibility to serve as a bargaining agent.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">8 <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 683" p2="J.D. Irving Limited" pre.rptr="2025" post.rptr="99213" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 683 v. J.D. Irving Limited">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 683 v. J.D. Irving Limited (c.o.b. Grant Lake Timber), 2025 CanLII 99213 sub nom. UBCJA, Local 683 and J.D. Irving Ltd., Re) 2025 CarswellNB 490 (N.B. Labour &amp; Employment Bd.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Both the trial and appeal decisions rejected the local&apos;s defences. First, the courts did not find that CUPE, by providing what the local deemed to be an unacceptably low level of services, had fundamentally breached its contract with the local or expressly repudiated its constitutional obligations towards it. The courts left open the possibility of using this defence in the future, although it would appear that, to be successful, a serious and substantial breach would have to be established.<trace.deleted/> Second, both courts rejected the argument that, because the local was autonomous before joining CUPE, that status enabled it to survive the local&apos;s dissolution, creating a resulting trust that made it the heir to the disputed assets.<trace.deleted/> The courts gave precedence to the union constitution, which expressly provided that, upon the dissolution of a chartered local, all assets would revert to the parent.<trace.deleted/> The union, the Court of Appeal determined, had lost its autonomy when it obtained its CUPE charter. Even where the constitution is silent on the ownership of assets when a local is dissolved, a resulting trust in favour of the breakaway local may not necessarily occur.<trace.deleted/>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote>
          <trace.deleted/>
        </para.text>
      </para>
    </section.body>
  </section>
  <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
    <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
      <metadata field="classification">
        <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.D Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
      </metadata>
    </metadata.block>
    <section.front date.updated="0">
      <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
        <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
        <designator date.updated="0">5:11</designator>
        <name date.updated="0">Relations between International Unions and their Canadian Affiliates</name>
      </outline.name.block>
    </section.front>
    <section.body date.updated="0">
      <para date.updated="0">
        <para.text date.updated="0">Where internal governance issues arise between an international union and its Canadian affiliate, the question of which national labour laws apply &mdash; American or Canadian &mdash; sometimes arises. It is clear that Canadian labour law governs the regulation of the internal affairs of international trade unions <ital>in Canada.</ital> The Canadian courts and labour relations boards have consistently taken jurisdiction of internal disputes arising from the internal operations <ital>in Canada</ital> between an international parent union and its Canadian affiliate, whether the issue involves elections,<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="Taylor" p2="Atkinson" post.rptr="112" pre.rptr="6" ref.id="Id8bf60128cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Taylor v. Atkinson">Taylor v. Atkinson (1984), 6 C.C.E.L. 112 (Ont. H.C.J.), leave to appeal to Ont. C.A. refused 7 C.C.E.L. xxxvii</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote> trusteeships,<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145" p2="AFM" post.rptr="1128" pre.rptr="2015" ref.id="Id8bf60138cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145 v. AFM">Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145 v. AFM, 2015 CarswellBC 1128, 2015 BCCA 171</ref>
                  </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners, Local 1338" p2="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America" post.rptr="140" pre.rptr="148" ref.id="Id8bf60148cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners, Local 1338 v. United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners, Local 1338 v. United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America (1997), 148 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 140 (P.E.I.S.C.), overturned on other grounds, 148 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 152 (P.E.I.C.A.)</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote> ownership of union assets,<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="International Association of Bridge, Structural and Ornamental Ironworkers" p2="" post.rptr="1487" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1982&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bf60158cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="International Association of Bridge, Structural and Ornamental Ironworkers">International Association of Bridge, Structural and Ornamental Ironworkers, &lsqb;1982&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1487</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote> use of union insignias,<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="Stamos" p2="Belanger" post.rptr="&para;14,043" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8bf60168cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Stamos v. Belanger">Stamos v. Belanger (1994), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;14,043, 57 C.P.R. (3d) 390 (Ont. Ct. (Gen. Div.))</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote> mergers,<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="491" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1988&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bf87208cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)">IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 491, application dismissed with costs &lsqb;1990&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. December 1365 (Div. Ct.), motion for leave to Ont. C.A. dismissed with costs &lsqb;1992&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July 889</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote> or the removal of union officers by the parent union.<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="McEvoy" p2="Capri" post.rptr="474" pre.rptr="48" ref.id="Id8bf87218cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McEvoy v. Capri">McEvoy v. Capri (1964), 48 W.W.R. 474, 64 C.L.L.C. &para;14,009 (Man. C.A.)</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote>
        </para.text>
      </para>
      <para date.updated="0">
        <para.text date.updated="0">However, when the issue is the governance of the international union in its union-wide operations &mdash; such as selecting delegates to an international convention, or the election process to fill its union-wide officer positions &mdash; then the applicable legislative authority is the statutory law of the union&apos;s principal jurisdiction or country of residence. Canadian law, like the laws of most other western industrialized countries, draws a distinction between the <ital>residence</ital> and the <ital>domicile</ital> of organizations which operate internationally. Associations and corporations which operate internationally may be domiciled in the various jurisdictions where they operate, but the law deems that they have only one residence. Their residence is the location where its centre of control and management &mdash; the seat and directing power of the affairs of the organization &mdash; is based. Such organizations must comply with the laws of the various jurisdictions in which they operate, but the legislation governing their primary legal personality and the regulation of their central operations is the prevailing law of their residence.<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="Pet Milk Canada Ltd." p2="Olympia &amp; York Developments" post.rptr="640" pre.rptr="4" ref.id="Id8bfae308cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Pet Milk Canada Ltd. v. Olympia &amp; York Developments">Pet Milk Canada Ltd. v. Olympia &amp; York Developments (1974), 4 O.R. (2d) 640 (S.C.)</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote>
        </para.text>
      </para>
      <para date.updated="0">
        <para.text date.updated="0">Thus, when an international union, headquartered in the United States, conducts union-wide elections, it must comply with the U.S. <ital>Labour-Management Reporting and Disclosure Act of 1959</ital> (LMRDA),<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">73 Stat. 9 (1959), <cite finder="crime" type="lrre">
                    <ref date="1964" normalpub="statute-us-code" rangeend="et seq." rangetype="secref" ref.id="Icff89e108cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" secrange="y" secref="401" titleref="29">29 U.S.C. 401 et seq. (1964)</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
        </footnote> even though Canadian members are participating in the ballot. Moreover, Canadian delegates to an international convention of the parent union who will vote on union-wide matters must be selected in accordance with the <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-34" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;45" folio="5-33" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;33"?>minimum standards of the LMRDA, and must cast their votes according to the LMRDA. This flows from the principle that the operations of the central government must not only comply with the legislation of its resident jurisdiction, but that this legislation is also applicable to all aspects of its international operations that bear upon its central government.</para.text>
      </para>
      <para date.updated="0">
        <para.text date.updated="0">However, the election of Canadian officers of the international parent, who are selected solely by Canadian delegates or members, would be governed by Canadian law and the constitution of the international union. Since these officers are not being elected to union-wide offices by the membership as a whole, or by delegates representing the entire membership, then, in law, these elections would be no different from the election of Canadian local officers in Canada. This would be regardless of the fact that the election or convention is being conducted on American soil.</para.text>
      </para>
    </section.body>
  </section>
  <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
    <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
      <metadata field="classification">
        <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.D Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
      </metadata>
    </metadata.block>
    <section.front date.updated="0">
      <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
        <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
        <designator date.updated="0">5:12</designator>
        <name date.updated="0">Remedies</name>
      </outline.name.block>
    </section.front>
    <section.body date.updated="0">
      <para date.updated="0">
        <para.text date.updated="0">In intra-union disputes involving the control of assets, the interests of the parent will normally be to acquire an effective remedy to recover the contested property, which is usually in the hands of the seceding members. Where the courts have found a fiduciary duty resting on the local executive, they have held that the officers are personally liable for the sequestered assets. In <ital>Deveau</ital>, the local officers argued in their defence that, even if a <ital>prima facie</ital> case for personal liability had been made out against them, their actions were protected by the provincial <ital>Trustee Act.</ital>
          <footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">R.S.N.S. 1967, c. 317, s. 62.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote> The statute waived liability for an executive officer in a trustee position if he or she had acted &ldquo;honestly and reasonably&rdquo;. The Nova Scotia court judgments dismissed this defence, ruling that the officers, by intentionally converting the union assets in an attempt to put them beyond the reach of the parent, had committed a breach of trust and were not thereby relieved of personal liability for the funds.<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="CUPE" p2="Deveau" post.rptr="44" pre.rptr="19" ref.id="Id8bfae318cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. Deveau">CUPE v. Deveau (1976), 19 N.S.R. (2d) 44 at p. 70 (S.C.), affd 19 N.S.R. (2d) 24 (C.A.)</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote> The court in <ital>Raymond v. Doherty</ital> went somewhat further, holding that not only the executive but &ldquo;those persons to whom possession was delivered&rdquo; were personally liable to the parent for the converted property.<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="Raymond" p2="Doherty" post.rptr="99" pre.rptr="49" ref.id="Id8bfae328cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Raymond v. Doherty">Raymond v. Doherty (1965), 49 D.L.R. (2d) 99 at p. 105, &lsqb;1965&rsqb; 1 O.R. 593 (C.A.)</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote>
        </para.text>
      </para>
      <para date.updated="0">
        <para.text date.updated="0">Where union officers who led the secession have been found in breach of their contractual, fiduciary and trust duties, a general monetary remedy has been awarded. As well, in the same case,<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="UFCW, Local 1252" p2="Cashin" post.rptr="620" pre.rptr="217" ref.id="Id8bfd5408cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin">UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin (2002), 217 D.L.R. (4th) 620, 216 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 41, 84 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161 (Nfld. C.A.), affg 149 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 112 (Nfld. S.C.)</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
        </footnote> the Newfoundland Supreme Court awarded general damages against the seceding union for interference with bargaining rights and for conversion of the membership list and mailing labels, and also ordered the appropriation of the members&apos; benefit fund to be rescinded, and returned to the old union.</para.text>
      </para>
      <para date.updated="0">
        <para.text date.updated="0">In <ital>Leduc</ital>, the Ontario Superior Court ruled that all attempts by the former union local trustees to distribute the funds of the former local to the local membership were null and void, and that these former trustees were to provide all reasonable assistance to enable the transfer of the locals&apos; assets to the successor local.<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="Leduc" p2="International Brotherhood Electrical Workers" post.rptr="19179" pre.rptr="2019" ref.id="Id8bfd5418cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers">Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers, 2019 CarswellOnt 19179, 2019 ONSC 6507</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote>
        </para.text>
      </para>
      <para date.updated="0">
        <para.text date.updated="0">A parent union is entitled to restrict a seceding local from using its name, trade marks or insignia by seeking interlocutory relief under the <ital>Trade-marks Act</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">R.S.C. 1985, c. T-13.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote> particularly if these names or marks have come to identify the parent&apos;s reputation and there is a potential issue of misrepresentation or confusion.<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="Stamos" p2="Belanger" post.rptr="&para;14,043" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8bffc508cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Stamos v. Belanger">Stamos v. Belanger (1994), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;14,043, 57 C.P.R. (3d) 390 (Ont. Gen. Div.)</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote> However, where there would be no public confusion between the name of the old union and the name chosen by the seceding union, then a claim of misrepresentation by the parent union would fail.<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="UFCW, Local 1252" p2="Cashin" post.rptr="620" pre.rptr="217" ref.id="Id8bffc518cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin">UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin (2002), 217 D.L.R. (4th) 620, 216 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 41, 84 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161 (Nfld. C.A.), affg 149 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 112 (Nfld. S.C.)</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote> A bitter rivalry between a parent union and a former affiliate has persuaded the British Columbia Labour Relations Board to decline to grant ownership over a union logo to either entity.<footnote date.updated="0">
            <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                    <ref p1="British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters and CJA (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="B43/2008" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2008&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8bffc528cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters and CJA (Re)">British Columbia Provincial Council of Carpenters and CJA (Re), &lsqb;2008&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B43/2008</ref>
                </cite>.</para.text>
              </para>
            </footnote.body>
          </footnote>
        </para.text>
      </para>
    </section.body>
  </section>
</section.block>
</analytical.level.body>
</analytical.level>
<analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
    <metadata field="classification">
      <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
    </metadata>
  </metadata.block>
  <front date.updated="0">
    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
      <designator date.updated="0">III</designator>
      <name date.updated="0">Trusteeships</name>
    </outline.name.block>
  </front>
  <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">A</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Introduction</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:13</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Introduction</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">A trusteeship is probably the single most powerful instrument available to a parent labour organization to control the affairs of a union local. It permits a parent to suspend the autonomy of a local or other subordinate body (such as a regional council or a provincial division), and to appoint a representative to directly manage the affairs of the local until the conditions which triggered the trusteeship have been resolved. The constitutions of Canada&apos;s largest unions all contain a specific grant of authority to permit a parent to invoke a trusteeship, usually for stated reasons such as a failure to abide by the constitution, policies or decisions of the union, for internal corruption, or for a general neglect of duty.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Canadian Union of Public Employees<trace> (CUPE) Constitution</trace> (<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>2025</trace>), articles 3.4, 7.7; Public Service Alliance of Canada<trace> (PSAC) Constitution</trace> (<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>2024</trace>), article 8(6); Unifor<trace> Constitution</trace> (<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>2025</trace>), article 15.M; United Steelworkers of America<trace> (USW) Constitution</trace> (<trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>2025</trace>), article IX.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> To check this power, however, most union constitutions also contain provisions to ensure that the parent&apos;s decision to impose a trusteeship is reviewable and, when so deemed, reversable.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>CUPE Constitution</trace> (<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>2025</trace>), articles 7.7&ndash;7.9; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>PSAC Constitution</trace> (<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>2024</trace>), article 8(6); Unifor<trace> Constitution</trace> (<trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>2025</trace>), article 15.M.2; <trace.deleted/>
                          <trace>USW Constitution</trace> (<trace.deleted/>
                        <trace>2025</trace>), article IX.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">The imposition of a trusteeship is obviously a significant intervention into the democratic life of a union local or subordinate body. The British Columbia Court of Appeal in <ital>Kiniak</ital> has called the authority of a parent union to invoke a trusteeship a &ldquo;drastic disciplinary remedy&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Warner" p2="Kiniak" post.rptr="117" pre.rptr="20" ref.id="Id8c023608cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Warner v. Kiniak">Warner v. Kiniak (1980), 20 B.C.L.R. 117, 1980 CarswellBC 79 (C.A.), <point>at para. 23,</point> leave to appeal refused (1980), 33 N.R. 347n (S.C.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Usually, a trusteeship involves the removal from office of the local&apos;s executive, the surrender of control over its finances and decision-making, and the suspension of membership meetings. For these reasons, in Canada, trusteeships are imposed rather infrequently,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="223" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c023618cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)">Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar. 223</ref>
                        </cite>, where the Ontario Labour Relations Board noted, at p. 225, that the:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; lack of impediments or constraints &lsqb;on a parent union when it places a local under trusteeship&rsqb; would appear to reflect a recognition on the part of the Legislature that most trusteeships are imposed as a result of real and legitimate concerns on the part of the union involved. For example, a trusteeship may be imposed because of mismanagement or dishonest use of local funds, because a local has become so torn by dissent that it cannot function properly, or perhaps to remove officers who have either become dictatorial or who have failed to administer the local in a responsible manner. At times, particularly with small locals, a trusteeship may be imposed simply because none of the members of the local are willing to assume the responsibilities of elected office.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and generally only for a short duration. There have been few cases where the motive for the trusteeship was not directly linked to a justifiable labour relations reason. This may explain why Canadian labour law has left the regulation of union trusteeships largely up to the labour movement itself, unlike the extensive regulatory scheme in the United States.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <ital>Labour-Management Reporting and Disclosure (Landrum-Griffin) Act</ital>, 73 State. 519, ss. 301&ndash;306, <cite finder="legacy" type="lrre">
                            <ref date="1988" normalpub="statute-us-code" ref.id="Icff8c5208cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" secref="461 to 6" titleref="29">29 U.S.C. ss. 461 to 6 (1988)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block date.trace="20260211" date.updated="20260211" toc.include="y">
          <designator date.updated="0">B</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Board Regulation of Trusteeships</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block date.trace="20260211" date.updated="20260211" toc.include="y">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:14</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Statutory Regulation of Trusteeships</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">Only two provinces &mdash; Ontario and British Columbia &mdash; have enacted provisions in their labour statutes that explicitly address trusteeships. Section 89 of the Ontario <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital> requires a parent union that imposes a trusteeship over a subordinate body to file, within sixty days of imposition, a statement with the Labour Relations Board setting out the terms by which the trusteeship is to be exercised.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">S.O. 1995, c. 1, s. 89(1).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Board is entitled to require that additional information be filed by the parent.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">S.O. 1995, c. 1, s. 89(1).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The trusteeship cannot continue beyond twelve months without the consent of the Labour Relations Board, which has the discretion to extend it for up to a further twelve months.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">S.O. 1995, c. 1, s. 89(2).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Ontario Board has consistently held that it has no jurisdiction under section 89 to supervise or intervene with the trusteeship during the first year.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit" post.rptr="16" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2004&rsqb;" ref.id="Id1b00c30fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit">Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit, &lsqb;2004&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 16</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, this does not displace the board&apos;s jurisdiction to review a trusteeship where the parent&apos;s actions are alleged to have breached another section of the <ital>Act</ital>.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Gaudette" p2="OSSTF" post.rptr="16949" pre.rptr="2016" ref.id="Id1b00c31fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="Gaudette v. OSSTF">Gaudette v. OSSTF, 2016 CarswellOnt 16949 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>, at para. 18. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit" post.rptr="16" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2004&rsqb;" ref.id="Id1b00c32fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit">Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit, &lsqb;2004&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 16</ref>
                        </cite> at para. 6.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">The British Columbia <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital> provides for virtually the same provisions.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">R.S.B.C. 1996, c. 244, s. 150.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">Neither Act directly places any impediments or constraints on a parent when it puts a local under trusteeship.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">For a review of the policy reasons behind s. 89 of the Ontario Act, see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="584" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1992&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c04a718cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re)">Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re), &lsqb;1992&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 584</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="223" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c04a728cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)">Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar. 223</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">Historically, Ontario also prohibited a parent union or council of trade unions in the construction industry from placing a local union under &ldquo;supervision or control&rdquo; or interfering with the responsibilities of a local officer, without just cause.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.trace="20260211" date.updated="20260211">See e.g. Labour Relations Act, S.O. 1995, c. 1, Sch A, s. 149, as it appeared on 1 January 2018.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In 2018, Ontario introduced provisions that apply these prohibitions to all parent unions in Ontario.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8.50</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, S.O. 1995, c. 1, s. 89.1. See further discussion at <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref">
                            <ref ref.id="Id1b05a50fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" secref="5:27">&sect;5:27</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">Given the limited express oversight of union trusteeships, the Ontario Labour Relations Board has generally given the trusteeship provisions in the Act a permissive interpretation and allowed a parent union to suspend local autonomy where its motives did not offend labour relations policy. For example, should a parent fail to inform the Ontario Labour Relations Board of the imposition of a trusteeship within the 60-day requirement, the trusteeship will not necessarily be invalid despite the mandatory language of the statute.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="20211028">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UNITE-HERE" p2="Hospitality &amp; Service Trades Union, Local 261" post.rptr="1018" pre.rptr="2021" ref.id="I5dee2240370e11ec80e0c0b599422da1" title="UNITE-HERE v. Hospitality &amp; Service Trades Union, Local 261">UNITE-HERE v. Hospitality &amp; Service Trades Union, Local 261, 2021 CarswellOnt 1018 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 4943" post.rptr="11774" pre.rptr="2019" ref.id="I5dee2241370e11ec80e0c0b599422da1" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 4943">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 4943, 2019 CarswellOnt 11774 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <trace>
                            <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="Interior Systems Contractors Assn. of Ontario and Drywall Acouctic Lathing and Insulation, Local 675 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1082" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1995&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c071808cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Interior Systems Contractors Assn. of Ontario and Drywall Acouctic Lathing and Insulation, Local 675 (Re)">Interior Systems Contractors Assn. of Ontario and Drywall Acoustic Lathing and Insulation, Local 675 (Re), &lsqb;1995&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. August 1082</ref>
                            </cite>
                          </trace>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="584" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1992&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c071818cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re)">Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re), &lsqb;1992&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 584</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Even in circumstances where the initial filing was more than twelve months after the trusteeship was imposed, the Board will not necessarily strike down the trusteeship, although it may seek the views of the local membership who would be directly affected by a continuation of the trusteeship.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="20211028">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UNITE-HERE" p2="Hospitality &amp; Service Trades Union, Local 261" post.rptr="4294" pre.rptr="2021" ref.id="I5dee4950370e11ec80e0c0b599422da1" title="UNITE-HERE v. Hospitality &amp; Service Trades Union, Local 261">UNITE-HERE v. Hospitality &amp; Service Trades Union, Local 261, 2021 CarswellOnt 4294 (Ont. L.R.B.), varied on reconsideration 2021 CarswellOnt 4295 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UNITE-HERE" p2="Hospitality &amp; Service Trades Union, Local 261" post.rptr="1018" pre.rptr="2021" ref.id="I5dee7060370e11ec80e0c0b599422da1" title="UNITE-HERE v. Hospitality &amp; Service Trades Union, Local 261">UNITE-HERE v. Hospitality &amp; Service Trades Union, Local 261, 2021 CarswellOnt 1018 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Interior Systems Contractors Assn. of Ontario and Drywall Acouctic Lathing and Insulation, Local 675 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1082" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1995&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c071828cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Interior Systems Contractors Assn. of Ontario and Drywall Acouctic Lathing and Insulation, Local 675 (Re)">Interior Systems Contractors Assn. of Ontario and Drywall Acouctic Lathing and Insulation, Local 675 (Re), &lsqb;1995&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. August 1082</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Chemical Workers&apos; Union" p2="Canadian Chemical Workers&apos; Union, Local 28 (Re)" post.rptr="499" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c071838cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Chemical Workers&apos; Union v. Canadian Chemical Workers&apos; Union, Local 28 (Re)">Int&apos;l Chemical Workers&apos; Union v. Canadian Chemical Workers&apos; Union, Local 28 (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 499</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="223" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c071848cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)">Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar. 223</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In addition, the imposition of a trusteeship by a parent to effect a merger with another union,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Astgen" p2="Smith" post.rptr="657" pre.rptr="7" ref.id="Id8c098908cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Astgen v. Smith">Astgen v. Smith (1969), 7 D.L.R. (3d) 657, &lsqb;1970&rsqb; 1 O.R. 129 (C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> or to prevent a local from breaking away to join another union,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canadian Rexall Corp. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="557" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1976&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c098918cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canadian Rexall Corp. (Re)">Canadian Rexall Corp. (Re), &lsqb;1976&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept. 557</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> have been found to be lawful exercises of a union&apos;s trusteeship authority and would not require the Board to interfere. The Board has dismissed an application alleging a breach of the trusteeship provisions as serving no labour relations purpose, where the applicant is no longer a member of the defendant union, and the union local has been displaced as the bargaining agent for the applicant by another union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Marenchin" p2="LIUNA" post.rptr="16384" pre.rptr="2012" ref.id="Id8c098928cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Marenchin v. LIUNA">Marenchin v. LIUNA, 2012 CarswellOnt 16384 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lockett" p2="LIUNA" post.rptr="16350" pre.rptr="2012" ref.id="Id8c098938cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lockett v. LIUNA">Lockett v. LIUNA, 2012 CarswellOnt 16350 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:15</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Labour Board Jurisdiction &amp; Proceedings</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">
                  <trace>Trusteeship may have an impact on the substance of union conduct. For example, once a parent or national imposes a trusteeship, the trustee may take a different approach to bargaining, grievance resolution, or member relations than might have been taken by prior leadership. Nonetheless, </trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>where</trace> the governing labour statute is silent on trusteeship, labour relations boards will decline jurisdiction to hear a member&apos;s complaint regarding the legality of a trusteeship <ital>per se</ital>. In <ital>White v. Public Service Alliance of Canada</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="White" p2="Public Service Alliance of Canada" post.rptr="38" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c0bfa08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="White v. Public Service Alliance of Canada">White v. Public Service Alliance of Canada, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; C.P.R.S.R.B. No. 38</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Canada Public Service Staff Relations Board ruled that it had no authority to &ldquo;interfere in the internal affairs of the bargaining agent &hellip; in the absence of any specific legal provision giving it jurisdiction to decide such matters,&rdquo; even where discrimination may have occurred. <trace>Those actions may give rise to a duty of fair representation claim. The Alberta Labour Relations Board has clearly stated that the validity of a trusteeship cannot be addressed through a duty of fair representation complaint, regardless of the union&rsquo;s conduct while under trusteeship.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">27</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">
                            <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="Falcon" p2="Canadian Union of Public Employees, Local 1961" pre.rptr="2025" post.rptr="49" title="Falcon v. Canadian Union of Public Employees, Local 1961">Falcon v. Canadian Union of Public Employees, Local 1961, 2025 ALRB 49, 2025 CarswellAlta 1038 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                </footnote>1.5 </trace>In the absence of statutory authority, the proper legal forum for a <trace>complaint about the imposition of a </trace>trusteeship <trace.deleted/>is the courts.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">
                  <trace>Even </trace>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>where</trace> statutory provisions expressly grant labour relations boards authority to review trusteeships, that authority has been defined narrowly. The Ontario Board has <trace.deleted/>declined jurisdiction to hear a member&apos;s complaint regarding the legality of a trusteeship under those provisions. It has stated the restricted nature of its jurisdiction in trusteeship matters in <ital>Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel</ital>:<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="HERE, Local 75 and Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1166" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1994&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c0bfa18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="HERE, Local 75 and Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel (Re)">HERE, Local 75 and Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel (Re), &lsqb;1994&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Aug. 1166</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; section 84 &lsqb;now s. 89&rsqb; of the Act expressly contemplates the possibility of a &ldquo;trusteeship&rdquo;, and does not contemplate or provide for supervision or review by this Board until a year has gone by. Until that time, the actions of the Trustee are regulated by the union Constitution, with reference, as necessary, to the ordinary civil Courts. They are matters of internal union affairs over which this Board has no original jurisdiction, and it is by no means clear that the Board is the place where they can or should be resolved.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="HERE, Local 75 and Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1166" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1994&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c0bfa28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="HERE, Local 75 and Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel (Re)">HERE, Local 75 and Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel (Re), &lsqb;1994&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Aug. 1166</ref>
                            </cite>, at p. 1175.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
                <para.text date.updated="0">Similarly, the British Columbia Labour Relations Board stated in <ital>Construction, Maintenance &amp; Allied Workers Union, Local 2020 v. CEP, Local 470</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Construction, Maintenance &amp; Allied Workers Union, Local 2020" p2="CEP, Local 470" post.rptr="135" pre.rptr="213" ref.id="Id8c0e6b08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Construction, Maintenance &amp; Allied Workers Union, Local 2020 v. CEP, Local 470">Construction, Maintenance &amp; Allied Workers Union, Local 2020 v. CEP, Local 470 (2012), 213 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 135 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> that s. 150 of the <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital> &ldquo;&hellip; does not authorize the Board to scrutinize a national union&apos;s decision to impose a trusteeship against a standard of review or for conformity with its applicable constitutions and bylaws. Those are matters for a court to decide.&rdquo; Indeed, where the issue in a disputed trusteeship action involves the constitutional authority of a parent union to impose a trusteeship on a local, the appropriate judicial forum for the litigation would be the courts, even in a jurisdiction &mdash; such as British Columbia &mdash; where the labour relations board has some statutory authority over a local union&apos;s challenge to a trusteeship.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145" p2="AFM" post.rptr="1128" pre.rptr="2015" ref.id="Id8c0e6b18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145 v. AFM">Vancouver Musicians&apos; Assn., Local 145 v. AFM, 2015 CarswellBC 1128, 2015 BCCA 171</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">However, labour boards may have jurisdiction to challenge or review trusteeships under their inherent authority to hear complaints about and remedy contraventions of labour relations legislation. In such cases, applications and complaints will be subject to the same procedural and jurisdictional constraints the board uses to address other complaints. For example, labour boards have a general policy to require complainants to exhaust internal union appeal procedures before seeking statutory relief. However, if the complainant can demonstrate that the internal procedures are impractical, unfair or uneconomic, then a labour relations board may take jurisdiction.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UA, Local 170 and UA, Re" p2="" post.rptr="155" pre.rptr="7" ref.id="Id8c0e6b28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UA, Local 170 and UA, Re">UA, Local 170 and UA, Re (2018), 7 C.L.R.B.R. (3d) 155, 2018 CarswellBC 676 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>; <ital>Shayler</ital>, B.C.L.R.B. No. B411/94.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>IASTE, Local 348 (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.trace="20260211" date.updated="20260211">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IATSE, Local 348 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="79" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2001&rsqb;" ref.id="Id1b0f690fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="IATSE, Local 348 (Re)">IATSE, Local 348 (Re), &lsqb;2001&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 79</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the British Columbia Labour Relations Board ruled that, without assurances that the executive board of the parent union would be able to deal with an internal appeal against a trusteeship at its forthcoming board meeting, it would declare the trusteeship null and void. In Ontario, the courts have held that, because a union is not a &ldquo;person,&rdquo; a local union placed in trusteeship has no standing under the Ontario <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital> to complain in its own name that the imposition of a trusteeship violates unfair labour practice provisions. Such a complaint has to be filed by members of the local executive acting in their own name.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit" post.rptr="16" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2004&rsqb;" ref.id="Id1b11da0fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit">Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit, &lsqb;2004&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 16</ref>
                          </cite>. On the distinctive regulation of union personality in Ontario, see <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref">
                            <ref ref.id="Id1b11da1fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" secref="1:12">&sect;1:12</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:16</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Extending a Trusteeship</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Where the parent union has sought consent under s. 89(2) of the Ontario Act to continue a trusteeship after the initial 12 months, the Labour Relations Board has closely scrutinized the request to determine whether the continued suspension of local self-government is justified. The expectation of the Board is that 12 months should be a sufficient length of time in most cases to resolve the internal problems of the local under trusteeship. However, the legislation has also recognized that not every problem within a local can be settled within a year, and the Board will show some flexibility in special cases to extend a trusteeship for any period up to a second twelve months.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="223" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c10dc08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)">Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar. 223</ref>
                        </cite> at p. 225:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">The section &lsqb;84(1) now s. 89(1)&rsqb; allows a full twelve months in which a union can resolve the problems which led to the trusteeship being imposed. The very existence of such a time limit should prompt responsible union officials to move with reasonable dispatch to correct the problems which led to the imposition of trusteeship. The section, however, also makes allowance for the fact that not every problem which leads to a trusteeship being imposed may be able to be resolved in 12 months, and thus some flexibility has been provided by stipulating that trusteeships can be continued &hellip; for any further period of time up to twelve months &lsqb;with the consent of the Board&rsqb;.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                        <para.text date.updated="20241205">Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 1328" post.rptr="1524" pre.rptr="2023" ref.id="I62ffdd80a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 1328">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 1328, 2023 CarswellOnt 1524 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 1623" post.rptr="18899" pre.rptr="2019" ref.id="I62ffdd81a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 1623">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 1623, 2019 CarswellOnt 18899 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 4429" post.rptr="16385" pre.rptr="2012" ref.id="Id8c10dc18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 4429">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 4429, 2012 CarswellOnt 16385 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE, Local 4374" p2="CUPE, Local 4374" post.rptr="40478" pre.rptr="2006" ref.id="Id8c10dc28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE, Local 4374">CUPE, Local 4374, 2006 CanLII 40478 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canadian Paperworkers Union and CPU, Local 1199 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="99" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1993&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c134d08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canadian Paperworkers Union and CPU, Local 1199 (Re)">Canadian Paperworkers Union and CPU, Local 1199 (Re), &lsqb;1993&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Feb. 99</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The general test applied by the Board for a s. 89(2) consent is an assessment of the likely prejudice that may be suffered both by the local membership and by the parent if the trusteeship is extended, measured on the balance of conveniences.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Chemical Workers&apos; Union" p2="Canadian Chemical Workers&apos; Union, Local 28 (Re)" post.rptr="499" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c134d18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Chemical Workers&apos; Union v. Canadian Chemical Workers&apos; Union, Local 28 (Re)">Int&apos;l Chemical Workers&apos; Union v. Canadian Chemical Workers&apos; Union, Local 28 (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 499</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> When assessing the balance of convenience, the Ontario Board has stated that it will consider a number of factors, including:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <trace>&hellip;</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>
                      <trace>how</trace> many of the total local union members have indicated their opposition to the extension; how long it might take to adjudicate the dispute compared to the length of the extension sought; the need for an orderly transition from trusteeship to local governance; the extent of any plans outlined for achieving that; the grounds advanced for the extension; and the length of the extension sought.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 3903" post.rptr="3935" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2010&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c134d28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 3903">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 3903, &lsqb;2010&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 3935</ref>
                              </cite>, at para. 7<trace>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                  <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 5089" post.rptr="91468" pre.rptr="2025" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 5089">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 5089, 2025 CanLII 91468, 2025 CarswellOnt 14792 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                              </cite>.</trace>
                              <trace.deleted/>
                            </para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
                <para.text date.updated="0">When considering whether to grant an extension of a trusteeship, the Ontario Board requires that the union seeking the extension is to provide the bargaining unit members with notice of its request for an extension. The union must also notify the bargaining unit members that they have the opportunity to advise the Board of their position in response to the union&apos;s request.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="COPE (CLC)" p2="COPE, Local 473" post.rptr="1328" pre.rptr="2016" ref.id="Id8c134d38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="COPE (CLC) v. COPE, Local 473">COPE (CLC) v. COPE, Local 473, 2016 CarswellOnt 1328 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                  <trace> Where notice has been provided, the Board will normally grant an extension if the request is unoppposed.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">4.5 <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 3903" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2010&rsqb;" post.rptr="3935" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 3903">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 3903, &lsqb;2010&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 3935</ref>
                            </cite>, at para. 7; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 5089" pre.rptr="2025" post.rptr="91468" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 5089">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 5089, 2025 CanLII 91468, 2025 CarswellOnt 14792 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>4.5</trace>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">While the Ontario Board is prepared to permit a trusteeship to continue for a second period of 12 months, it has ruled that it is without jurisdiction to extend the trusteeship any longer than 24 months.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 4276 and 4276-01" post.rptr="3913" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2009&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c134d48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 4276 and 4276-01">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 4276 and 4276-01, &lsqb;2009&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 3913</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="20211028">
                <para.text date.updated="20241205">On applications for a trusteeship extension, the Board has consented to a continuation where a valid labour relations purpose has been established. However, the extension may be coupled with the imposition of conditions in the appropriate situation. In circumstances where the parent and the former executive of the local under trusteeship were engaged in ongoing litigation over the ownership of union assets before other judicial and arbitral forums, the Ontario Board was satisfied that the local membership would not be prejudiced by the parent&apos;s ongoing control, and it consented to an extension for another year in order to preserve the <ital>status quo.</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Chemical Workers&apos; Union" p2="Canadian Chemical Workers&apos; Union, Local 28 (Re)" post.rptr="499" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c15be08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Chemical Workers&apos; Union v. Canadian Chemical Workers&apos; Union, Local 28 (Re)">Int&apos;l Chemical Workers&apos; Union v. Canadian Chemical Workers&apos; Union, Local 28 (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 499</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lynch (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1145" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2005&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c15be18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lynch (Re)">Lynch (Re), &lsqb;2005&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 1145</ref>
                          </cite>, where the Ontario Board extended the trusteeship by a further seven months; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE and Metro Toronto Civic Employees&apos; Union, Local 43 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="478" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1997&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c15be28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE and Metro Toronto Civic Employees&apos; Union, Local 43 (Re)">CUPE and Metro Toronto Civic Employees&apos; Union, Local 43 (Re), &lsqb;1997&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May/June 478</ref>
                        </cite>, where the Ontario Board extended a trusteeship by six months in order to allow the parent union to rectify the ongoing financial and organizational problems of the local.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Where the local under trusteeship continued to suffer from ongoing internal strife, the Board accepted a plan of action presented by the parent and supported by a number of the local&apos;s members to restore self-government, and allowed the trusteeship to continue for an additional period of time.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20211028">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 4943" post.rptr="11774" pre.rptr="2019" ref.id="I5df0e160370e11ec80e0c0b599422da1" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 4943">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 4943, 2019 CarswellOnt 11774 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1568" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1980&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c182f08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America (Re)">United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America (Re), &lsqb;1980&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1568</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Similarly, the Board has granted its consent where the parent wanted the suspension of local autonomy to last only long enough to hold local elections,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="223" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c182f18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)">Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar. 223</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to complete a forensic audit,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 786" post.rptr="9543" pre.rptr="2018" ref.id="Id8c182f28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 786">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 786, 2018 CarswellOnt 9543 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to finish a review of the local&apos;s by-laws,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 2331" post.rptr="3185" pre.rptr="2018" ref.id="Id8c182f38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 2331">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 2331, 2018 CarswellOnt 3185 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> in order to bring the trusteeship to an orderly end,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="223" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c182f48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)">Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar. 223</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> to clear up a serious backlog of grievances and to initiate internal trial procedure complaints,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11.30</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 1328" post.rptr="1524" pre.rptr="2023" ref.id="I63018b30a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 1328">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 1328, 2023 CarswellOnt 1524 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and to rebuild trust with the local membership while training new officers.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11.70</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 3651" post.rptr="4992" pre.rptr="2024" ref.id="I63018b31a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 3651">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 3651, 2024 CarswellOnt 4992 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> When a parent union requested a short extension in order to prepare the new local executive for the next round of collective bargaining, the Board granted the application.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="CUPE, Local 416" post.rptr="2532" pre.rptr="2016" ref.id="Id8c182f58cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. CUPE, Local 416">CUPE v. CUPE, Local 416, 2016 CarswellOnt 2532 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">But where it has been asked to grant, in effect, a revolving-door consent, it has refused to do so.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canadian Union of Public Employees, Local 1883" p2="Canadian Union of Public Employees, Local 1883" post.rptr="1675" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c182f68cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canadian Union of Public Employees, Local 1883">Canadian Union of Public Employees, Local 1883, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; O.L.R.B. No. 1675</ref>
                        </cite>, where the Ontario Labour Relations Board refused a request by the parent union to renew the year-long trusteeship by a further year, and extended the trusteeship only by four months, with a possibility for a further renewal only if circumstances warranted.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="833" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1972&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c1aa008cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America (Re)">United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America (Re), &lsqb;1972&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 833</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Board had already given a four-month extension, and it refused any further continuation because it was not satisfied that the original purpose of the trusteeship was still being served. And where the parent had given no assurances that an election in the local would be held in the near future as per the international constitution, the Ontario Board has refused to grant an interim extension of a trusteeship.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW and IBEW, Local 1788 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="244" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1996&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c1aa018cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW and IBEW, Local 1788 (Re)">IBEW and IBEW, Local 1788 (Re), &lsqb;1996&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar./Apr. 244</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">When a parent union has submitted a late filing under s. 89(2) to request an extension of the trusteeship, the Ontario Board has shown various degrees of flexibility. In <ital>United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America, Local 1946 (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America, Local 1946 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="10" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1976&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c1d1108cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America, Local 1946 (Re)">United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America, Local 1946 (Re), &lsqb;1976&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Feb. 10</ref>
                          </cite>. See also <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1072" p2="" post.rptr="489" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1998&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c1d1118cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1072">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1072, &lsqb;1998&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 489</ref>
                          </cite>; and <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW and IBEW, Local 1788 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="244" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1996&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c1d1128cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW and IBEW, Local 1788 (Re)">IBEW and IBEW, Local 1788 (Re), &lsqb;1996&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar./Apr. 244</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Board refused to extend a trusteeship where a parent union had failed to show &ldquo;reasonable diligence&rdquo; in applying to the Board for a continuation. The Board also ruled that the time limits for initiating the twelve-month count are triggered when the local&apos;s autonomy is formally suspended, not when the parent&apos;s appointed trustee finally achieves full control of the local at some later date.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">17</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America, Local 1946 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="10" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1976&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c1d1138cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America, Local 1946 (Re)">United Brotherhood of Carpenters &amp; Joiners of America, Local 1946 (Re), &lsqb;1976&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Feb. 10</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 16.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, two years later in <ital>Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">18</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="223" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c1d1148cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re)">Operative Plasterers&apos; &amp; Cement Masons&apos; Int&apos;l Assn. of the United States and Canada (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar. 223</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> it consented to an extension despite a late filing by the parent. The Board reasoned that an inflexible policy of automatically dismissing an extension request could leave some locals in a state of disorder, thus thwarting the legislative goal of stable labour relations.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The imposition of a trusteeship by a parent and the abrogation of local democracy may trigger various natural justice rights. The British Columbia Labour Relations Board has stated that a local executive cannot be denied the right to counsel during an internal union appeal of a trusteeship.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IATSE, Local 348 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="79" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2001&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c1d1158cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IATSE, Local 348 (Re)">IATSE, Local 348 (Re), &lsqb;2001&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. 79</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Nor can it be denied the right to a fair internal hearing before an unbiased tribunal.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">20</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UA, Local 170 and UA, Re" p2="" post.rptr="155" pre.rptr="7" ref.id="Id8c1f8208cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UA, Local 170 and UA, Re">UA, Local 170 and UA, Re (2018), 7 C.L.R.B.R. (3d) 155, 2018 CarswellBC 676 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Joe Shayler and UA, Local 170, Re" p2="Joe Shayler and UA, Local 170, Re" post.rptr="4034" pre.rptr="1994" ref.id="Id8c1f8218cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Joe Shayler and UA, Local 170, Re">Joe Shayler and UA, Local 170, Re, 1994 CarswellBC 4034 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Board has refused to grant an interim order &mdash; which offers procedural relief only &mdash; to deposed executive members of a union local who have challenged the parent union&apos;s imposition of a trusteeship, where the granting of the request would amount to a substantive remedy.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">21</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="FEESO / OSSTF, District 31" p2="OSSTF" post.rptr="8101" pre.rptr="2016" ref.id="Id8c1f8228cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="FEESO / OSSTF, District 31 v. OSSTF">FEESO / OSSTF, District 31 v. OSSTF, 2016 CarswellOnt 8101 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="D&eacute;mor&eacute;" p2="OSSTF" post.rptr="8342" pre.rptr="2016" ref.id="Id8c1f8238cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="D&eacute;mor&eacute; v. OSSTF">D&eacute;mor&eacute; v. OSSTF, 2016 CarswellOnt 8342 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:17</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Unfair Labour Practice Provisions</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">While union trusteeship is only expressly regulated by the Ontario and British Columbia labour statutes, and only indirectly in those cases, that does not prevent a local union or its members from directly challenging a newly imposed trusteeship before a labour relations board. A potential source of legal attack is available through the labour statute&apos;s union unfair labour practice provisions. In <ital>Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Ironworkers (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Ironworkers (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1487" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1982&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c21f308cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Ironworkers (Re)">Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Ironworkers (Re), &lsqb;1982&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Oct. 1487</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Ontario Board ruled that it had the authority to exercise its broad remedial powers under the unfair labour practice provisions of the Act to inquire into a trusteeship, regardless of how long it has been in place. In the decision, the parent union was found to have placed the local under trusteeship because, in part, it wanted to penalize officers of the local who had filed a complaint against the parent under the Act respecting the legality of a recently signed collective agreement. The Ontario Board ruled that this was contrary to s. 80(2) &lsqb;now s. 87(2)&rsqb; of the Act (which forbids retaliation by a trade union against a person who has made a complaint or who may be about to participate in a proceeding before the Board), and it therefore had jurisdiction to intervene in the trusteeship. Although the Board fashioned a remedy that in this case only moderated the parent&apos;s ongoing conduct of the trusteeship, it quite clearly would have terminated the trusteeship had the circumstances justified doing so.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Ironworkers (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1487" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1982&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c21f318cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Ironworkers (Re)">Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Ironworkers (Re), &lsqb;1982&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Oct. 1487</ref>
                          </cite>, at p. 1531. The Ontario Board adopted the standard &ldquo;taint&rdquo; theory to analyze the complaint brought by the dissatisfied members of the trusteed local. Under this theory, if any of the reasons for the imposition of the trusteeship was motivated by an issue that was contrary to the unfair labour practice provisions in the <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital> &mdash; even if the reason was not the principal one &mdash; then the parent&apos;s actions would be open to attack by the Board, in spite of its limited jurisdiction to regulate trusteeships under s. 89. See the Board&apos;s reasoning at p. 1522. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit, District 12, OSSTF (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="682" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2003&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c21f328cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit, District 12, OSSTF (Re)">Toronto Occasional Teachers&apos; Bargaining Unit, District 12, OSSTF (Re), &lsqb;2003&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July/August 682</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Board subsequently revisited the issue of a challenge to a trusteeship by way of an unfair labour practice complaint in <ital>Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel.</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="HERE, Local 75 and Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1166" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1994&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c21f338cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="HERE, Local 75 and Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel (Re)">HERE, Local 75 and Westbury Howard Johnson Hotel (Re), &lsqb;1994&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Aug. 1166</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> It ruled that the imposition of a trusteeship upon a union local is not, <ital>per se</ital>, an unfair labour practice, nor is the attempt of a trustee to assume and manage the affairs of a local, by itself, illegal. While the Board warned that it has the discretionary authority to intervene in the appropriate case, it would do so only with persuasive evidence of &ldquo;coercion&rdquo; or &ldquo;intimidation&rdquo;.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">Statutory remedies on this basis are arguably available to members of locals that have been placed under a trusteeship in all jurisdictions across the country, depending on the particular language of the union unfair labour practice provisions in the applicable labour legislation.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="20211028">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Canada Labour Code</ital>, R.S.C. 1985, c. L-2, ss. 95, 96; Alta., <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, R.S.A. 2000, c. L-1, ss. 22, 151, 152; B.C., <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, R.S.B.C. 1996, c. 244, ss. 5(1), 9, 10(2),(3); Man., <ital>The Labour Relations Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. 1987, c. L10, ss. 8, 19; N.B., <ital>Industrial Relations Act</ital>, R.S.N.B. 1973, c. I-4, ss. 5, 8(3); Nfld. &amp; Lab., <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital>, R.S.N.L. 1990, c. L-1, ss. 25(3), 28(1); N.S., <ital>Trade Union Act</ital>, R.S.N.S. 1989, c. 475, s. 54; Ont., <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, S.O. 1995, c. 1, ss. 51(2), 73(2), 76, 87(2); P.E.I., <ital>Labour Act</ital>, R.S.P.E.I. 1988, c. L-1, s. 10(2); Que., <ital>Labour Code</ital>, C.Q.L.R., c. C-27, s. 13; Sask., <ital>The Trade Union Act</ital>, R.S.S. 1978, c. T-17, ss. 11(2), 36.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Such a complaint to a labour relations board would not have to await the exhaustion of internal procedures, if the remedies available under the union&apos;s constitution could not adequately address the issues between the parties, or if a substantial delay in the resolution of the issues was likely.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="233" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1982&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c246408cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re)">Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re), &lsqb;1982&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 233</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-46" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;47" folio="5-45" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;45" ?>
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:18</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Other</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A union local under trusteeship is still considered to be a &ldquo;union&rdquo; for the purposes of the applicable labour relations statute.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="584" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1992&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c26d508cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re)">Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re), &lsqb;1992&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 584</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bethlehem Copper Corp. and CAIMAW, Local 8 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="410" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8c26d518cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bethlehem Copper Corp. and CAIMAW, Local 8 (Re)">Bethlehem Copper Corp. and CAIMAW, Local 8 (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; 2 Can. L.R.B.R. 410 (B.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="S. McCord &amp; Co. Ltd. and Ontario Labour Relations Board (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="455" pre.rptr="4" ref.id="Id8c26d528cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="S. McCord &amp; Co. Ltd. and Ontario Labour Relations Board (Re)">S. McCord &amp; Co. Ltd. and Ontario Labour Relations Board (Re) (1956), 4 D.L.R. (2d) 455, &lsqb;1956&rsqb; O.R. 645 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Accordingly, despite its trusteed status, a union local or subordinate body is entitled to apply for a bargaining certificate to represent a unit of employees,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="584" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1992&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c26d538cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re)">Int&apos;l Assn. of Bridge, Structural &amp; Ornamental Iron Workers (Re), &lsqb;1992&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 584</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and it is expected to fulfill its fair representation duties.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bethlehem Copper Corp. and CAIMAW, Local 8 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="410" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8c26d548cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bethlehem Copper Corp. and CAIMAW, Local 8 (Re)">Bethlehem Copper Corp. and CAIMAW, Local 8 (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; 2 Can. L.R.B.R. 410 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Ontario Board has also taken jurisdiction of a continuation application even in a situation where the locals under trusteeship no longer held bargaining rights.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canadian Paperworkers Union and CPU, Local 1199 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="99" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1993&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c26d558cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canadian Paperworkers Union and CPU, Local 1199 (Re)">Canadian Paperworkers Union and CPU, Local 1199 (Re), &lsqb;1993&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Feb. 99</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">C</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Judicial Regulation of Trusteeships</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:19</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Introduction</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Judicial regulation of a union trusteeship arises where members of the affected local claim that the parent has violated the union&apos;s constitution in its suspension of the local&apos;s autonomy. Relying on the accepted legal principle that the constitution forms a contract among the members,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See generally: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Orchard" p2="Tunney" post.rptr="273" pre.rptr="8" ref.id="Id8c294608cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Orchard v. Tunney">Orchard v. Tunney (1957), 8 D.L.R. (2d) 273, &lsqb;1957&rsqb; S.C.R. 436</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lee" p2="Showmen&apos;s Guild of Great Britain" post.rptr="329" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1952&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8c294618cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lee v. Showmen&apos;s Guild of Great Britain">Lee v. Showmen&apos;s Guild of Great Britain, &lsqb;1952&rsqb; 2 Q.B. 329 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>. But see the evolution in the law expressed in <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Berry" p2="Pulley" post.rptr="651" pre.rptr="211" ref.id="Id8c294628cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Berry v. Pulley">Berry v. Pulley (2002), 211 D.L.R. (4th) 651 (S.C.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the courts have viewed local union autonomy as an important part of the constitutional bargain, and will intervene to strike down trusteeships where the parent&apos;s action has breached natural justice or the procedural guarantees in the constitution.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:20</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Natural Justice and Due Process Requirements</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The leading Canadian decision on natural justice and trusteeships is <ital>Warner v. Kiniak.</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Warner" p2="Kiniak." post.rptr="117" pre.rptr="20" ref.id="Id8c294638cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Warner v. Kiniak.">Warner v. Kiniak. (1980), 20 B.C.L.R. 117 (C.A.), leave to appeal to S.C.C. refused 33 N.R. 347n</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> In this case, the British Columbia Court of Appeal confirmed a trial judge&apos;s decision to declare a trusteeship null and void, on the grounds that the president of the international union had no authority under the union&apos;s constitution to appoint the members of the internal panel to hear the local&apos;s appeal. The parent had argued that, even if the appointments had been improperly made, the subsequent confirmation of the hearing panel&apos;s decision by the international executive board validated the trusteeship. The Court of Appeal rejected this argument, holding that the executive board could not cure the original defect in the panel&apos;s appointment:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; it is the hearing itself which affords the membership that due process. It is in that hearing that all the evidence is to be heard and, in fact, the real decision to be made as to whether the president was right in imposing the trusteeship. In view of the severe consequences of the imposition of a trusteeship, it is my view that the defect in the manner of appointment of the panel cannot be cured by the rubber stamp of the board.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="Warner" p2="Kiniak." post.rptr="117" pre.rptr="20" ref.id="Id8c2bb708cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Warner v. Kiniak.">Warner v. Kiniak. (1980), 20 B.C.L.R. 117 (C.A.), leave to appeal to S.C.C. refused 33 N.R. 347n</ref>
                            </cite>, at p. 126.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
                <para.text date.updated="0">It went on to rule that there are three elements of a trusteeship that a court will scrutinize in order to judge its validity: whether the constitution properly provides for the authority to impose a trusteeship, whether the constitutional provisions provide for natural justice, and whether the parent has strictly followed the constitutional provisions.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Other judicial decisions where a trusteeship has been challenged have also stressed the central importance of natural justice and procedural rights. The Manitoba Court of Appeal in <ital>McEvoy v. Capri</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McEvoy" p2="Capri" post.rptr="&para;14,009" pre.rptr="64" ref.id="Id8c2bb718cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McEvoy v. Capri">McEvoy v. Capri (1964), 64 C.L.L.C. &para;14,009 (Man. C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> held that it was contrary to natural justice to impose a trusteeship prior to the local being notified of the results of its internal appeal. An interim injunction suspending the trusteeship was continued. Similarly, in <ital>Kennedy v. Gillis</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Kennedy" p2="Gillis" post.rptr="82" pre.rptr="30" ref.id="Id8c2bb728cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Kennedy v. Gillis">Kennedy v. Gillis (1961), 30 D.L.R. (2d) 82, &lsqb;1961&rsqb; O.R. 878 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Astgen" p2="Smith" post.rptr="545" pre.rptr="69" ref.id="Id8c2bb738cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Astgen v. Smith">Astgen v. Smith (1968), 69 D.L.R. (2d) 545, &lsqb;1968&rsqb; 2 O.R. 454 (H.C.J.), affd 7 D.L.R. (3d) 657, &lsqb;1970&rsqb; 1 O.R. 129 (C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>, where the Court of Appeal invalidated a trusteeship and the removal of the local executive from office, where no prior notice of the charges had been given nor had a hearing been held.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the parent union had initiated a trusteeship and dismissed the <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-48" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;49" folio="5-47" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;47" ?>local executive from office without providing the local with a hearing. The Ontario High Court declared that the trusteeship was invalid. In its judgment, it ruled that even though the union constitution did not expressly provide for a hearing, the right of the local and its officers to have their case heard before an internal body was a condition precedent to the valid imposition of the trusteeship. The court warned that it would strictly construe general powers in a union constitution so as to curb the danger of abuse. As well, where a court has not been persuaded that the union&apos;s constitution authorized the president of the parent union to impose a trusteeship, and that the danger of irreparable harm has passed, it will grant an injunction to the union local to lift the trusteeship and permit local elections.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Liivoja" p2="Pomeroy" post.rptr="83" pre.rptr="78" ref.id="Id8c2e2808cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Liivoja v. Pomeroy">Liivoja v. Pomeroy (1998), 78 A.C.W.S. (3d) 83 (Ont. Ct. (Gen. Div.))</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Indeed, even in circumstances where a court endorsed the invocation of a trusteeship, it did so only after first satisfying itself that the internal hearings conformed to the constitution, the procedures were fairly applied, and the parent acted throughout in good faith.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Flaherty" p2="Callahan" post.rptr="177" pre.rptr="2017" ref.id="Id8c2e2818cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Flaherty v. Callahan">Flaherty v. Callahan, 2017 CarswellNfld 177, 2017 NLTD(G) 79 (N.L. T.D.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Flaherty" p2="Fewer" post.rptr="63" pre.rptr="66" ref.id="Id8c2e2828cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Flaherty v. Fewer">Flaherty v. Fewer (1987), 66 Nfld. &amp; P. E.I.R. 63 (Nfld. S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Only in <ital>CUPE v. Deveau</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE" p2="Deveau" post.rptr="44" pre.rptr="19" ref.id="Id8c2e2838cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE v. Deveau">CUPE v. Deveau (1976), 19 N.S.R. (2d) 44 (S.C.), affd 19 N.S.R. (2d) 24 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Astgen" p2="Smith" post.rptr="545" pre.rptr="69" ref.id="Id8c2e2848cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Astgen v. Smith">Astgen v. Smith (1968), 69 D.L.R. (2d) 545, &lsqb;1968&rsqb; 2 O.R. 454 (H.C.J.), affd 7 D.L.R. (3d) 657, &lsqb;1970&rsqb; 1 O.R. 129 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>, where Laskin J., in dissent, commented, at p. 684 (C.A.), upon the reading of a union constitution in words that would support the flexible interpretation given by the courts in <ital>Deveau</ital>: &ldquo;I would not read a trade union constitution as if it was a common law conveyance. The construction should be liberal, not restrictive &hellip;&rdquo;. For an example of the English courts giving a flexible interpretation of a union constitution, see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="British Actors&apos; Equity Association" p2="Goring" post.rptr="791" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c309908cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="British Actors&apos; Equity Association v. Goring">British Actors&apos; Equity Association v. Goring, &lsqb;1978&rsqb; I.C.R. 791 (H.L.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> has a Canadian court adjudicating a case involving a union trusteeship shown a willingness to read the constitutional procedures in a more relaxed manner. In this case, the union constitution stated that the trustee appointed by the parent to administer the local had to be re-appointed after twelve months. As no reappointment had been made within that time period, the local argued that the trustee no longer had authority to control the disputed assets or run the affairs of the local. Eschewing a strict reading of the constitution,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">It is noteworthy that the CUPE Constitution provided very strict limitations on the authority of the parent. In article 7.9, the constitution stated that its trusteeship provisions:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; shall not be construed as an encroachment on the autonomy of the chartered organizations &hellip; It is the intent of these sections to protect both the chartered organizations and the members of these chartered organizations. <ital>The intent of these sections shall be interpreted in the strictest legal sense</ital> &hellip; (Emphasis added.)</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Cowan C.J.T.D. held that, despite the expiry of the appointment, the trustee was entitled to retain possession of the local&apos;s assets, even if he could no longer control the local itself. The decision was upheld on appeal. While <ital>Deveau</ital> is not authority for a perpetual trusteeship, it does lend support for a more practical reading of a constitution in circumstances where it may be justified.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:21</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Procedure for Challenging a Trusteeship</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Procedurally, the preferred judicial avenue for a union local to challenge a trusteeship is by way of an action before the trial division of the appropriate court. Generally, the complexity of the facts involved in a trusteeship challenge may preclude the matter from being dealt with by way of an application for judicial review, despite the fact that the issues may revolve only around whether procedural errors were committed in the imposition of the trusteeship. As well, the Ontario Superior Court of Justice has stated a procedural preference for organizing a complex challenge to a trusteeship by way of a representative action, rather than through a class proceeding.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Brewery, Malt and Soft Drink Workers, Local 304" p2="BFCSD" post.rptr="66" pre.rptr="11" ref.id="Id8c309918cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Brewery, Malt and Soft Drink Workers, Local 304 v. BFCSD">Brewery, Malt and Soft Drink Workers, Local 304 v. BFCSD (1985), 11 O.A.C. 66 (Div. Ct.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A challenge to a trusteeship is subject to the requirement of first exhausting internal remedies.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Anderson" p2="Amalgamated Clothing Workers of America" post.rptr="&para; 14,015" pre.rptr="67" ref.id="Id8c330a08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Anderson v. Amalgamated Clothing Workers of America">Anderson v. Amalgamated Clothing Workers of America (1966), 67 C.L.L.C. &para; 14,015 (Man. Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>. It is to be noted that, as there is no common law duty to exhaust internal remedies, the parent must point to a constitutional provision requiring a local or a member to first resort to and complete internal appeal processes in order to rely upon the exhaustion defence in court.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, should the local be able to establish that the parent&apos;s decision was <ital>ultra vires</ital> its authority, or that an effective remedy would be unavailable through the internal appeal procedures, or that the constitutional rule relied upon by the parent was in conflict with public policy, then the courts would assume jurisdiction despite the local&apos;s failure to exhaust.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Anderson" p2="Amalgamated Clothing Workers of America" post.rptr="&para; 14,015" pre.rptr="67" ref.id="Id8c330a18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Anderson v. Amalgamated Clothing Workers of America">Anderson v. Amalgamated Clothing Workers of America (1966), 67 C.L.L.C. &para; 14,015 (Man. Q.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Howard" p2="Parrinton" post.rptr="395" pre.rptr="21" ref.id="Id8c330a28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Howard v. Parrinton">Howard v. Parrinton (1971), 21 D.L.R. (3d) 395, &lsqb;1971&rsqb; 3 O.R. 659 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Leroux" p2="Molgat" post.rptr="29" pre.rptr="67" ref.id="Id8c330a38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Leroux v. Molgat">Leroux v. Molgat (1985), 67 B.C.L.R. 29, 86 C.L.L.C. &para;14,004 (S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bimson" p2="Johnston" post.rptr="11" pre.rptr="10" ref.id="Id8c330a48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bimson v. Johnston">Bimson v. Johnston (1957), 10 D.L.R. (2d) 11, &lsqb;1957&rsqb; O.R. 519 (H.C.J.), affd 12 D.L.R. (2d) 379, &lsqb;1958&rsqb; O.W.N. 217 (C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A union member seeking relief against his union local, at a <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-50" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;77" folio="5-49" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;49"?>time when the local had been under a prolonged trusteeship, was nevertheless directed to utilize the union&apos;s local appeal process rather than seek relief through the courts. The New Brunswick Court of Queen&apos;s Bench was prepared to allow the union&apos;s internal mechanisms a reasonable opportunity to control and regulate its own affairs, notwithstanding the trusteeship.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Savard" p2="Dunn" post.rptr="359" pre.rptr="849" ref.id="Id8c330a58cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Savard v. Dunn">Savard v. Dunn (2008), 849 A.P.R. 359 (N.B.Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
                <value>REM.II Remedies&mdash;Injunctions</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:22</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Interlocutory Injunction</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Ousted local union officers whose local has been placed into trusteeship have the opportunity of challenging the parent union&apos;s decision by way of an interlocutory or interim injunction. This is a preliminary civil procedure step which permits an applicant to argue that the <ital>status quo ante</ital> should be restored by the courts until the matter can be litigated on the merits. The well-accepted test for whether an interlocutory injunction should be granted is found in the Supreme Court of Canada&apos;s 1994 ruling in <ital>RJR-MacDonald Inc.</ital>:<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RJR-MacDonald Inc." p2="Canada (Attorney General)" post.rptr="120" pre.rptr="1994" ref.id="Id8c357b08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RJR-MacDonald Inc. v. Canada (Attorney General)">RJR-MacDonald Inc. v. Canada (Attorney General), 1994 CarswellQue 120, &lsqb;1994&rsqb; 1 S.C.R. 311 <point>at p. 342</point> (S.C.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
                <list list.style="block" date.updated="0">
                  <list.item date.updated="0">
                    <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <designator date.updated="0">(i)</designator>
                    </name.block>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">There is a serious issue to be tried;</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </list.item>
                  <list.item date.updated="0">
                    <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <designator date.updated="0">(ii)</designator>
                    </name.block>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Irreparable harm would be sustained should the requested order not be granted; and</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </list.item>
                  <list.item date.updated="0">
                    <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <designator date.updated="0">(iii)</designator>
                    </name.block>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">The balance of convenience favours granting the injunction, after determining which of the two parties would suffer the grater harm from the granting or the refusal to grant the injunction pending the decision on the merits.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </list.item>
                </list>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In <ital>Kinnear v. Hanley</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Kinnear" p2="Hanley" post.rptr="3003" pre.rptr="2017" ref.id="Id8c357b18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Kinnear v. Hanley">Kinnear v. Hanley, 2017 CarswellOnt 3003, 2017 ONSC 1165</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Ontario Superior Court of Justice ruled that the actions taken by the international parent union to place a large Toronto transit union local into trusteeship and remove the local executive from office, shortly after the union president took several initial steps to secede from the parent union, justified the granting of an interlocutory injunction as requested by the ousted president. The Court was persuaded that union members have a right to dissent and to choose their representatives, therefore there was a serious issue to be tried; the parent union had used the trusteeship to remove elected union leaders from office for exercising their right to dissent, which deprived the membership of an open and informed debate, thereby satisfying the criteria for irreparable harm; and the balance of convenience favoured restoring the pre-trusteeship <ital>status quo</ital> because it would allow the ousted leadership access to the local&apos;s resources and enable a free and open membership debate on the local&apos;s future.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">An interlocutory injunction was also sought by ousted local executive members before the Newfoundland and Labrador Supreme Court (Trial Division) in <ital>Flaherty v. Callahan.</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Flaherty" p2="Callahan." post.rptr="177" pre.rptr="2017" ref.id="Id8c37ec08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Flaherty v. Callahan.">Flaherty v. Callahan., 2017 CarswellNfld 177, 2017 NLTD(G) 79</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> The international parent union had placed the local into trusteeship and removed all the elected and appointed staff without a hearing, following the release of an internal investigation report which alleged financial mismanagement of the local&apos;s funds. Applying the <ital>RJR MacDonald</ital> test, the Court determined that the international president did rely upon evidence by which he could rationally determine the basis for imposing a trusteeship, pending an internal hearing, thereby defeating the &ldquo;serious issue&rdquo; criteria; only speculative evidence of irreparable harm was presented by the applicants; and the balance of convenience did not favour the return of the <ital>status quo</ital>, given that a full internal hearing would be held within the month.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management&mdash;Trusteeships</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:23</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Other</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In Newfoundland and British Columbia, where the governing legislation requires the trade union to acquire provincial domicile,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="crime" type="secondaryxref">
                            <ref ref.id="Icff9d6908cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" secref="1:14 to 1:26">&dblsect;1:14 to 1:26</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> a judicial challenge by a local to a trusteeship may require the issue of legal status to be first resolved.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Fishermen, Food and Allied Workers Union" p2="UFCW, Local 1252" post.rptr="332" pre.rptr="117" ref.id="Id8c37ec18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Fishermen, Food and Allied Workers Union v. UFCW, Local 1252">Fishermen, Food and Allied Workers Union v. UFCW, Local 1252 (1994), 117 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 332, 94 C.L.L.C. &para;14,037 (Nfld. S.C.), affd 124 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 201 sub nom. UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin (Nfld. C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In these provinces, neither the imposition of a trusteeship, nor its lengthy maintenance, necessarily alters the local or provincial status of a trade union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Fishermen, Food and Allied Workers Union" p2="UFCW, Local 1252" post.rptr="332" pre.rptr="117" ref.id="Id8c37ec28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Fishermen, Food and Allied Workers Union v. UFCW, Local 1252">Fishermen, Food and Allied Workers Union v. UFCW, Local 1252 (1994), 117 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 332, 94 C.L.L.C. &para;14,037 (Nfld. S.C.), affd 124 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 201 sub nom. UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin (Nfld. C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Where a parent has successfully obtained a judicial order to enjoin local executive members from attempting to disaffiliate their locals, it may seek a subsequent motion for contempt against the local executive members if it believes that the judicial order has been disobeyed. However, unless it establishes the factual basis for the contempt order with adequate particulars, its motion will be denied.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ginter" p2="Gardon" post.rptr="489" pre.rptr="53" ref.id="Id8c3a5d08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ginter v. Gardon">Ginter v. Gardon (2001), 53 O.R. (3d) 489 (S.C.J.), additional reasons 2001 CarswellOnt 1335</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
  </analytical.level.body>
</analytical.level>
<analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
    <metadata field="classification">
      <value>LAB.I.1.b Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Labour relations boards&mdash;Jurisdiction</value>
      <value>LAB.I.2.b Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs</value>
    </metadata>
  </metadata.block>
  <front date.updated="0">
    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
      <designator date.updated="0">IV</designator>
      <name date.updated="0">Statutory Regulation of Parent-Local relations in Ontario: Just Cause</name>
    </outline.name.block>
  </front>
  <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
    <section.block>
      <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
        <section.front date.updated="0">
          <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
            <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
            <designator date.updated="0">5:24</designator>
            <name date.updated="0">Introduction</name>
          </outline.name.block>
        </section.front>
        <section.body date.updated="0">
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">Beyond the express regulation of trusteeship in Ontario and British Columbia, discussed above, Ontario is the only jurisdiction in Canada that regulates the relationship between a parent and its local directly by statute. Ontario enacted amendments to the <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital>
              <footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">R.S.O. 1990, c. L.2, ss. 138.2, 138.3, 138.5, 138.6 (added 1993, c. 36, ss. 1, 2) &mdash; now <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, S.O. 1995, c. 1, ss. 145&ndash;150.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> in 1993 that applied to unions in the Ontario construction industry. The purpose of the amendments was to provide greater autonomy to union locals in Ontario&apos;s construction industry from their parent unions, which are predominately headquartered in the United States.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">In <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="CJA, Local 1072 and CJA (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="942" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1997&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c3a5d18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CJA, Local 1072 and CJA (Re)">CJA, Local 1072 and CJA (Re), &lsqb;1997&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept./Oct. 942</ref>
                    </cite>, the Ontario Labour Relations Board held that, for the provisions of Bill 80 to apply, both the parent and the local union had to be a &ldquo;construction&rdquo; union. It defined a construction union as a union that represents at least one bargaining unit comprised exclusively of construction employees, according to established trade union practice as it pertains to the construction industry. In this case, although the parent union predominately represented construction workers, the local was primarily comprised of industrial workers, and did not represent a bargaining unit made up of exclusively construction workers. Thus, the application to the Labour Relations Board by the local complaining against the trusteeship imposed by the parent did not fall under the provisions of Bill 80.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">The core of these amendments is a prohibition against interference in the government of a local union by a parent without just cause. They also increase the role and authority of construction locals within the union in the areas of bargaining rights, work and geographic jurisdiction, and the administration of benefit plans.</para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">In 2018, the <ital>Act</ital> was amended further to extend such &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; protection to all union locals in the province.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, S.O. 1995, c. 1, Sched. A, s. 89.1.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
            </footnote> Modeled on the construction industry provisions, the new s. 89.1 directs that parent trade unions &ldquo;shall not, without just cause, assume supervision or control, or otherwise interfere with a local trade union directly or indirectly in such a way that the autonomy of the local trade union is affected.&rdquo; It expressly prohibits a parent from removing or changing the duties of a local union official without just cause. And it prohibits a parent union from imposing penalties on local members and officials without just cause.</para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.trace="20260211" date.updated="20260211">
            <para.text date.updated="0">In cases where the legislation conflicts with the parent union constitution, the legislation prevails. In particular, compliance with the constitution is not determinative of whether there was just cause for a parent union&apos;s interference with local autonomy.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">Ont., <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, ss. 89.1(4) &amp; 145(3).</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.trace="20241205" date.updated="20241205">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">The definition of a &ldquo;parent union&rdquo; in these provisions is broad.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, S.O. 1995, c. 1, Sched. A, ss. 89.1(1) &amp; 145(1).</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> In addition to the statutory inclusion of &ldquo;provincial, national or international trade unions&rdquo; within the definition, the Ontario Labour Relations Board has also found that a &ldquo;parent union&rdquo; can include a council of trade unions and a provincial district council.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Carpenters&apos; District Council of Ontario, UBCJA" p2="Ironclad Developments Inc." post.rptr="13421" pre.rptr="2023" ref.id="I6307ccc0a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="Carpenters&apos; District Council of Ontario, UBCJA v. Ironclad Developments Inc.">Carpenters&apos; District Council of Ontario, UBCJA v. Ironclad Developments Inc., 2023 CarswellOnt 13421 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Greater Toronto Sewer and Watermain Contractors Assn." p2="LIUNA, Local 183" post.rptr="6681" pre.rptr="2022" ref.id="I6307f3d0a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="Greater Toronto Sewer and Watermain Contractors Assn. v. LIUNA, Local 183">Greater Toronto Sewer and Watermain Contractors Assn. v. LIUNA, Local 183, 2022 CarswellOnt 6681 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.trace="20260211" date.updated="20260211">
            <para.text date.updated="0">Given the intended purpose of the just cause provisions, the Board has ruled that they create new rights only for union locals themselves. While the provisions prohibit interference with members and officials of the local, individual members have no standing to bring complaints under these provisions where it cannot be established that their claim is grounded in an alleged interference by a parent in local autonomy.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Mitchinson" p2="First District, Canada" post.rptr="2684" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2009&rsqb;" ref.id="Id1b4c720fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="Mitchinson v. First District, Canada">Mitchinson v. First District, Canada, &lsqb;2009&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 2684, reconsideration refused &lsqb;2009&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 3610</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="McKenny" post.rptr="1042" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2002&rsqb;" ref.id="Id1b4c721fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="McKenny">McKenny, &lsqb;2002&rsqb; O.L.R.B. No. 1042</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
        </section.body>
      </section>
      <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
        <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
          <metadata field="classification">
            <value>LAB.I.1.b Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Labour relations boards&mdash;Jurisdiction</value>
          </metadata>
        </metadata.block>
        <section.front date.updated="0">
          <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
            <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
            <designator date.updated="0">5:25</designator>
            <name date.updated="0">Local Participation in Collective Bargaining</name>
          </outline.name.block>
        </section.front>
        <section.body date.updated="0">
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">Section 146 of the Ontario <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital> creates minimum standards for union local participation in collective bargaining. Where the parent union is either the certified bargaining agent or a party to the collective agreement, the local union is deemed to share the bargaining rights or be a party to the collective agreement. While these provisions do not apply to the industrial, commercial and institutional (ICI) sectors of the Ontario construction industry, the Minister of Labour has the discretion, upon the request of either the local, the parent or the employer, to require the parent and its locals in the ICI sector to form a council of trade unions for the purpose of conducting bargaining and concluding a collective agreement.</para.text>
          </para>
        </section.body>
      </section>
      <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
        <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
          <metadata field="classification">
            <value>LAB.I.2.b.i.E Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Constitution&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
          </metadata>
        </metadata.block>
        <section.front date.updated="0">
          <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
            <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
            <designator date.updated="0">5:26</designator>
            <name date.updated="0">&ldquo;Just Cause&rdquo; Required for Dissolution and Alteration of Jurisdiction</name>
          </outline.name.block>
        </section.front>
        <section.body date.updated="0">
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">Section 147 of the Ontario Act provides that a parent cannot, without &ldquo;just cause&rdquo;, alter the geographic, sectoral or work jurisdiction of a local trade union. In determining whether &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; for an alteration exists, the Labour Relations Board is required to consider a number of factors, including the union constitution, the ability of the local to carry out its duties under the Act, the wishes of the local membership, and whether the alteration by the parent would affect labour relations in the construction industry. Applying these factors, the Ontario Labour Relations Board has ruled that &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; requires more than the &ldquo;right to be wrong&rdquo; standard found in the application of the duty of fair representation under s. 74. The Board held that &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; means:<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IBEW, Local 1739" p2="IBEW, First District, Canada" post.rptr="4753" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c3f3f08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada">IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4753</ref>
                      </cite>; Also see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="LIUNA, Local 1081" p2="LIUNA" post.rptr="10968" pre.rptr="2015" ref.id="Id8c3f3f18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="LIUNA, Local 1081 v. LIUNA">LIUNA, Local 1081 v. LIUNA, 2015 CarswellOnt 10968, &lsqb;2015&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 2084</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="UBCJA, Local 1256" p2="UBCJA" post.rptr="3176" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c3f3f28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UBCJA, Local 1256 v. UBCJA">UBCJA, Local 1256 v. UBCJA, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 3176, reconsideration refused 2011 CarswellOnt 12039</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IBEW, Local 1788 and IBEW (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="161" pre.rptr="29" ref.id="Id8c3f3f38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 1788 and IBEW (Re)">IBEW, Local 1788 and IBEW (Re) (1996), 29 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161 <point>at p. 192,</point> &lsqb;1996&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. February 70</ref>
                      </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="CJA, Local 1256 and CJA (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="715" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2008&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c3f3f48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CJA, Local 1256 and CJA (Re)">CJA, Local 1256 and CJA (Re), &lsqb;2008&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept/Oct 715</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Labourers&apos; International Union of North America" p2="Labourers&apos; International Union of North America" post.rptr="2859" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c3f3f58cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Labourers&apos; International Union of North America">Labourers&apos; International Union of North America, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 2859</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
            <quote.block date.updated="0">
              <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">&ldquo;&lsqb;W&rsqb;ell grounded, fair, equitable and proper.&rdquo; It has been suggested that &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; requires a higher standard than merely acting reasonably and in good faith in that it requires an examination and evaluation of the basis for the decision under consideration, and the justice and reasonableness thereof.</para.text>
              </quote.para>
            </quote.block>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">In <ital>IBEW, Local 1788 and IBEW (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IBEW, Local 1739" p2="IBEW, First District, Canada" post.rptr="4753" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c41b008cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada">IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4753</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> the Ontario Board&apos;s first significant ruling under the so-called Bill 80 provisions, it considered an application by a construction union local against the decision of its parent International to re-assign some of its jurisdictional work to other union locals within the province. To determine whether the International had just cause to alter its local&apos;s jurisdiction, the Board asked itself two questions: (i) did the International act in accordance with its constitution, and (ii) would the alteration facilitate viable and stable collective bargaining without causing serious labour relations problems? On the evidence, the Board was satisfied that the International&apos;s alteration &ldquo;was a sensible and rational way of finally putting an end to the internal IBEW discord and putting the jurisdictional issue to rest. From a labour relations perspective, that can only be a good thing.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IBEW, Local 1739" p2="IBEW, First District, Canada" post.rptr="4753" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c41b018cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada">IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4753</ref>
                      </cite>, at p. 198. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="UA, Local 508" p2="UA" post.rptr="118" pre.rptr="232" ref.id="Id8c41b028cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UA, Local 508 v. UA">UA, Local 508 v. UA (2013), 232 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 118, 2013 CarswellOnt 14592 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="0">Later decisions have elaborated on the meaning of &ldquo;just cause&rdquo;. Where a local union had voluntarily affiliated with a provincial council, and agreed to abide by both the council&apos;s by-laws and the international parent&apos;s constitution, it was unable to subsequently argue that the alteration of its local jurisdiction was without just cause.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="CJA, Local 93 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="52" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2006&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c41b038cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CJA, Local 93 (Re)">CJA, Local 93 (Re), &lsqb;2006&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Jan./Feb. 52</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> The actions of an international parent in altering the ability of the local union to elect its own business representatives and removing the local&apos;s ability to enter into certain collective agreements in its own name were found to constitute just cause, because the international parent had acted on rational grounds to improve the effectiveness of the union and it had obtained majority support for the changes from the broader union leadership in Ontario following a period of wide consultation.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="UBCJA, Local 1256" p2="UBCJA" post.rptr="3176" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c442108cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UBCJA, Local 1256 v. UBCJA">UBCJA, Local 1256 v. UBCJA, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 3176, reconsideration refused 2011 CarswellOnt 12039</ref>
                    </cite>. At para. 108, the Board stated:</para.text>
                    <quote.block date.updated="0">
                      <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; sections 147 and 149 did not freeze all union structures in the form they existed at the time those sections were made effective (May 1, 1992) absent unanimous consent &hellip; on the part of the local union.</para.text>
                      </quote.para>
                    </quote.block>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Board has also found just cause for the intervention of a parent union under Bill 80 where the affairs of the local were in disarray, its political and administrative officers had quit or defected to a rival, and its finances had been subverted,<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Interior Systems Contractors Assn. of Ontario and Drywall Acouctic Lathing and Insulation, Local 675 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1082" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1995&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c442118cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Interior Systems Contractors Assn. of Ontario and Drywall Acouctic Lathing and Insulation, Local 675 (Re)">Interior Systems Contractors Assn. of Ontario and Drywall Acouctic Lathing and Insulation, Local 675 (Re), &lsqb;1995&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. August 1082</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> and where the mandated merger of two locals with another larger local was the only practical solution to the union&apos;s declining share of regional construction work.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IBEW, Local 1739" p2="IBEW, First District, Canada" post.rptr="4753" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c442128cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada">IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4753</ref>
                      </cite>. In subsequent rulings on this issue, the Ontario Board has stated that it has no primary jurisdiction over union welfare trust funds and pension funds, nor does it have authority over the union locals&apos; building corporations. Applying its statutory powers, the Board did provide direction with respect to out-of-work lists and hiring hall rules, the hiring of union organizers, the provision of certified public audits and the contribution rules towards the locals&apos; stabilization and market recovery funds. See <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IBEW, Local 1739" p2="IBEW" post.rptr="7912" pre.rptr="2012" ref.id="Id8c469208cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW">IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, 2012 CarswellOnt 7912 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                      </cite>; and <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IBEW, Local 1739" p2="IBEW, First District, Canada" post.rptr="5472" pre.rptr="2012" ref.id="Id8c469218cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada">IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada, 2012 CarswellOnt 5472 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                      </cite>. These questions were subsequently adjudicated and determined by the Ontario Superior Court of Justice: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Leduc" p2="International Brotherhood Electrical Workers" post.rptr="19179" pre.rptr="2019" ref.id="Id8c469228cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers">Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers, 2019 CarswellOnt 19179, 2019 ONSC 6507</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> However, where a union decision-making process was designed by the international parent union to exclude select Canadian construction union locals from an effective voice in the restructuring of their Canadian operations, the Ontario Board has ruled that this would breach s. 147. In <ital>BAC, Locals 6 and 7 and Brick and Allied Craft Union of Canada (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="BAC, Locals 6 and 7 and Brick and Allied Craft Union of Canada (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="193" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2004&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c469238cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="BAC, Locals 6 and 7 and Brick and Allied Craft Union of Canada (Re)">BAC, Locals 6 and 7 and Brick and Allied Craft Union of Canada (Re), &lsqb;2004&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar./Apr. 193</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
            </footnote> the Board found that the actions of the parent union amounted to bad faith and &ldquo;a travesty of lawful, democratic decision making&rdquo;, and therefore failed to meet the &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; standard.</para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Board, in <ital>CJA, Local 1256 and CJA (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IBEW, Local 1739" p2="IBEW, First District, Canada" post.rptr="4753" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c469248cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada">IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4753</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> has stated that parent unions and their affiliated locals owe reciprocal duties of openness to each other. The local union has a duty of honest and open communication with the parent on matters where the parent has a legitimate interest and a right to be informed. Similarly, a parent union must both be receptive to the local union&apos;s concerns and the local&apos;s values, and also be open to considering the information that it receives from the local. &ldquo;Before the parent union can evaluate the nature of any dispute and make a decision about what, if anything, it should do, an open and honest flow of all information relevant to the dispute or the potential dispute, must take place.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IBEW, Local 1739" p2="IBEW, First District, Canada" post.rptr="4753" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c490308cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada">IBEW, Local 1739 v. IBEW, First District, Canada, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4753</ref>
                    </cite>, at para. 53.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="0">The occasion may arise when a parent union wishes to dissolve its relationship with a local under the Bill 80 provisions. The Ontario Board, in <ital>IUBAC, Local 25 and IUBAC (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IUBAC, Local 25 and IUBAC (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="101" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2007&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c490318cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IUBAC, Local 25 and IUBAC (Re)">IUBAC, Local 25 and IUBAC (Re), &lsqb;2007&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Jan./Feb. 101</ref>
                    </cite>, at para. 18.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> has provided examples of circumstances under s. 147 that may justify an international parent union dissolving a local union, such as: a precipitous fall-off in local membership; a dysfunctional level of internal strife in the local; or a particularly poorly performing local leadership. The Board has also ruled that a parent union was entitled to dissolve a local, despite its opposition, and amalgamate it with a regional council or other union locals in order to preserve bargaining strength,<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America" p2="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America" post.rptr="1" pre.rptr="70" ref.id="Id8c490328cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America (2000), 70 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 1 sub nom. Lake Ontario Carpenters&apos; District Council and CJA (Re), &lsqb;2001&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 1631</ref>
                      </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="UBCJA" p2="UBCJA, Local 446" post.rptr="972" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2009&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c490338cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UBCJA v. UBCJA, Local 446">UBCJA v. UBCJA, Local 446, &lsqb;2009&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 972</ref>
                    </cite>, where the Ontario Labour Relations Board stated, at para. 59:</para.text>
                    <quote.block date.updated="0">
                      <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">The evidence before both the UBCJA and this Board overwhelmingly demonstrates that the alteration would facilitate viable and stable collective bargaining without causing serious labour relations problems, and would be in the best interests of the UBCJA and the members of Locals 446 and 2486, despite the opposition expressed by the membership of Local 446. The UBCJA&apos;s decision was accordingly, &ldquo;a fair and reasonable one in all the circumstances&rdquo;.</para.text>
                      </quote.para>
                    </quote.block>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> in circumstances where a local was resistant to adopting an effective organizing and training strategy.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="LIUNA, Local 1081" p2="LIUNA" post.rptr="1" pre.rptr="289" ref.id="Id8c4b7408cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="LIUNA, Local 1081 v. LIUNA">LIUNA, Local 1081 v. LIUNA (2016), 289 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 1, 2016 CarswellOnt 9771 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> A parent union is also entitled to sever an unproductive relationship with a local under s. 147, as long as it does so without a coercive or punitive motive and it can establish &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; for the termination of relations.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IUBAC, Local 25 and IUBAC (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="101" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2007&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c4b7418cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IUBAC, Local 25 and IUBAC (Re)">IUBAC, Local 25 and IUBAC (Re), &lsqb;2007&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Jan./Feb. 101</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
            </footnote> However, if the consequence of the severance would likely cause serious harm to labour relations in the construction industry, then the Board will deny the parent&apos;s request to revoke its relationship with the local because one of the key elements of the &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; requirement has not been satisfied.</para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="0">The provisions of s. 147(5) provide that, once a challenge is made by a local union to the alteration of its jurisdiction through the filing of a complaint under the Act, the organizational changes instituted by the parent are undone until the Ontario Labour Relations Board disposes of the matter. This applies even where the parent&apos;s changes are ultimately justified.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Labourers&apos; International Union of North America" p2="Labourers&apos; International Union of North America" post.rptr="2859" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c4b7428cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Labourers&apos; International Union of North America">Labourers&apos; International Union of North America, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 2859</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU" p2="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU" post.rptr="161" pre.rptr="64" ref.id="Id8c4b7438cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU">IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU (2000), 64 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept./Oct. 891</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Board has accepted that it is entitled to consider the parent union&apos;s conduct under ss. 147 and 149 against a natural justice standard, but it has also rejected the argument that a failure on the parent union&apos;s part to afford the local union natural justice will necessarily void the parent union&apos;s actions. While a consideration of natural justice factors falls within the Board&apos;s discretion under these provisions, much depends upon the procedural requirements in the specific union constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America" p2="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America" post.rptr="1" pre.rptr="70" ref.id="Id8c4de508cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America (2000), 70 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 1 sub nom. Lake Ontario Carpenters&apos; District Council and CJA (Re), &lsqb;2001&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 1631</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers" p2="International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers" post.rptr="2470" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1999&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c4de518cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers">International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers, &lsqb;1999&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 2470</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
        </section.body>
      </section>
      <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
        <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
          <metadata field="classification">
            <value>LAB.I.1.a Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Labour relations boards&mdash;Nature and composition</value>
            <value>LAB.I.2.b.ii Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Organization and management</value>
          </metadata>
        </metadata.block>
        <section.front date.updated="0">
          <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
            <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
            <designator date.updated="0">5:27</designator>
            <name date.updated="0">&ldquo;Just Cause&rdquo; Required to Impose Trusteeships</name>
          </outline.name.block>
        </section.front>
        <section.body date.updated="0">
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">The Act also restricts the ability of a parent union or council of trade unions to interfere with the autonomy of a local. Section 89.1 forbids the parent from placing the local under trusteeship or supervision, or from removing, changing the duties, or penalizing local union officers,<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">For a union officer removed from office to successfully claim a remedy under s. 149(2), she or he must establish that the removal from office was directly linked to the autonomy of the union local. The Ontario Board will not intervene under this provision where the removal was an exclusively internal union matter unrelated to an autonomy issue: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="CJA, Local 93 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="52" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2006&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c4de528cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CJA, Local 93 (Re)">CJA, Local 93 (Re), &lsqb;2006&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 52</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Smith and CJA, Local 27 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="214" pre.rptr="32" ref.id="Id8c4de538cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Smith and CJA, Local 27 (Re)">Smith and CJA, Local 27 (Re) (1996), 32 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 214, &lsqb;1996&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July/August 621</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
            </footnote> unless it can establish &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; for the action. The subsection requires the Board to consider the union constitution when determining whether &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; exists, but it is not bound by it and is free to consider other appropriate factors. Notably, all of the caselaw concerning the application of the just cause standard in the review of such interference occurred when these protections applied only the construction industry, under the now-repealed section 149. Section 89.1, which expands the just cause standard against interference to all sectors, has not yet been tested. While the expansion of the provision to all sectors is unlikely to disrupt established interpretations of these provisions as they apply to the construction sector, it should be borne in mind that slightly different principles may apply in applications to non-construction unions.</para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="0">Reasonable dissent is one of the appropriate factors that the Ontario Board will consider. In <ital>Woods and IBEW, Local 1788 (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Woods and IBEW, Local 1788 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1022" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1997&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c4de548cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Woods and IBEW, Local 1788 (Re)">Woods and IBEW, Local 1788 (Re), &lsqb;1997&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. November/December 1022</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> the Board stated that &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; for the imposition by the parent union of a trusteeship will not be found if the local has engaged in dissent that has not irreparably affected the core values of the parent. Applying this standard, the Ontario Board has held that the expenditure of union funds on surreptitious surveillance in order to preserve the positions of those in the local union leadership, and the forging of collective agreements by the local&apos;s officers, are inconsistent with the core values of the parent union, and thereby satisfy the &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; standard.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Amacon Construction Ltd. and LIUNA, Local 183 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="48" pre.rptr="132" ref.id="Id8c505608cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Amacon Construction Ltd. and LIUNA, Local 183 (Re)">Amacon Construction Ltd. and LIUNA, Local 183 (Re) (2007), 132 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 48 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> The Board has also stated that it will not, as a rule, &ldquo;micromanage&rdquo; the parent&apos;s trusteeship of a local. However, as an exception, it would be prepared to order the parent to prepare a plan for the termination of the trusteeship in circumstances where the level of parent-local animosity has corroded the ability of the parties to arrive at a viable plan by themselves.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Amacon Construction Ltd. and LIUNA, Local 183 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="48" pre.rptr="132" ref.id="Id8c505618cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Amacon Construction Ltd. and LIUNA, Local 183 (Re)">Amacon Construction Ltd. and LIUNA, Local 183 (Re) (2007), 132 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 48 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">The Ontario Board provided a detailed list of principles on how it will assess a contested trusteeship, in <ital>Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC.</ital>
              <footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC" p2="" post.rptr="120" pre.rptr="71" ref.id="Id8c52c708cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC">Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC (2001), 71 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 120 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
            </footnote> These principles seek to balance the right of the parent union to protect and advance the primary values and institutional integrity of the union, and the right of the union local to engage in legitimate dissent. The Board&apos;s non-exhaustive list of principles consists of the following:</para.text>
            <list list.style="block" date.updated="0">
              <list.item date.updated="0">
                <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <designator date.updated="0">(1)</designator>
                </name.block>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">A parent union has the right to protect and advance the primary values and the institutional integrity of the union. A parent and a local union may be in conflict, but they are not strangers in an arm&apos;s-length relationship.</para.text>
                </para>
              </list.item>
              <list.item date.updated="0">
                <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <designator date.updated="0">(2)</designator>
                </name.block>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">The parent union does not require the consent of the local union to take action.</para.text>
                </para>
              </list.item>
              <list.item date.updated="0">
                <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <designator date.updated="0">(3)</designator>
                </name.block>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">The parent union may impose a temporary trusteeship on a local union to correct a specific problem and must relinquish that control of the local union once the problem has been corrected.</para.text>
                </para>
              </list.item>
              <list.item date.updated="0">
                <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <designator date.updated="0">(4)</designator>
                </name.block>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">The power of the parent union to impose a trusteeship (in the classic sense) is limited by s. 149 &lsqb;of the Ontario <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>&rsqb;. The section is in part an attempt to address a central tension in the internal affairs of most construction unions. That tension springs from the reality that while a union&apos;s organizational authority is highly concentrated in the hands of the parent, the primary activities of the union (collective bargaining and benefit administration) are in the hands of the local. The Act has changed that relationship by statutorily mandating a level of institutional autonomy for local unions, notwithstanding anything in the parent union&apos;s constitution.</para.text>
                </para>
              </list.item>
              <list.item date.updated="0">
                <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <designator date.updated="0">(5)</designator>
                </name.block>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">Local unions are entitled to be in conflict with their parent unions. The extent to which they are entitled to be in conflict is inevitably fact driven. It will usually be the case that a local union is entitled to avail itself of any rights to which it is entitled <ital>by virtue of the statute</ital>, notwithstanding any degree of conflict with the parent that the exercise of such rights may generate. The issue of the institutional relationship between the parent union and local unions in Ontario is one which may lead to conflict. The fact that a local union may wish to pursue a more independent course from the parent than the parent would like is not, <ital>in and of itself</ital>, just cause for interfering in local union&apos;s affairs by means of trusteeship or other methods of control. The steps a local union undertakes in pursuit of such greater autonomy <ital>may</ital> justify trusteeship.</para.text>
                </para>
              </list.item>
              <list.item date.updated="0">
                <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <designator date.updated="0">(6)</designator>
                </name.block>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">The parent union may have just cause to impose a form of trusteeship when the actions of the local union go beyond &ldquo;legitimate dissent&rdquo; and constitute a threat to the values of a union, or to its ultimate institutional integrity, or to the financial or institutional stability or survival of the local union, provided that none of these factors would unduly prevent the local union from effectively exercising its rights, duties and privileges under the Act. Much will depend on an assessment of the facts in each case.</para.text>
                </para>
              </list.item>
              <list.item date.updated="0">
                <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <designator date.updated="0">(7)</designator>
                </name.block>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">Process, which need not meet the standards of &ldquo;natural justice&rdquo; that a tribunal such as this Board must adhere to, will be of varying degrees of importance &hellip; On this issue, context will entirely define the degree to which process and the application of any standards of process are relevant to a finding of just cause or a lack thereof.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">
                            <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC" p2="" post.rptr="120" pre.rptr="71" ref.id="Id8c52c718cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC">Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC (2001), 71 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 120 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>, at para. 25.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> &lsqb;Emphasis in original.&rsqb;</para.text>
                </para>
              </list.item>
            </list>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">In a long-running case that reviewed the scope of s. 149, the Ontario Board affirmed in <ital>BAC, Local 10 and BAC (Re)</ital>
              <footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="BAC, Local 10 and BAC (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="230" pre.rptr="58" ref.id="Id8c553808cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="BAC, Local 10 and BAC (Re)">BAC, Local 10 and BAC (Re) (2000), 58 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 230 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> that a parent union will meet the just cause standard if it can demonstrate that its decision was a fair and reasonable one in all the circumstances. The Board ruled that the parent union was justified in placing one of its locals under receivership, after the local had resisted the parent&apos;s request to produce its financial books and records following an internal union complaint of fiscal disorder. Further, the Board found that the imposition of a &ldquo;gentle receivership&rdquo; was a measured response in the circumstances. In a preliminary decision involving the same parties, the Ontario Board had directed the parent, through an interim order, to cease and desist from interfering with the provincial council&apos;s bargaining agent, and to stop restricting the council from instructing its lawyers and preventing local funds from being used to pursue its various legal challenges.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers" p2="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers" post.rptr="285" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1998&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c553818cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers v. Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers">International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers v. Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers, &lsqb;1998&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. March/April 285, 42 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 1, upheld on judicial review 2000 C.L.L.C. &para; 220-018 (Ont. S.C.J. (Div. Ct.))</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> As well, in an intermediate ruling between these two parties, the Ontario Board also stated, under s. 149, that it has the authority to assess the conduct of the parent on a natural justice standard.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers" p2="International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers" post.rptr="2470" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1999&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c553828cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers">International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers, &lsqb;1999&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 2470</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> Subsequently, the Ontario Board found that the parent union had not sought a reasonable accommodation with the Ontario local on the contentious issue of Canadian autonomy, and its imposition of receivership failed to meet the &ldquo;just cause&rdquo; standard. Rather, the Board held that the parent union had acted as an &ldquo;absolute dictator&rdquo;, and lifted the receivership.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC" p2="" post.rptr="120" pre.rptr="71" ref.id="Id8c553838cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC">Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC (2001), 71 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 120 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="0">Section 149(2) does not cover situations where a regional body of the union has removed an officer from his union positions. The focus of this provision is on the autonomy of a construction union local; beyond that, it is not engaged in any other matter dealing with internal union conflict.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Mitchinson" p2="IBEW, First District, Canada" post.rptr="2684" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2009&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c553848cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Mitchinson v. IBEW, First District, Canada">Mitchinson v. IBEW, First District, Canada, &lsqb;2009&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 2684, reconsideration refused &lsqb;2009&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 3610</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> As well, the Ontario Board has declined to hear complaints under s. 149 involving challenges by defeated union officers to the integrity of the union election, where no meaningful labour relations purpose would be served by the litigation because of the presumed length of the proceedings and the mootness of the issues.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Terceira" p2="LIUNA, Local 183" post.rptr="30" pre.rptr="209" ref.id="Id8c57a908cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Terceira v. LIUNA, Local 183">Terceira v. LIUNA, Local 183 (2012), 209 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 30, 2012 CarswellOnt 3897 (Ont. L.R.B.), application for judicial review allowed 2013 ONSC 3344 (Ont. Div. Ct.), revd 2014 CarswellOnt 16542, 2014 ONCA 839</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="20260211">The Ontario Board has ruled it has the authority to issue interim relief when adjudicating a dispute between a local and its parent in appropriate circumstances.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Greater Ontario Regional Council of Carpenters, Drywalls and Allied Workers and CJA, Local 93 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="35" pre.rptr="123" ref.id="Id8c57a918cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Greater Ontario Regional Council of Carpenters, Drywalls and Allied Workers and CJA, Local 93 (Re)">Greater Ontario Regional Council of Carpenters, Drywalls and Allied Workers and CJA, Local 93 (Re) (2005), 123 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 35 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Universal Workers Union and Labourers&apos; International Union of North America Local 183 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="338" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2004&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c57a928cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Universal Workers Union and Labourers&apos; International Union of North America Local 183 (Re)">Universal Workers Union and Labourers&apos; International Union of North America Local 183 (Re), &lsqb;2004&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. March/April 338</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> In considering whether to provide interim relief, the Board is guided by the principle of preserving the integrity of the litigation by ensuring that one party would not be effectively rendered powerless by the very act that was the subject of the application. Three factors will be considered by the Board: (i) whether there is a serious <ital>prima facie</ital> issue; (ii) whether there would be irreparable harm to the applicant if interim relief was not granted; and (iii) where the balance of labour relations harm and the public interest lies. In <ital>Marble, Tile &amp; Terrazzo, Local 31 and BAC (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Marble, Tile &amp; Terrazzo, Local 31 and BAC (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="587" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2007&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c5a1a08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Marble, Tile &amp; Terrazzo, Local 31 and BAC (Re)">Marble, Tile &amp; Terrazzo, Local 31 and BAC (Re), &lsqb;2007&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May/June 587</ref>
                      </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="LIUNA" p2="Universal Workers Union (LIUNA, Local 183)" post.rptr="92" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2010&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c5a1a18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="LIUNA v. Universal Workers Union (LIUNA, Local 183)">LIUNA v. Universal Workers Union (LIUNA, Local 183), &lsqb;2010&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 92</ref>
                    </cite>, where the Ontario Labour Relations Board denied an application for interim relief under s. 149 on the grounds that the facts presented by the applicant local union were insufficient to establish that such an order was necessary to protect the Ontario Board&apos;s process.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> the Ontario Board granted interim relief to the parent union to continue its trusteeship because the local had purportedly failed to resist overtures from another union. However, in <ital>Universal Workers Union (LIUNA, Local 183) v. LIUNA</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Universal Workers Union (LIUNA, Local 183)" p2="LIUNA" post.rptr="89" pre.rptr="198" ref.id="Id8c5a1a28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Universal Workers Union (LIUNA, Local 183) v. LIUNA">Universal Workers Union (LIUNA, Local 183) v. LIUNA (2011), 198 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 89 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
            </footnote> the Ontario Board dismissed an application for interim relief in circumstances where the applicants had sought an interim order under s. 149 to suspend the results of a recent local executive board election. The Ontario Board ruled that it could only intervene to review the results of a local union election under s. 149, where the international parent had fraudulently interfered in order to produce a desired result.</para.text>
          </para>
        </section.body>
      </section>
      <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
        <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
          <metadata field="classification">
            <value>LAB.I.2.b.iii Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Internal affairs&mdash;Officers</value>
          </metadata>
        </metadata.block>
        <section.front date.updated="0">
          <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
            <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
            <designator date.updated="0">5:28</designator>
            <name date.updated="0">Other Provisions</name>
          </outline.name.block>
        </section.front>
        <section.body date.updated="0">
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="0">Finally, s. 150 provides for the appointment of trustees of employment benefit plans by local unions, allowing them the majority of union representation on the trustee administration board.</para.text>
          </para>
        </section.body>
      </section>
    </section.block>
  </analytical.level.body>
</analytical.level>
<analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
    <metadata field="classification">
      <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.A Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Merger or amalgamation</value>
    </metadata>
  </metadata.block>
  <front date.updated="0">
    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
      <designator date.updated="0">V</designator>
      <name date.updated="0">Union Successor Rights: Mergers, Amalgamations and Transfers of Jurisdiction</name>
    </outline.name.block>
  </front>
  <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.A Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Merger or amalgamation</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">A</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Introduction</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:29</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Generally</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <trace.deleted/>
                  <trace>W</trace>As Canadian corporations expand, contract or even disappear in the face of a constantly transforming economy, trade unions frequently respond by undergoing their own internal reorganizations to protect their bargaining strength and their membership&apos;s jobs. Often, these reorganizations have taken the form of one of several types of union mergers: locals within a union combining together, small unions merging with larger unions, or several unions joining together to form an entirely new union. As a method of rationalizing scarce resources, mergers have a lot to offer to trade unions. As several observers of the Canadian industrial relations scene have pointed out, mergers provide unions with an opportunity to increase or, at least, preserve their bargaining power through enhanced finances, a broader membership base, more internal research and operational resources, and the elimination of both costly jurisdictional battles and organizational duplication.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                            <ref ref.id="Icffa99e08cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" short="y">M.A. Coady, &ldquo;Trade Union Mergers and Their Significance in the Canadian Union Movement&rdquo; (unpublished Master&apos;s Thesis, Osgoode Hall Law School, 1976), reproduced in Labour Law: Cases, Materials and Commentary, 5th ed. (Kingston, Industrial Relations Centre, Queen&apos;s University, 1991), at p. 909</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                            <ref ref.id="Icffa99e18cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" short="y">G. Chaison and J. Rose, &ldquo;Unions: Growth, Structure and Internal Dynamics&rdquo; in Union-Management Relations in Canada, 2nd ed., J. Anderson, M. Gunderson and A. Ponak, eds. (Don Mills, Addison Wesley, 1989), at pp. 135&ndash;8</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In significant sectors of the Canadian economy, such as transportation, retail services, primary resource industries and manufacturing, recent decades have witnessed an increasing number of union mergers, as the attractions of small unions and single-industry representation have given way to the acceptance that larger, multi-industry unions often provide a more effective structure to defend jobs and workplace rights.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                            <ref ref.id="Icffa99e28cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" short="y">J. D. Arrowsmith, Canada&apos;s Trade Unions: An Information Manual, (Kingston, Industrial Relations Centre, Queen&apos;s University, 1992), at p. 111</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="20211028">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In its evolving attitude towards trade union restructuring, Canadian labour law has determined that the courts and labour relations boards have divided jurisdiction over the issue.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bennett" p2="UFCW, Local 401" post.rptr="190" pre.rptr="107" ref.id="Id8c5c8b08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401">Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401 (2004), 107 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 190 sub nom. Gateway Casinos Canada Inc. and UFCW, Local 401 (Re) (Alta.), application for judicial review dismissed 23 Admin. L.R. (4th) 244, 108 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 141 (Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> On the one hand, questions of whether one union is the lawful successor of another union&apos;s property and assets upon the stated completion of a merger or amalgamation is not a concern of labour relations boards, flowing from their restricted jurisdiction under the governing statutes. That is a question for the courts, and they would regard a dispute over the ownership of office furniture or bank accounts as an application of the common law to the claim of one unincorporated association to be the successor to the rights and obligations of another.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See generally, <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Leduc" p2="International Brotherhood Electrical Workers" post.rptr="19179" pre.rptr="2019" ref.id="Id8c5efc08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers">Leduc v. International Brotherhood Electrical Workers, 2019 CarswellOnt 19179, 2019 ONSC 6507</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Toffiq" p2="Canadian Workers Union, Local 5" post.rptr="220-051" pre.rptr="2000" ref.id="Id8c5efc18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Toffiq v. Canadian Workers Union, Local 5">Toffiq v. Canadian Workers Union, Local 5, 2000 C.L.L.C. 220-051, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; B.C.J. No. 139 (S.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW and Unitel Communications Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="59" pre.rptr="86" ref.id="Id8c5efc28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW and Unitel Communications Inc. (Re)">IBEW and Unitel Communications Inc. (Re) (1991), 86 di 59</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1252" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1985&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c5efc38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re)">L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1985&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1252</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McMillin" p2="Yandell" post.rptr="398" pre.rptr="22" ref.id="Id8c5efc48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McMillin v. Yandell">McMillin v. Yandell (1971), 22 D.L.R. (3d) 398, &lsqb;1972&rsqb; 1 O.R. 146 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                        </cite>, where the Ontario High Court of Justice drew a distinction between the rights conferred by the statutory merger provisions and those merger provisions in a union&apos;s constitution.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, the courts have stated that, where a merger dispute is already before a labour relations board, they will generally defer to the labour board proceedings unless the applicant can establish that irreparable harm would result.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Krogstad" p2="Aspinall" post.rptr="155" pre.rptr="64" ref.id="Id8c5efc58cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Krogstad v. Aspinall">Krogstad v. Aspinall (2000), 64 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 155 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">On the other hand, however, Canadian labour legislation is keenly interested in the allocation of statutory bargaining rights<trace> and the powers and duties incident to them</trace>, and labour relations boards are empowered to conduct their own review of a trade union merger<trace>,</trace>
                  <trace.deleted/> amalgamation <trace>or transfer </trace>On the other hand, however, Canadian labour legislation is keenly interested in the allocation of statutory bargaining rights, and labour relations boards are empowered to conduct their own review of a trade union merger or amalgamation before granting bargaining rights to a union claiming successor status. Labour boards have taken the view that, while union mergers may take place independent of any board authority,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">This point is reinforced by the fact that labour board declarations of a successorship have a retrospective effect. In <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Construction Labour Relations &mdash; an Alberta Association and HFIA, Local 110" p2="" post.rptr="337" pre.rptr="14" ref.id="Id8c616d08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Construction Labour Relations &mdash; an Alberta Association and HFIA, Local 110">Construction Labour Relations &mdash; an Alberta Association and HFIA, Local 110 (1986), 14 C.L.R.B.R. (N.S.) 337 (Alta.)</ref>
                          </cite>, the Alberta Labour Relations Board found that s. 135 &lsqb;now 47&rsqb; of the Alberta Act contemplated that a union merger was effective for the purposes of the labour legislation as soon as it occurred and without the need for Board blessing to be consummated. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UA, Local 488" p2="Gateway Refrigeration Ltd." post.rptr="&para;14,035" pre.rptr="85" ref.id="Id8c616d18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UA, Local 488 v. Gateway Refrigeration Ltd.">UA, Local 488 v. Gateway Refrigeration Ltd. (1985), 85 C.L.L.C. &para;14,035 (Alta. Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> statutory bargaining rights are not a species of property that can be conveyed from one union to another.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">In <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1252" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1985&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c616d28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re)">L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1985&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1252</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 1266, the Ontario Board stated:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">The members of a trade union may take effective proceedings to transfer collective contractual and property obligations <ital>inter se</ital> to a larger group. They cannot, in their capacity as members of the trade union, effectively convey to another entity the rights and obligations of that union as bargaining agent of a unit or units of employees, any more than a trustee or agent can transfer his trust or agency to another without the agreement of his <ital>cestuis que trust</ital> or principals.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="GreenField Ethanol Inc." p2="CEP, Local 2003" post.rptr="687" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2009&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c616d38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="GreenField Ethanol Inc. v. CEP, Local 2003">GreenField Ethanol Inc. v. CEP, Local 2003, &lsqb;2009&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 687</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bennett" p2="UFCW, Local 401" post.rptr="190" pre.rptr="107" ref.id="Id8c616d48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401">Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401 (2004), 107 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 190 sub nom. Gateway Casinos Canada Inc. and UFCW, Local 401 (Re) (Alta.), application for judicial review dismissed 23 Admin. L.R. (4th) 244, 108 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 141 (Q.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Hyde Park Masonry Ltd. and Brick and Allied Craft Union, Local 5 (Re)" p2="Hyde Park Masonry Ltd. and Brick and Allied Craft Union, Local 5 (Re)" post.rptr="296" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2004&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c616d58cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Hyde Park Masonry Ltd. and Brick and Allied Craft Union, Local 5 (Re)">Hyde Park Masonry Ltd. and Brick and Allied Craft Union, Local 5 (Re), &lsqb;2004&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. March/April 296</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Winston Park Retirement Home" p2="Winston Park Retirement Home" post.rptr="137" pre.rptr="76" ref.id="Id8c63de08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Winston Park Retirement Home">Winston Park Retirement Home (2001), 76 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 137 sub nom. CAW-Canada and SEIU, Local 220 (Re), &lsqb;2001&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1479</ref>
                          </cite>; and <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="BAC, Local 6" p2="BACU" post.rptr="161" pre.rptr="64" ref.id="Id8c63de18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="BAC, Local 6 v. BACU">BAC, Local 6 v. BACU (2000), 64 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept./Oct. 891</ref>
                        </cite>, where the Ontario Board said at para. 138: &ldquo;Bargaining rights &hellip; are not a species of private property. They may not, as of right, be sold, leased, transferred or disposed of. They are creatures of the statute.&rdquo;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Rather, that designatory power rests solely with a labour board, and it will only rule that a union should be declared the successor to the statutory and collective agreement rights of a predecessor union if it is satisfied that the merger, amalgamation or transfer has occurred within the constitutional authority of the unions involved. As the British Columbia Labour Relations Board stated in <ital>United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local No. 1998</ital>:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">The Board&apos;s role is to oversee institutional collective bargaining relationships of the union in its role as statutory bargaining agent, not to police the application of the rules in the constitutions and bylaws of unions. Whether an alleged successor is properly the successor to property rights is not of direct concern to the Board; the interest of the Board is in the designation of the exclusive bargaining agent and the declaration of a transfer of any bargaining rights arising as a consequence of succession accomplished by parties themselves.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local No. 1998" p2="" post.rptr="77" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c63de28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local No. 1998">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local No. 1998, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 77</ref>
                            </cite>, at para. 77.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Thus, while statutory bargaining rights are not a form of property, they are often the most important right that a predecessor union may possess. This may explain why most legal disputes over the validity or impact of a union merger occur before labour boards rather than in the courts. The legal consequence of a statutory successorship declaration that is so often fought over in front of labour boards is simple but important. The successor union steps into the bargaining shoes of its predecessor.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">For example, a successor union can assume jurisdiction over the uncompleted disciplinary proceedings of a predecessor: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McVitae" p2="Unison" post.rptr="33" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1996&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c664f08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McVitae v. Unison">McVitae v. Unison, &lsqb;1996&rsqb; I.R.L.R. 33 (Ch. D.)</ref>
                          </cite>. As well, a successor union can be the proper institutional body for assuming liability for damages caused to third parties or to an employer by a predecessor union that had merged with it: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Montreal (Ville)" p2="SCFP, section locale 3489" post.rptr="97" pre.rptr="142" ref.id="Id8c664f18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Montreal (Ville) v. SCFP, section locale 3489">Montreal (Ville) v. SCFP, section locale 3489 (2005), 142 L.A.C. (4th) 97 (Que. C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>. However, the successor union does not automatically assume liability, particularly in circumstances where its actions were primarily limited to providing financial and bargaining support to its predecessor&apos;s local: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Fullowka" p2="Royal Oak Ventures Inc." post.rptr="577" pre.rptr="315" ref.id="Id8c664f28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Fullowka v. Royal Oak Ventures Inc.">Fullowka v. Royal Oak Ventures Inc. (2010), 315 D.L.R. (4th) 577 (S.C.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> As one board ruling has put it, a statutory declaration &ldquo;has no greater effect than to substitute one union for another in a bargaining relationship. The bargaining rights and privileges possessed by the successor union are no greater than, or different than, the rights and privileges formerly possessed by the predecessor union.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Deseronto Public Utilities Commission" p2="" post.rptr="248" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1977&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c664f38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Deseronto Public Utilities Commission">Deseronto Public Utilities Commission, &lsqb;1977&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 248</ref>
                          </cite>, at p. 249. See also <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173" p2="" post.rptr="244" pre.rptr="27" ref.id="Id8c664f48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173">Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173 (1995), 27 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 244 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                  <trace.deleted/>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.A Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Merger or amalgamation</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">B</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Statutory Provisions</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.A Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Merger or amalgamation</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:30</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Overview</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The fundamental principle underlying the statutory regulation of union successorships is that labour boards have exclusive jurisdiction over the designation of certified bargaining agents and the allocation of the corresponding duties. As a matter of the law governing unincorporated associations, one union may be the successor of another. As matter of the law governing collective bargaining, however, the successor union is not treated as the bargaining agent until the labour board has said so. In the initial period after the enactment of modern labour legislation in Canada, in the absence of any provisions governing successorships, labour boards applied these principles to hold that if a trade union ceased to exist per se, its bargaining and collective agreement rights were extinguished, not transferred. If a union changed its organizational identity through a merger or transfer of jurisdiction, it was deemed to have lost its statutory rights.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Put in another way, on &lsqb;November 29, 1955, the effective date of the reorganization&rsqb;, Local 889 of the &ldquo;International&rdquo; lost status as a local of the &ldquo;International&rdquo; and it became a local of the new Canadian organization. Since, in the result, Local 889 became a new juridical entity, and since no rights of succession are provided for under the legislation as it stands at this time, Local 889 cannot assert a claim under <ital>The Labour Relations Act</ital> to be the bargaining agent of the employees ...</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In 1956, Ontario became the first Canadian jurisdiction to amend its labour legislation to provide for the potential flow-through of statutory bargaining rights upon a trade union successorship, and the other Canadian common law jurisdictions subsequently followed suit. As a result of the Ontario amendment, the merger of the two principal trade union federations in Canada, the Trades and Labour Congress and the Canadian Congress of Labour, became possible, and later in 1956 they united to form the Canadian Labour Congress.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ontario Hydro Employees Union" p2="Hydro-Electric Power Commission of Ontario" pre.rptr="57" post.rptr="18,080" title="Ontario Hydro Employees Union and Hydro-Electric Power Commission of Ontario (Re)" date="1957">Ontario Hydro Employees Union and Hydro-Electric Power Commission of Ontario (Re) (1957), 57 C.L.L.C. &p;18,080 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 1655.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> While legislation now provides for bargaining rights to be transferred, the basic principle remains, so that a successful successorship declaration at the relevant labour is a condition precedent of a union exercising the rights, or being subject to the duties, of a certified bargaining. However, where a union claimed to be a successor and there was consent from the complainant, employer, and formally certified union, the Canada Industrial Relations Board was willing to add the putative successor as a respondent on a duty of fair representation complaint without having adjudicated the underlying successorship claim.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">32</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Penner" p2="ILWU Canada, Local 400" pre.rptr="2025" post.rptr="1206" title="Penner and ILWU Canada, Local 400, Re">Penner and ILWU Canada, Local 400, Re, 2025 CIRB 1206, 2025 CarswellNat 5448 (C.I.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The 1956 Ontario amendment providing for trade union successorship has survived unaltered to the present day,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, S.O. 1995, c. 1, Sch. A, s. 68.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and has formed the basis for the statutory provisions on union successorships in the other Canadian common law jurisdictions.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20221125">
                        <ital>Canada Labour Code</ital>, R.S.C. 1985, c. L-2, s. 43; <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, R.S.A. 2000, c. L-1, s. 49; <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, R.S.B.C. 1996, c. 244, s. 37; <ital>The Labour Relations Act</ital>, R.S.M. 1987, c. L10 (C.C.S.M., c. L10), s. 55; <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital>, R.S.N.L. 1990, c. L-1, s. 94; <ital>Industrial Relations Act</ital>, R.S.N.B. 1973, c. I-4, s. 58; <ital>Trade Union Act</ital>, R.S.N.S. 1989, c. 475, s. 32; <ital>Labour Act</ital>, R.S.P.E.I. 1988, c. L-1, s. 40; <ital>The Saskatchewan Employment Act,</ital> S.S. 2013, c.S-15.1, s. 6-21.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Under s. 68 of the Ontario <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, a union claiming that it is, as the result of a merger, amalgamation, or transfer of jurisdiction, the successor to another union&apos;s statutory bargaining rights may seek a declaration to that effect from the Labour Relations Board. The union seeking the declaration may apply at any time, or may raise the issue during any other proceeding it may be involved in before the Board. When determining whether to apply its discretion, the Board has the authority to hold an inquiry and gather any relevant evidence. If deemed appropriate, the Board may also require a representation vote among the affected union membership. Finally, the effect of a Board declaration under s. 68 is twofold: it clothes the successor union with all of the statutory bargaining rights and duties of its predecessor, and it requires the employers of the affected union membership to recognize the change in bargaining agent status in all respects.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The statutory provisions in the other Canadian common law jurisdictions offer some slight variances from the Ontario legislation. The union successorship section in the New Brunswick Act<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">N.B., <ital>Industrial Relations Act</ital>, s. 58.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> is virtually identical to the Ontario provision. The statutory provisions in Alberta, British Columbia, Newfoundland, Nova Scotia and Prince Edward Island<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Alta., <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, s. 49; B.C., <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, s. 37; Nfld. &amp; Lab., <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital>, s. 94; N.S., <ital>Trade Union Act</ital>, s. 32; P.E.I., <ital>Labour Act</ital>, s. 40.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> differ from the Ontario legislation in only one respect: they do not explicitly mention that the employers of the union membership affected by the reorganization are obligated to recognize the declared change in union status. However, it is unlikely that this absence has any substantive consequences, as the provisions in each of these provinces state that, upon the board&apos;s declaration, the successor union acquires the rights, privileges and duties of the predecessor union. An employer in Canada could not, without severe consequences, ignore the statutorily acquired bargaining status of the new union representing its employees.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Man., <ital>The Labour Relations Act</ital>, s. 55.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> provides for a similar successorship process as Ontario, with two inconsequential differences. First, s. 55 of <ital>The Labour Relations Act</ital> does not include a specific provision granting the Board the power to hold an inquiry or to order a representation vote in a successorship matter, unlike its counterpart in many jurisdictions. However, it would appear that the general procedural powers in the Manitoba Act give the Labour Board the ability to conduct a successorship application with authority similar to other boards.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Man., <ital>The Labour Relations Act</ital>, s. 142.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Second, the rights and obligations of a successor union are more explicitly provided for in Manitoba than in other statutes, but it would appear that, in substance, this grants Manitoba unions no broader or different range of rights and obligations than unions acquire elsewhere in Canada.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20241205">The federal jurisdiction<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Canada Labour Code</ital>, s. 43.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> also provides for some of the same provisions as Ontario, with four variances. First, similar to a number of the provinces mentioned above, s. 43 of the <ital>Canada Labour Code</ital> does not explicitly require an employer to recognize the Canada Labour Relations Board&apos;s declaration of the bargaining authority of the successor union. But, for the same reason already given, the absence of the explicit requirement would probably not lessen the employer&apos;s obligation to accept the new union as the statutorily sanctioned bargaining agent for its employees.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20241205">However, the remaining variances are more consequential, and appear to restrict the authority of the Canada Board in ways that other labour boards in common law jurisdictions are not. The second variance is found in s. 43(1) of the Code, which states that where a union succeeds another by way of a merger, amalgamation or transfer, it shall be deemed to have acquired the rights and obligations of its predecessor. The acquisition of the successorship rights flows directly from the act of union reorganization, and is not dependent upon the Board&apos;s assent. Unlike other labour boards, the Canada Board has not been given primary jurisdiction to make a declaration of a merger, and it has interpreted its authority in union successorship matters as being restricted to determining what these rights and obligations may consist of.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20241205">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW and Unitel Communications Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="59" pre.rptr="86" ref.id="Id8c6da208cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW and Unitel Communications Inc. (Re)">IBEW and Unitel Communications Inc. (Re) (1991), 86 di 59 (C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 64. The CIRB stated: &ldquo;It seems to us that Parliament intentionally left the matter of mergers, amalgamations and transfers of jurisdiction among trade unions as private contract considerations between the parties involved. These are internal union matters in which the Board cannot and, in our respectful opinion, ought not to interfere. We agree with counsel for CACAW that section 43 as it is presently worded deems that successor rights have been acquired and that the Board&apos;s jurisdiction has been restricted to questions about what these rights consist of &hellip;&rdquo;.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The third variance reinforces the Board&apos;s limited role in union successorships. Section 43(2) of the Code states that the Canada Board has the power to specifically determine what rights and obligations have been acquired by the successor union. By implying that there may be some rights or obligations of the predecessor union that are not passed on, this part of the Code provision is quite arguably more restrictive than its counterparts in other Canadian jurisdictions, which provide that the successor union acquires the rights and obligations, without limitation or circumscription, of its predecessor.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.trace="20241205" date.updated="20241205">
                <para.text date.updated="0">And the fourth variance goes to the issue of membership approval of a union successorship determination. In circumstances where a parent union had determined that one local was the successor union to another local, the Canada Industrial Relations Board ruled that the <ital>Code</ital> was silent on the requirement for membership support for the successorship, and it could not add, by its own initiative, such a requirement. All that is required in the federal sector is for the union to have followed its own internal constitutional process.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8.50</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Teamsters, Local 938" p2="Teamsters, Local 847" post.rptr="601" pre.rptr="2011" ref.id="I630fbc00a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="Teamsters, Local 938 v. Teamsters, Local 847">Teamsters, Local 938 v. Teamsters, Local 847, 2011 CIRB 601 (C.I.R.B.), overturning Purolator Courier Ltd., 2011 CIRB LD 2511</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CAW-Canada, Local 2006" p2="Bridge Terminal Transport Canada Inc." post.rptr="347" pre.rptr="2006" ref.id="I630fbc01a7a611ef88f3910b7099d345" title="CAW-Canada, Local 2006 v. Bridge Terminal Transport Canada Inc.">CAW-Canada, Local 2006 v. Bridge Terminal Transport Canada Inc., 2006 CIRB 347 (C.I.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan provides a somewhat unique drafting approach to the determination of union rights following a merger. <ital>The Saskatchewan Employment Act</ital> does not explicitly provide a procedure for the determination of a union successorship.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.trace="20221125" date.updated="20221125">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">S.S. 2013, c. S-15.1, s. 6-21.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Instead, the section defines the merger process in negative wording, so as to provide that no board order, collective agreement or board proceeding shall be adversely affected by a union merger, amalgamation, transfer or affiliation. Otherwise, it would appear that the Saskatchewan provisions are substantively similar to those in the other jurisdictions.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Quebec <ital>Labour Code</ital> does not contain a specific union successorship provision. However, it is arguable that ss. 32 and 41 of the Code, applied together, could achieve the same result. As an alternative, s. 10 and ss. 19, 20 and 21 of the <ital>Professional Syndicates Act</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20211028">C.Q.L.R., c. S-40.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> may be available to provide a successorship.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                            <ref ref.id="Icffb0f108cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" short="y">P. Verge and G. Murray, Le Droit et les Syndicats: Aspects du Droit Syndical Qu&eacute;b&eacute;cois (Sainte-Foy, Presses de l&apos;Universit&eacute; Laval, 1991), at p. 172</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                            <ref ref.id="Icffb0f118cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" short="y">R.P. Gagnon, L. leBel and P. Verge, Droit du Travail, 2nd ed. (Sainte-Foy, Presses de l&apos;Universit&eacute; Laval, 1991), at pp. 329&ndash;30</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A word of warning. In British Columbia, the Labour Board has extensive powers to form, regulate and manage councils of trade unions in the construction sector.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">12 <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, RSBC 1996, c 244, s. 41.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The regime has been used to review and in some cases direct mergers and transfers of jurisdiction among unions.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">13 <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, Local No.170" post.rptr="134" pre.rptr="2025" title="United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, Local No.170">United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, Local No.170, 2025 BCLRB 134, 2025 Carswell 2181 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="B.C. Provincial Council of Carpenters" p2="Construction, Maintenance and Allied Workers Bargaining Council" post.rptr="43582" pre.rptr="2009" title="B.C. Provincial Council of Carpenters v. Construction, Maintenance and Allied Workers Bargaining Council">B.C. Provincial Council of Carpenters v. Construction, Maintenance and Allied Workers Bargaining Council, 2009 CanLII 43582 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>
                        </para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In its decisions under the regime, the board has applied a different set of standards, and exercised powers significantly exceeding those available in other successorship contexts.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">14 See e.g. <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="B.C. Provincial Council of Carpenters" p2="Construction, Maintenance and Allied Workers Bargaining Council" pre.rptr="2009" post.rptr="43582" title="B.C. Provincial Council of Carpenters v. Construction, Maintenance and Allied Workers Bargaining Council">B.C. Provincial Council of Carpenters v. Construction, Maintenance and Allied Workers Bargaining Council, 2009 CanLII 43582 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, in which the board inter alia made various holdings regarding the disposition of property among various successor union locals.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The ins and outs of that regime are not considered or evaluated here in any detail. Readers should be cautious about relying on decisions made under the regime as precedent for board regulation of successorship more broadly, or vice versa.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">15 For further guidance, see Stephen A. McArthur et al, <ital>Canadian Construction Labour and Employment Law </ital>(LexiNexis Canada, looseleaf)</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.A Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Merger or amalgamation</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:31</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Definitions: Merger, Amalgamation, Transfer of Jurisdiction</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">All of the Canadian common law jurisdictions except for Saskatchewan<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Sections 6-21(1)(c) of <ital>The Saskatchewan Employment Act</ital> uses the terms &ldquo;amalgamation, merger, or <ital>affiliation of a trade union</ital> or any part thereof with another trade union&rdquo;. This, however, appears to be a distinction without a difference.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> employ the terms &ldquo;merger&rdquo;, &ldquo;amalgamation&rdquo; and &ldquo;transfer of jurisdiction&rdquo; in their legislative provisions on union successorship. A frequently cited definition of the terms is found in <ital>Ontario Hydro Employees Union and Hydro-Electric Power Commission of Ontario (Re)</ital>, where the Ontario Board stated:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">&ldquo;Merger&rdquo; is defined in <ital>Bouvier&apos;s Law Dictionary</ital> as &ldquo;the absorption of a thing of lesser importance by a greater, whereby the lesser ceases to exist but the greater is not increased&rdquo;; &ldquo;amalgamation&rdquo; is there defined as the &ldquo;union of different &hellip; societies, so as to form a homogeneous whole or new body&rdquo;. In trade-union circles, the terms merger and amalgamation are frequently used interchangeably. A transfer of jurisdiction takes place when one parent body assigns control over one of its subordinate branches or lodges to another parent body.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="Ontario Hydro Employees Union and Hydro-Electric Power Commission of Ontario (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;18,080" pre.rptr="57" ref.id="Id8c701308cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ontario Hydro Employees Union and Hydro-Electric Power Commission of Ontario (Re)">Ontario Hydro Employees Union and Hydro-Electric Power Commission of Ontario (Re) (1957), 57 C.L.L.C. &para;18,080 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                            </cite>, at p. 1654.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">While labour boards in subsequent years have commonly referred to this definition, they have not held themselves strictly bound by any distinctions among the three terms. The Ontario Board in <ital>Ontario Hydro Employees Union</ital> itself noted that trade unions used the terms &ldquo;merger&rdquo; and &ldquo;amalgamation&rdquo; interchangeably.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE and Hamilton-Wentworth Roman Catholic Separate School Board Professional Staff Assn. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="49" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1990&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c701318cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE and Hamilton-Wentworth Roman Catholic Separate School Board Professional Staff Assn. (Re)">CUPE and Hamilton-Wentworth Roman Catholic Separate School Board Professional Staff Assn. (Re), &lsqb;1990&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Jan. 49</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In 1984, the Ontario Board stated that &ldquo;we do not think that anything turns on the fact that the transaction with the UFCW was framed as a merger rather than a transfer of bargaining jurisdiction&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="542" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1984&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c701328cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)">UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1984&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 542</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 557.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Similarly, in 1987, the same Board held that the purpose of the successorship section is to give effect to a transfer of bargaining rights, and suggested that it will not draw narrow definitions around these terms so as to defeat the purpose of the provision.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." p2="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." post.rptr="1413" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c701338cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc.">M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc., &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1413</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,261" pre.rptr="63" ref.id="Id8c728408cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)">IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re) (1962), 63 C.L.L.C. &para;16,261 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, where the Ontario Board avoided any reference to the definition in <ital>Ontario Hydro Employees Union and Hydro-Electric Power Commission of Ontario (Re)</ital> when declaring a union to be the successor to another union&apos;s bargaining rights, in circumstances where the transfer of jurisdiction did not conform to the restricted definition adopted in 1957.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> And in <ital>Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173" p2="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173" post.rptr="954" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1995&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c728418cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173">Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173, &lsqb;1995&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July 954 <point>at p. 966,</point> 27 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 244</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Ontario Board ruled that &ldquo;jurisdiction&rdquo; has a wide meaning that encompasses the variety of ways in which a trade union chooses to define or subdivide its organizational territory, including geographical, industrial and skill delineations.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Forward Transport Ltd. and CLAC, Local 67 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-016" pre.rptr="2006" ref.id="Id8c728428cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Forward Transport Ltd. and CLAC, Local 67 (Re)">Forward Transport Ltd. and CLAC, Local 67 (Re), 2006 C.L.L.C. &para;220-016 (B.C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at para. 19, where the British Columbia Labour Relations Board stated that the term &ldquo;transfer of jurisdiction&rdquo; has acquired &ldquo;a broad and liberal interpretation, embracing the wide variety of circumstances in which one union may seek to stand in place of another&rdquo;.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Alberta Labour Relations Board has suggested that, while strict definitions should not be placed on any of the terms, a merger involves the absorption of one union by another, an amalgamation involves two or more unions combining together to form an entirely new union, and a transfer of jurisdiction involves either a transfer of a local from one parent union to another, or a change in a local&apos;s geographic boundaries.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para; 16,038" pre.rptr="88" ref.id="Id8c728438cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)">IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re) (1988), 88 C.L.L.C. &para; 16,038 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 14,276.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">C</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Development of the Law on Union Successorship</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:32</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Generally</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Although the language of the present s. 68 of the Ontario Act has remained unchanged since its inception in 1956, the approach by the Board and the courts to this provision, and to the common law on union mergers, has undergone several significant shifts over the years. The shifts in the Ontario law are particularly important, since most of the developments in the Canadian law on union successorship have originated in this province. One probable explanation for the focusing of these legal developments in Ontario is that many unions in Canada have their headquarters in the province, and the union successorship provision in the Ontario Act has governed many of the significant and contentious mergers in the Canadian labour movement.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In the initial years after the 1956 amendment to the Act, the practice of the Ontario Board was to grant a successorship declaration only after several prerequisites had been established.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,261" pre.rptr="63" ref.id="Id8c74f508cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)">IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re) (1962), 63 C.L.L.C. &para;16,261 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, where the Board, on p. 1119, stated that:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">The practice of the Board has been to inquire whether the predecessor trade union has exhibited the desire and has taken the necessary steps to effect the merger, amalgamation or transfer of jurisdiction. The Board must also be satisfied that a majority of the employees in the bargaining unit concerned, have given their approval to the action of the predecessor trade union. The successor trade union must also have exhibited a desire and taken the necessary steps to merge or amalgamate with or accept the transfer of jurisdiction over the bargaining unit.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The most important of these prerequisites required that a majority of the union members in the affected bargaining unit or union had given their approval to the reorganization. The Board defined &ldquo;majority&rdquo; in a liberal manner and, in one early decision, it issued a successorship declaration in circumstances where thirty members of a 100-member bargaining unit attended a duly called meeting and, by a majority vote of the thirty, approved a merger with another union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace>
                            <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,038" pre.rptr="88" ref.id="Id8c74f518cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)">IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re) (1988), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; Alta. L.R.B.R. 182, 88 C.L.L.C. &para;16,038 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                            </cite>
                        </trace>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">This liberal approach to union successorships changed dramatically in the aftermath of the 1969 Ontario Court of Appeal decision in <ital>Astgen v. Smith.</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Astgen" p2="Smith." post.rptr="657" pre.rptr="7" ref.id="Id8c74f528cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Astgen v. Smith.">Astgen v. Smith. (1969), 7 D.L.R. (3d) 657, &lsqb;1970&rsqb; 1 O.R. 129 (C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> The decision involved two unions that had reached a merger agreement where one of the unions &mdash; the Mine, Mill and Smelter Workers &mdash; would extinguish its own separate identity. The Mine, Mill and Smelter Worker&apos;s constitution did not contain any merger provisions. The union membership approved the merger agreement by a 2:1 majority vote. Dissidents within the union challenged the validity of the merger process, and both the Ontario High Court and the Court of Appeal upheld the challenge and nullified the merger.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20260211">The majority decision of the Court of Appeal in <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital> marked a step backwards in the ongoing attempts by Canadian unions to acquire a modern legal status that recognized the realities of contemporary labour relations. The majority ruling applied the antiquated &ldquo;gentlemen&apos;s club&rdquo; analogy to trade unions, and held that members were bound to each other by a complex of individual contracts represented by the constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See generally <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bimson" p2="Johnston" post.rptr="11" pre.rptr="10" ref.id="Id8c74f538cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bimson v. Johnston">Bimson v. Johnston (1957), 10 D.L.R. (2d) 11, &lsqb;1957&rsqb; O.R. 519 (H.C.J.), affd 12 D.L.R. (2d) 379, &lsqb;1958&rsqb; O.W.N. 217 (C.A.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Orchard" p2="Tunney" post.rptr="273" pre.rptr="8" ref.id="Id8c776608cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Orchard v. Tunney">Orchard v. Tunney (1957), 8 D.L.R. (2d) 273, &lsqb;1957&rsqb; S.C.R. 436</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The court reasoned that since a merger involves a complete extinction of these contractual rights, and since a voluntary organization has no inherent power to dissolve its existence by mergers with another association, then the power to merge must be clearly found in the constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Astgen" p2="Smith." post.rptr="657" pre.rptr="7" ref.id="Id8c776618cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Astgen v. Smith.">Astgen v. Smith. (1969), 7 D.L.R. (3d) 657, &lsqb;1970&rsqb; 1 O.R. 129 (C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>. The Court of Appeal majority upheld the legal fiction that a new union member somehow makes a multitude of contracts with all other members immediately upon admission, rather than recognizing the more legally realistic approach that a member agrees upon joining a union to subordinate herself or himself to the prevailing constitution and by-laws. But for the majority to have adopted this latter approach would have meant accepting that a union at common law has an identity that is distinct from its members. On pp. 662&ndash;3, Evans J.A., for the majority, wrote:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; &lsqb;Mine Mill&rsqb; is an unincorporated group or association of workmen who have banded together to promote certain objectives for their mutual benefit and advantage and in law nothing is recognizable other than the totality of members related one to another by contract &hellip; Each member of Mine Mill, upon being granted membership, subscribed to &lsqb;the&rsqb; purposes and objects &lsqb;of the union&apos;s constitution&rsqb; and in so doing entered into a contractual relationship with every other member of Mine Mill &hellip;</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; The contract of association is not between the member and some undefined entity which lacks the capacity to contract; it is a complex of contracts between each member and every other member of the union. These are individual contracts impressed with rights and obligations which cannot be destroyed in the absence of the specific consent of each person whose rights would be affected thereby.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> If the constitution was silent on this power, then the membership must give unanimous consent to such a fundamental change to its objects. Since the referendum among the Mine, Mill membership did not produce a unanimous vote in favour of the proposal, the Court of Appeal majority ruled that the merger was void.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Laskin J., in a long and subsequently influential dissent, expressed concerns with the application of the &ldquo;gentlemen&apos;s club&rdquo; model to a trade union setting. After noting the inadequate state of the common law in grasping the unique features of modern trade unions, Laskin J. went on to point out that a union merger, while affecting a union&apos;s identity and affiliation, would not alter its fundamental objects. These objects &mdash; the improvement of the condition of workers &mdash; would remain essentially the same from union to union. This, Laskin J. held, marked a significant difference between unions on the one hand and the variety of religious and social organizations on the other that the common law, including the majority&apos;s decision, lumped in all together as voluntary unincorporated associations. His dissenting opinion concluded that, as the fundamental objects of Mine, Mill were not being altered by its joining the Steelworkers, the absence of a specific merger provision in the Mine, Mill constitution and the subsequent failure to achieve unanimity among the membership should not be fatal to the merger agreement.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In Ontario, the Labour Relations Board relied heavily upon the majority opinion in <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital> for some time afterwards. The shadow of the Court of Appeal&apos;s decision meant unions that were seeking a successorship declaration before the Ontario Board, but which lacked the required merger provisions in their constitutions, would have to establish that unanimous approval of the change had been given by the membership, or face a dismissal of their application.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Per-fec-tion Insulations Limited" p2="Per-fec-tion Insulations Limited" post.rptr="84" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1980&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c79d708cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Per-fec-tion Insulations Limited">Per-fec-tion Insulations Limited, &lsqb;1980&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 84</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Dufferin-Peel Roman Catholic Separate School Board" p2="" post.rptr="821" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1976&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c79d718cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Dufferin-Peel Roman Catholic Separate School Board">Dufferin-Peel Roman Catholic Separate School Board, &lsqb;1976&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 821</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Redi Steel Products Inc." p2="" post.rptr="857" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1970&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c79d728cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Redi Steel Products Inc.">Redi Steel Products Inc., &lsqb;1970&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 857</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McMillin" p2="Yandell" post.rptr="398" pre.rptr="22" ref.id="Id8c79d738cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McMillin v. Yandell">McMillin v. Yandell (1971), 22 D.L.R. (3d) 398, &lsqb;1972&rsqb; 1 O.R. 146 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> The practical impact of <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital> meant that small, independent unions wishing to merge with larger unions to enhance their bargaining power, sometimes found that their unsophisticated constitutions had become an unintended barrier to change.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">But, by the early 1980s, the Ontario Board was emerging from under the shadow of <ital>Astgen v. Smith.</ital> Its decisions on union successorship were now drawing a distinction between the considerations required for a declaration under the <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital> and those employed by the courts when determining whether a union merger had been consummated at common law.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1252" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1985&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c79d748cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re)">L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1985&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1252</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Trans Nations Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1298" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1981&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c79d758cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Trans Nations Inc. (Re)">Trans Nations Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1981&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1298</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="542" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1984&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c79d768cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)">UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1984&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 542</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> a decision where the predecessor union&apos;s constitution contained no merger provisions and there had not been unanimity, the Board nevertheless issued a successor declaration. It, in effect, did away with the curse of unanimous approval, noting the absence of an express unanimity requirement in the union&apos;s constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="542" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1984&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c7c4808cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)">UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1984&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 542</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 559.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Board contrasted the purpose and powers of a trade union with those of &ldquo;voluntary associations&rdquo;, finding that trade unions are distinct.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="542" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1984&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c7c4818cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)">UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1984&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 542</ref>
                          </cite>, at p. 556. The argument used by the Board in <ital>UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)</ital> echoes many of the themes raised almost two decades before by Laskin J. in his dissenting opinion in <ital>Astgen v. Smith.</ital>
                        </para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> This justified an approach which placed less emphasis then the common law did on the union constitution when resolving internal union disputes.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In subsequent decisions, the Ontario Board further distanced itself from the yoke of <ital>Astgen v. Smith.</ital> In <ital>National Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers Union of Canada and Melnor Manufacturing Ltd. (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="National Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers Union of Canada and Melnor Manufacturing Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="360" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1989&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c7eb908cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="National Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers Union of Canada and Melnor Manufacturing Ltd. (Re)">National Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers Union of Canada and Melnor Manufacturing Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1989&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Apr. 360</ref>
                          </cite>. See also <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,007" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8c7eb918cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re) (1993), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;16,007 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414, 422, 440, 448, 461, 483, 488, 1000 &amp; 1688 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1" pre.rptr="22" ref.id="Id8c7eb928cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414, 422, 440, 448, 461, 483, 488, 1000 &amp; 1688 (Re)">Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414, 422, 440, 448, 461, 483, 488, 1000 &amp; 1688 (Re) (1993), 22 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 1, &lsqb;1993&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 1350</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Ontario Board adopted the same argument made in Laskin J.&apos;s dissent: that a merger of two unions did not result in a change in the fundamental objects of the organization, since both existed to regulate the relations between the employer and the employees. The Board went on to issue a successorship declaration, despite the lack of a specific merger provision in the constitution as well as a less than unanimous agreement among the membership for a merger.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="1">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Alberta Labour Relations Board, while not completely breaking with <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital>, has expressed doubt in its efficacy. In <ital>IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace>
                            <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,038" pre.rptr="88" ref.id="Id8c7eb938cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)">IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re) (1988), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; Alta. L.R.B.R. 182, 88 C.L.L.C. &para;16,038 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                            </cite>
                        </trace>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Board stated that a union constitution in a reorganization situation should be seen from the perspective of workers wanting to form a union to represent their individual and collective interests in the workplace, rather than from the view of &ldquo;that group of Victorian gentlemen forming a club with a well-stocked wine cellar to provide an overnight London residence that figures so prominently in the law of clubs said to govern trade unions&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace>
                            <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                              <ref p1="IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,038" pre.rptr="88" ref.id="Id8c7eb948cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re)">IWA-Canada, Local 1-207 and Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. (Re) (1988), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; Alta. L.R.B.R. 182, 88 C.L.L.C. &para;16,038 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                            </cite>
                        </trace>, at p. 14,273.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> More recently, in <ital>UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20221125">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-069" pre.rptr="97" ref.id="Id8c7eb958cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)">UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re) (1997), 97 C.L.L.C. &para;220-069, 38 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 10 (Alta.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters, Local 1325 and PCL Industrial Constructors Inc., Re" post.rptr="606" pre.rptr="2022" ref.id="I050e7e50696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters, Local 1325 and PCL Industrial Constructors Inc., Re">United Brotherhood of Carpenters, Local 1325 and PCL Industrial Constructors Inc., Re, &lsqb;2022&rsqb; Alta. L.R.B.R. LD-021, 2022 CarswellAlta 606 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bennett" p2="UFCW, Local 401" post.rptr="190" pre.rptr="107" ref.id="Id8c812a08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401">Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401 (2004), 107 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 190 sub nom. Gateway Casinos Canada Inc. and UFCW, Local 401 (Re) (Alta. L.R.B.), application for judicial review dismissed 23 Admin. L.R. (4th) 244, 108 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 141 (Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>; <ital>Staff Nurses Assn. of Alberta and Rivercrest Lodge Nursing Home Ltd.</ital>, (1995), Alta. L.R.B.R. 83.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Alberta Board has gone farther, and said that the unanimity requirement is unrealistic, and a socially unproductive rule.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The British Columbia Labour Relations Board has refused to follow <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital>. In <ital>Griffin and V.M.R.E.U. (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Griffin and V.M.R.E.U. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="60" pre.rptr="29" ref.id="I6478dca0a34711ebbaba98c7d202b8a1" title="Griffin and V.M.R.E.U. (Re)">Griffin and V.M.R.E.U. (Re) (1995), 29 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 60 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Board approved of a union merger even where one of the unions did not have a merger provision in its constitution. It adopted the reasoning in Laskin J.&apos;s dissent, and went on to say:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">This policy of focusing on a testing of the wishes of the affected workers is far more consistent with the view of unions expressed by Mr. Justice Laskin in his dissent in <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital>, than it is with the &ldquo;gentlemen&apos;s club&rdquo; analogy used by the majority in that decision. Statutory developments since <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital> have only strengthened the logic of treating unions as statutory institutions with their own special characteristics and reinforced the conceptual problems of lumping them in with private clubs.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="Griffin and V.M.R.E.U. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="60" pre.rptr="29" ref.id="I64792ac0a34711ebbaba98c7d202b8a1" title="Griffin and V.M.R.E.U. (Re)">Griffin and V.M.R.E.U. (Re) (1995), 29 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 60 (B.C.)</ref>
                            </cite>, at p. 70.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Even the Ontario courts by the mid-1980s were adopting approaches to union mergers that sought to escape the strictures of <ital>Astgen v. Smith.</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">17</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See, for example, <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McGhie and CALFAA (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="333" pre.rptr="58" ref.id="Id8c839b08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McGhie and CALFAA (Re)">McGhie and CALFAA (Re) (1986), 58 O.R. (2d) 333 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Supreme Court of Canada has now hammered the final nail in the coffin of <ital>Astgen v. Smith.</ital> In <ital>Berry v. Pulley</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">18</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Berry" p2="Pulley" post.rptr="493" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2002&rsqb; 2" ref.id="Id8c839b18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Berry v. Pulley">Berry v. Pulley, &lsqb;2002&rsqb; 2 S.C.R. 493, 211 D.L.R. (4th) 651</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Court rejected the legal fiction that a union member, upon joining a union, enters into a contract with every other member of the union. Instead, the Supreme Court stated, a union member enters into an enforceable contract with the union. For merger and union successorship purposes, this removes any remaining pillar of support for the rule in <ital>Astgen v. Smith.</ital> Without the legal fiction that a union, as a voluntary association, is made up of a multiple complex of contracts among all union members, unanimity is no longer the default rule for union constitutional change when one union joins another.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ahenakew" p2="MacKay" post.rptr="314" pre.rptr="241" ref.id="Id8c839b28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ahenakew v. MacKay">Ahenakew v. MacKay (2004), 241 D.L.R. (4th) 314, 187 O.A.C. 162 (C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">D</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Substantial Compliance with the Union Constitution</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:33</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Introduction</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="20221125">A long-standing labour board requirement for the issuance of a successorship declaration is that both the predecessor union and the successor union must complete the necessary steps in their respective constitutions to effect the merger, amalgamation or jurisdictional transfer.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,261" pre.rptr="63" ref.id="Id8c839b38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)">IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re) (1962), 63 C.L.L.C. &para;16,261 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In the years immediately following <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital>, labour boards in a successorship proceeding interpreted this requirement in a strict manner, and a failure by a union to comply with any of the procedures deemed to be mandatory in the constitution would often be fatal to the request for the declaration.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See, for example, <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Brewers&apos; Warehousing Co. Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="34" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1975&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8c839b48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Brewers&apos; Warehousing Co. Ltd. (Re)">Brewers&apos; Warehousing Co. Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1975&rsqb; 1 Can. L.R.B.R. 34 (Ont.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> But the requirement was later interpreted by labour boards and the courts in a more flexible and liberal manner, and the law now will relieve against technical flaws in the merger process where there has been substantial compliance with the spirit of existing constitutional provisions. &ldquo;Complete constitutional correctness&rdquo; is no longer a prerequisite to a successful union successorship application.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414, 422, 440, 448, 461, 483, 488, 1000 &amp; 1688 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1350" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1993&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c860c08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414, 422, 440, 448, 461, 483, 488, 1000 &amp; 1688 (Re)">Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414, 422, 440, 448, 461, 483, 488, 1000 &amp; 1688 (Re), &lsqb;1993&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1350</ref>
                          </cite>, at para. 84. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Tiercon Corp." p2="Workers United Ontario Council" post.rptr="4953" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2010&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c860c18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council">Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council, &lsqb;2010&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4953</ref>
                        </cite>, at para. 138.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Indeed, it is no longer necessary for a union to even have a specific provision in its constitution or by-laws regarding a merger or transfer in order for a successorship to be effective.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Napier Personal Care Home Ltd." p2="" post.rptr="B75/96" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1996&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c860c28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Napier Personal Care Home Ltd.">Napier Personal Care Home Ltd., &lsqb;1996&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B75/96</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Broadway Pentecostal Care Assn." p2="" post.rptr="B408/95" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1995&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c860c38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Broadway Pentecostal Care Assn.">Broadway Pentecostal Care Assn., &lsqb;1995&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B408/95</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Griffin and VMREU (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="60" pre.rptr="29" ref.id="Id8c860c48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Griffin and VMREU (Re)">Griffin and VMREU (Re) (1995), 29 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 60 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Indicative of this legal sea-change are the 2002 comments of the British Columbia Labour Relations Board, which said that &ldquo;flaws which may constitute even significant technical flaws in proceedings before court, may not preclude the issuing of a &lsqb;successorship&rsqb; declaration.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="General Workers Union of Canada, Local 1" p2="Mulligan" post.rptr="3787" pre.rptr="2002" ref.id="Id8c887d08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="General Workers Union of Canada, Local 1 v. Mulligan">General Workers Union of Canada, Local 1 v. Mulligan, 2002 CarswellBC 3787, &lsqb;2002&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 102</ref>
                        </cite>, at para. 149.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, the Ontario Board has signalled that, in circumstances where one of the unions seeking to affiliate with another union did not have a specific provision in its constitution permitting the union to merge, a declaration under s. 68(2) of the <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital> will not be issued, notwithstanding the fact that a majority of the members had approved the merger.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Healthcare, Office and Professional Employees, Local 2220, UBCJA" p2="Heritage Glen Employees Union" post.rptr="7054" pre.rptr="2018" ref.id="Id8c887d18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Healthcare, Office and Professional Employees, Local 2220, UBCJA v. Heritage Glen Employees Union">Healthcare, Office and Professional Employees, Local 2220, UBCJA v. Heritage Glen Employees Union, 2018 CarswellOnt 7054 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> On the other hand, <ital>Chicopee Manufacturing Employees&apos; Association</ital> is an example of a successful merger where both unions complied with their statutory and internal constitutional requirements, and a successor trade union declaration was issued by the Ontario Labour Relations Board.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="USW" p2="CMEA" post.rptr="6904" pre.rptr="2022" ref.id="I050fb6d0696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="USW v. CMEA">USW v. CMEA, 2022 CarswellOnt 6904 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:34</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Substantial compliance and merger procedures</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A leading example of the application by a labour board of the &ldquo;substantial compliance&rdquo; rule is the Ontario Board decision in <ital>UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re).</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="542" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1984&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c887d28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)">UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1984&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 542</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Here, the union constitution did not contain a merger provision and, in a single vote at a specially called meeting, a majority of the membership voted both to amend the constitution and to join another union. Dissidents opposed to the merger challenged the outcome on, among other grounds, that the constitutional pro<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-78" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;79" folio="5-77" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;77" ?>visions had not been strictly followed. In an analysis which departed significantly from the &ldquo;strict reading&rdquo; rule cast by the Ontario Court of Appeal decision in <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital>, the Ontario Board found that there was nothing in the union&apos;s constitution or in the law to compel unanimity or a rigid interpretation of the governing documents:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">&lsqb;T&rsqb;here is nothing in the constitution to prevent a merger &hellip; Neither its terms, nor the Association&apos;s past practice, nor the testimony before us, suggest that those words must be limited so as to exclude an amendment to permit the merger or affiliation with another union &hellip; There is nothing in the <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital> to suggest that one should infer or read into a union constitution some unstated but fundamental objects which limit the union&apos;s freedom of action &mdash; at least in the absence of express constitutional limits.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="542" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1984&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c8aee08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)">UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1984&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 542</ref>
                            </cite>, at pp. 558&ndash;9.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Board went on to rule that the union&apos;s decision to complete the merger in a two-step process, and to combine the two steps into a single vote, did not invalidate the merger. &ldquo;To hold otherwise, would be to take an entirely too technical view of the required conduct of this Association.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="542" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1984&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c8aee18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)">UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1984&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 542</ref>
                          </cite>, at p. 559. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Navco Food Services Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="326" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1971&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c8aee28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Navco Food Services Ltd. (Re)">Navco Food Services Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1971&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. June 326</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Accordingly, the &ldquo;substantial compliance&rdquo; rule has been applied and successorship declarations have been issued in circumstances where probationary employees who had not been admitted into membership had not been given notice of the merger meeting,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="I.B.L. Industries Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1144" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c8aee38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="I.B.L. Industries Ltd. (Re)">I.B.L. Industries Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1144</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where employees who were eligible to vote were not given notice of the meeting and employees who had no right to vote were allowed to,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="I.B.L. Industries Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1144" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c8aee48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="I.B.L. Industries Ltd. (Re)">I.B.L. Industries Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1144</ref>
                          </cite>. In this case, the Board found that the improperly excluded and included votes would not have materially affected the outcome of the ballot. See also <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="542" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1984&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c8d5f08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)">UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1984&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 542</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where the merger meeting was held on a Sunday,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="86" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c8d5f18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)">Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Jan. 86</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where the constitution of the successor union did not contain the authority to represent employees working in another occupation,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-069" pre.rptr="97" ref.id="Id8c8d5f28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)">UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re) (1997), 97 C.L.L.C. &para;220-069, 38 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 10 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where the governing union constitutions did not expressly contemplate a merger or amalgamation,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Forward Transport Ltd. and CLAC, Local 67 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-016" pre.rptr="2006" ref.id="Id8c8d5f38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Forward Transport Ltd. and CLAC, Local 67 (Re)">Forward Transport Ltd. and CLAC, Local 67 (Re), 2006 C.L.L.C. &para;220-016 (B.C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where a marginal and honest error was made in calculating the precise number required for a two-thirds majority during a merger vote,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Waterloo Region District School Board and OSSTF (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="86" pre.rptr="72" ref.id="Id8c8fd008cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Waterloo Region District School Board and OSSTF (Re)">Waterloo Region District School Board and OSSTF (Re) (2001), 72 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 86 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where some employees signed union membership cards indicating that they agreed to be bound to a constitution and by-laws that had yet to be approved,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Teachers&apos; Federation Employees&apos; Union and IWA-Canada, Local Union Number 1-3567 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="16" pre.rptr="107" ref.id="Id8c8fd018cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Teachers&apos; Federation Employees&apos; Union and IWA-Canada, Local Union Number 1-3567 (Re)">Teachers&apos; Federation Employees&apos; Union and IWA-Canada, Local Union Number 1-3567 (Re) (2004), 107 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 16 sub nom. BCTF and IWA-Canada, Local 1-3567, application for reconsideration dismissed 107 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 23 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where some lax financial record keeping was evident,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="241" pre.rptr="105" ref.id="Id8c8fd028cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re)">Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re) (2004), 105 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 241 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and where a trade union had constitutionally transformed itself from a local of one union to an independent national union as an interim step to merging with another union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="241" pre.rptr="105" ref.id="Id8c8fd038cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re)">Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re) (2004), 105 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 241 (B.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,007" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8c8fd048cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re) (1993), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;16,007 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Where it was unclear whether the true successor in a union merger was the local or the parent union, the Alberta Labour Relations Board held that it would roll the two successorship applications into one, and not allow this ambiguity to stand as a legal impediment to a merger application.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-069" pre.rptr="97" ref.id="Id8c924108cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)">UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re) (1997), 97 C.L.L.C. &para;220-069, 38 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 10 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, in the same decision, the Alberta Board ultimately invalidated the merger because the union had wrongly decided not to count spoiled ballots as votes against the merger, and because it failed to hold membership meetings as required by its constitution. These were fundamental, not technical, defects in the eyes of the Board, and it declined to order a s. 47(2) vote. As well, where the two unions involved in the successorship application have conducted an employee vote under the supervision of the governing labour relations board, an employer challenge as to whether the vote should have occurred before as opposed to after the filing of the application will be dismissed.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Winston Park Retirement Home" p2="Winston Park Retirement Home" post.rptr="137" pre.rptr="76" ref.id="Id8c924118cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Winston Park Retirement Home">Winston Park Retirement Home (2001), 76 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 137 sub nom. CAW-Canada and SEIU, Local 220 (Re), &lsqb;2001&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1479</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-80" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;81" folio="5-79" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;79" ?>
                <para.text date.updated="0">In a more recent ruling,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bennett" p2="UFCW, Local 401" post.rptr="190" pre.rptr="107" ref.id="Id8c924128cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401">Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401 (2004), 107 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 190 sub nom. Gateway Casinos Canada Inc. and UFCW, Local 401 (Re) (Alta.), application for judicial review dismissed 23 Admin. L.R. (4th) 244, 108 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 141 (Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Alberta Labour Relations Board stated that it will apply a &ldquo;presumption of regularity&rdquo; when examining a challenge to a union successorship application, and that it will require &ldquo;clear and convincing&rdquo; evidence of non-compliance in order to reject such an application. When the Alberta Board applied this test to the &ldquo;substantial compliance&rdquo; standard, it upheld a merger process despite concerns about the low membership turnout to approve the agreement, the failure to advertise the merger meeting as a &ldquo;special meeting&rdquo; as per the union constitution, and the failure to hold more than one membership meeting for a workplace that operated on a 24-hour basis. In finding that the merger process met the necessary constitutional and statutory requirements, the Alberta Board stated it was satisfied that &ldquo;a member of ordinary experience and intelligence&rdquo; would have known from the union&apos;s history of frequent meetings and votes that the question of merging with another union was the topic of the meeting in question. The Board declined to order a vote membership vote. This ruling was upheld on judicial review by the Alberta Court of Queen&apos;s Bench.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bennett" p2="UFCW, Local 401" post.rptr="190" pre.rptr="107" ref.id="Id8c924138cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401">Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401 (2004), 107 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 190 sub nom. Gateway Casinos Canada Inc. and UFCW, Local 401 (Re) (Alta.), application for judicial review dismissed 23 Admin. L.R. (4th) 244, 108 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 141 (Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Board has shown flexibility on the issue of &ldquo;substantial compliance&rdquo;. In <ital>Trans Nations Inc. (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">17</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Trans Nations Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1298" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1981&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c94b208cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Trans Nations Inc. (Re)">Trans Nations Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1981&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept. 1298</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> two locals of the same international had merged, although neither local had a merger provision in their respective by-laws and neither local had attempted to amend their governing documents to permit a merger. Nevertheless, the Board issued a declaration, reasoning that since the parent union of both locals had a merger provision in its constitution, and members of a local are also members of the parent, there was substantial compliance with the constitution. Although the Board re-introduced a strict reading of the constitution and refused to issue a declaration in a 1986 ruling when a predecessor union held a single vote to amend its constitution and to approve a merger resolution,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">18</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Waterloo (Regional Municipality) (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1861" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1986&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c94b218cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Waterloo (Regional Municipality) (Re)">Waterloo (Regional Municipality) (Re), &lsqb;1986&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1861</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> it has subsequently re-affirmed the &ldquo;substantial compliance&rdquo; rule.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,007" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8c94b228cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re) (1993), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;16,007 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In British Columbia, the Labour Relations Board has similarly indicated that it will read the union constitution purposively to determine whether the parent union was acting within its jurisdiction when it invoked its powers under the merger provisions.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">20</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Weyerhaeuser Canada Ltd. and CEP, Local 10-B (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="119" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2003&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c94b238cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Weyerhaeuser Canada Ltd. and CEP, Local 10-B (Re)">Weyerhaeuser Canada Ltd. and CEP, Local 10-B (Re), &lsqb;2003&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 119</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">However, where the successorship application involves a local attempting to secede from its parent, the Ontario Board appears to require a more rigorous adherence to constitutional procedures. In <ital>Hillside Townhouses Ltd. and Hospitality, Commercial and Service Employees Union, Local, 73 (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">21</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Hillside Townhouses Ltd. and Hospitality, Commercial and Service Employees Union, Local, 73 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1075" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1995&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c94b248cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Hillside Townhouses Ltd. and Hospitality, Commercial and Service Employees Union, Local, 73 (Re)">Hillside Townhouses Ltd. and Hospitality, Commercial and Service Employees Union, Local, 73 (Re), &lsqb;1995&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. August 1075</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Board ruled that the union local tested membership opinion by a show of hands, and did not hold a secret ballot as required in the International constitution. Accordingly, the application failed. Similarly, where the union local attempting to secede did not clearly put the question of disaffiliation and succession to its membership, its attempt to leave the parent union was nullified.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">22</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW, Local 1252" p2="Cashin" post.rptr="620" pre.rptr="217" ref.id="Id8c972308cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin">UFCW, Local 1252 v. Cashin (2002), 217 D.L.R. (4th) 620, 216 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 41, 84 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161 (Nfld. C.A.), affg 149 Nfld. &amp; P.E.I.R. 112 (Nfld. S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Thus, older common law decisions which had held that voting by ineligible delegates at a union merger meeting thereby invalidated the entire process (&ldquo;far greater importance than a mere irregularity&rdquo;) are probably no longer reliable authority.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">23</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Faulds" p2="Hesford" post.rptr="292" pre.rptr="10" ref.id="Id8c972318cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Faulds v. Hesford">Faulds v. Hesford (1957), 10 D.L.R. (2d) 292, (1957-58) 23 W.W.R. 625 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>. The legal foundation for the court&apos;s decision was the same as the later decision in <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital>: a merger between two unions, both being unincorporated voluntary associations, was a &ldquo;fundamental&rdquo; change in their objects, and therefore required strict adherence to all of the constitutional steps.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The reasoning in this older jurisprudence was premised on the identification of a merger as a &ldquo;fundamental&rdquo; change to the objects of a union, a premise that labour law has more recently rejected.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">24</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Griffin and V.M.R.E.U. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="60" pre.rptr="29" ref.id="Id8c972328cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Griffin and V.M.R.E.U. (Re)">Griffin and V.M.R.E.U. (Re) (1995), 29 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 60 (B.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="National Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers Union of Canada and Melnor Manufacturing Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="360" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1989&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c999408cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="National Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers Union of Canada and Melnor Manufacturing Ltd. (Re)">National Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers Union of Canada and Melnor Manufacturing Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1989&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Apr. 360</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="542" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1984&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c999418cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re)">UFCW and Waterloo Spinning Mills Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1984&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 542</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Nevertheless, the Ontario Court of Justice has indicated that, where a merger referendum vote had not been <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-82" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;83" folio="5-81" hyphenated="y" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;81" ?>approved by the executive board of a union as required by the governing constitution, it would grant an injunction to prevent the vote from proceeding.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">25</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Markee" p2="Cook" post.rptr="&para;210-058" pre.rptr="96" ref.id="Id8c999428cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Markee v. Cook">Markee v. Cook (1996), 96 C.L.L.C. &para;210-058 (Ont. Ct. (Gen. Div.))</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:35</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Substantial compliance and notice</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A common procedural flaw in the merger process that unions are often challenged on by dissident employees is the question of notice. Often, the complaint will be either that the notice given fell short of the time requirements in the constitution, or that the notice did not adequately alert the membership to the significance of the merger meeting. However, labour boards have generally ruled that, while the constitutional provisions regarding notice will not be taken lightly, they will not be unduly concerned with form if, in substance, the notice requirement has been met.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RWDSU and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,007" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8c999438cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RWDSU and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">RWDSU and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re) (1993), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;16,007 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="637" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1977&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c999448cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)">Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1977&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Oct. 637</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Two distinct lines of case law authority have emerged over the degree of notice required in the union merger process. The early authority took the strict position that the notice provisions in the union constitution had to be fully complied with in order for the merger process to meet either the common law or the statutory requirements. Where a constitution was silent on the requirement for notice, the common law would imply specific notice where an issue as important as dissolution and merger was involved.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Faulds" p2="Hesford" post.rptr="292" pre.rptr="10" ref.id="Id8c9c0508cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Faulds v. Hesford">Faulds v. Hesford (1957), 10 D.L.R. (2d) 292, (1957&ndash;58) 23 W.W.R. 625 (B.C.S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">This strict line was subsequently adopted and applied by the Ontario Labour Relations Board throughout the 1970s. Notice of the specifics of a merger meeting were required, even where the constitution was silent.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="86" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c9c0518cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)">Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Jan. 86</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Where only slightly more than half the membership was provided with notice,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Beef Terminal Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="15" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1970&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c9c0528cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Beef Terminal Ltd. (Re)">Beef Terminal Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1970&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Apr. 15</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> or where there was no satisfactory evidence before the Board that the membership was adequately notified of a merger meeting,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="637" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1977&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c9c0538cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)">Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1977&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Oct. 637</ref>
                        </cite>. Note, however, that while the declaration was refused by the Board in this case, it did rule, on p. 640, that &ldquo;where in substance the notice requirements have been met and the members have in fact been provided with the information required by the constitution (or otherwise required by law), the Board should not be unduly concerned with the form which the notice might take&rdquo;.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> a successorship declaration was not issued.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">However, a more flexible approach to the requirements for notice began with the emergence of the &ldquo;substantial compliance&rdquo; rule. Beginning in the late 1970s, the Ontario Board granted successorship declarations in cases where a constitutional provision requiring only three days&apos; notice of a merger meeting was met,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Peerless Plastics Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="848" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c9e7608cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Peerless Plastics Ltd. (Re)">Peerless Plastics Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept. 848</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and where an additional membership meeting to vote on a merger proposal was called by the executive on notice shorter than permitted by the constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Children&apos;s Aid Society of Metropolitan Toronto (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="24" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1980&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c9e7618cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Children&apos;s Aid Society of Metropolitan Toronto (Re)">Children&apos;s Aid Society of Metropolitan Toronto (Re), &lsqb;1980&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Jan. 24</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, the significant turning point occurred with the decision in <ital>Trans Nations Inc. (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Trans Nations Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1298" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1981&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c9e7628cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Trans Nations Inc. (Re)">Trans Nations Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1981&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept. 1298</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where the Board declined to follow the rule that there was an absolute requirement for specific notice, even where a union constitution was silent on notice. The new rule would look no further than the notice requirements in the constitution: &ldquo;If there is a constitutional provision dealing with merger, it must be followed. If the provision does not require specific notice to each and every member, then none is required.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Trans Nations Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1298" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1981&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8c9e7638cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Trans Nations Inc. (Re)">Trans Nations Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1981&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept. 1298</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 1306.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> The Board held in this case that it was satisfied with a posted notice in some locations and verbal notice of a vote in other locations, and issued a declaration.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Since <ital>Trans Nations Inc. (Re)</ital>, the notice requirement has been further refined to the point where compliance with the spirit, if not necessarily the letter, of the governing constitution has become the applicable test. The Ontario High Court of Justice in <ital>McGhie and CALFAA (Re)</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McGhie and CALFAA (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="333" pre.rptr="58" ref.id="Id8c9e7648cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McGhie and CALFAA (Re)">McGhie and CALFAA (Re) (1986), 58 O.R. (2d) 333 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> would not interfere with a union merger, despite a number of constitutional irregularities involving notice. These included a merger notice that did not specify the agenda at the merger convention, and a merger report that was circulated to the delegates two and one-half weeks before the convention instead of the stipulated eight weeks. The court found that &ldquo;&lsqb;t&rsqb;here may have been some technical deficiencies, but there has been substantial compli<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-84" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;11;30" nep=";;85" folio="5-83" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="4" sp=";;83" ?>ance with the spirit of the existing constitutional provisions&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McGhie and CALFAA (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="333" pre.rptr="58" ref.id="Id8ca0e708cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McGhie and CALFAA (Re)">McGhie and CALFAA (Re) (1986), 58 O.R. (2d) 333 (H.C.J.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 344.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Subsequent decisions by the Board have affirmed the primacy of the &ldquo;substantial compliance&rdquo; rule in judging the adequacy of a merger notice.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20211028">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 1730" p2="USW" post.rptr="8273" pre.rptr="2021" ref.id="I5e00bfe0370e11ec80e0c0b599422da1" title="IBEW, Local 1730 v. USW">IBEW, Local 1730 v. USW, 2021 CarswellOnt 8273 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Tiercon Corp." p2="Workers United Ontario Council" post.rptr="4953" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2010&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ca0e718cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council">Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council, &lsqb;2010&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4953</ref>
                          </cite>, at para. 224; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,007" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8ca0e728cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re) (1993), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;16,007 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CAW" p2="I.B.L. Industries Ltd." post.rptr="1114" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ca0e738cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CAW v. I.B.L. Industries Ltd.">CAW v. I.B.L. Industries Ltd., &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1114</ref>
                          </cite>. But see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1252" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1985&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ca0e748cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re)">L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1985&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1252</ref>
                        </cite>, where the Board held, on p. 1268, that:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">&lsqb;W&rsqb;e were prepared to assume that the law will relieve against mere technical deficiencies when there has been substantial compliance with the spirit of the existing constitutional provisions. A requirement that a notice of meeting set out the business intended to be transacted at that meeting is not, however, a mere technical requirement.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                        <para.text date.updated="0">And later on the same page, the Board harkened back to <ital>Faulds v. Hesford</ital> when it said that:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">&ldquo;&lsqb;E&rsqb;xpress notice that merger is under consideration may be implicitly required even if there is no express constitutional requirement for detailed notice.&rdquo;</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Alberta Labour Relations Board has stated that it will judge the adequacy of the notice provided by the union by asking whether &ldquo;a member of ordinary experience and intelligence&rdquo; would have understood the purpose of the meetings and the merger process from the information made available by the union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bennett" p2="UFCW, Local 401" post.rptr="190" pre.rptr="107" ref.id="Id8ca35808cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401">Bennett v. UFCW, Local 401 (2004), 107 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 190 sub nom. Gateway Casinos Canada Inc. and UFCW, Local 401 (Re) (Alta.), application for judicial review dismissed 23 Admin. L.R. (4th) 244, 2005 C.L.L.C. &para;220-003 (Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> With regards to an interim step towards a union merger, the Alberta Labour Relations Board has held that it will not impose any notice requirements upon a union even where no requirement exists in the union constitution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Airtex Industries Ltd." p2="Engineered Air Employees Assn." post.rptr="251" pre.rptr="8" ref.id="Id8ca35818cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Airtex Industries Ltd. v. Engineered Air Employees Assn.">Airtex Industries Ltd. v. Engineered Air Employees Assn. (1990), 8 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 251 (Alta. L.R.B.), quashed on other grounds 109 A.R. 27 (Q.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In circumstances where a national parent union disputed the ability of a British Columbia local to merge with another provincial local and imposed a trusteeship, the British Columbia Labour Relations Board has defined its particular role in overseeing union mergers. In <ital>Construction, Maintenance &amp; Allied Workers Union, Local 2020 v. CEP, Local 470</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Construction, Maintenance &amp; Allied Workers Union, Local 2020" p2="CEP, Local 470" post.rptr="135" pre.rptr="213" ref.id="Id8ca35828cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Construction, Maintenance &amp; Allied Workers Union, Local 2020 v. CEP, Local 470">Construction, Maintenance &amp; Allied Workers Union, Local 2020 v. CEP, Local 470 (2012), 213 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 135 (B.C. L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CEP" p2="CEP, Local 1928" post.rptr="149" pre.rptr="213" ref.id="Id8ca5c908cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CEP v. CEP, Local 1928">CEP v. CEP, Local 1928 (2012), 213 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 149, 2012 CarswellBC 1567</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Board ruled that its adjudicative focus is not on the national parent union&apos;s constitution, but on whether the merging local had completed the requisite steps &mdash; including sufficient notice and a proper vote &mdash; to ensure substantial compliance for the merger had been reached prior to the imposition of the trusteeship. In these circumstances, it ruled that the merger of the two provincial locals satisfied the requirements under the <ital>Code.</ital>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:36</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Substantial Compliance and Interim Steps</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A seceding union local that intends to leave one union parent and join with another union initially may choose to become an independent employee association as a transitional step before the ultimate merger. This &ldquo;marriage of convenience&rdquo; does not, in itself, create any legal or industrial relations grounds for denial of a successorship application by a labour relations board.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="241" pre.rptr="105" ref.id="Id8ca5c918cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re)">Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re) (2004), 105 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 241 (B.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ontario Hydro and L.U.I.N.A., Ontario Provincial District Council" p2="Ontario Hydro and L.U.I.N.A., Ontario Provincial District Council" post.rptr="4561" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1998&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ca5c928cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ontario Hydro and L.U.I.N.A., Ontario Provincial District Council">Ontario Hydro and L.U.I.N.A., Ontario Provincial District Council, &lsqb;1998&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 4561</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Nor would the fact that the independent employee association is not the true or final applicant stain the application unless there was persuasive evidence of misrepresentation or abuse of process. This two-step process has sometimes occurred when a union local wishes to leave a parent union affiliated with the Canadian Labour Congress (CLC) and join another CLC-affiliated union. Whether this process offends the CLC&apos;s rules against raiding the locals of affiliated unions is not a matter of direct concern to a labour relations board examining a union successorship application, unless the affected employees have been misled as to whom they are supporting as a successor bargaining agent.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="241" pre.rptr="105" ref.id="Id8ca5c938cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re)">Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re) (2004), 105 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 241 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">E</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Employee Support and Representative Votes</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:37</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Introduction</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The legislative provisions in the Canadian common law jurisdictions on union successorships have left considerable discretion in the hands of labour relations boards to determine the criteria for the issuance of a successor declaration.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-86" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;11;30" nep=";;87" folio="5-85" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="4" sp=";;85" ?>
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:38</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Ontario</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In an early, influential decision, the Ontario Board ruled that one of its pre-conditions to issuing a declaration would be evidence of support by a majority of the affected employees for the union reorganization.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para; 16,261" pre.rptr="63" ref.id="Id8ca83a08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)">IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re) (1962), 63 C.L.L.C. &para; 16,261 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>. On p. 1119, the Board stated that it must be &ldquo;satisfied that a majority of the employees in the bargaining unit concerned, have given their approval to the action of the predecessor trade union&rdquo;.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Generally, this requirement for employee support was met by evidence of an internal membership meeting held with adequate notice, where a majority of the attending members voted in favour of the reorganization proposal.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,261" pre.rptr="63" ref.id="Id8ca83a18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)">IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re) (1962), 63 C.L.L.C. &para;16,261 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Support by a majority of all the employees in the affected union or bargaining unit was not demanded. In Ontario, the application of this criteria continued throughout the 1960s and 1970s. The Ontario Court of Appeal&apos;s ruling in <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Astgen" p2="Smith" post.rptr="657" pre.rptr="7" ref.id="Id8ca83a28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Astgen v. Smith">Astgen v. Smith (1969), 7 D.L.R. (3d) 657, &lsqb;1970&rsqb; 1 O.R. 129 (C.A.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> despite suggestions to the contrary,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See, for example, <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="637" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1977&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ca83a38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)">Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1977&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Oct. 637</ref>
                        </cite>, where, on p. 640, the Board stated that:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; at one time the Board refused to recognize any merger, amalgamation or transfer of jurisdiction unless it had been approved by a majority of employees in the affected bargaining unit. (See, for example, <cite finder="legacy" type="periodical">
                                <ref normalpub="lr" ref.id="Icffc20808cd211eba0a7cba16a32519f" reftext="The Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton">The Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton, 63 C.L.L.C. Para. 16,261</ref>
                            </cite>). However, since the decision of the Ontario Court of Appeal in <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital> (1969) 7 D.L.R. (3d) 657, the board has instead required only assurance that either the constitutional provisions of the predecessor trade union regarding a merger, amalgamation, or transfer of jurisdiction have been followed (<ital>Navco Food Services Limited</ital> &lsqb;1971&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Feb. 80) &hellip;</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                        <para.text date.updated="0">The Board in <ital>Zehrs Markets</ital> overestimated the change involved. Even in <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,261" pre.rptr="63" ref.id="Id8caaab08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re)">IBEW, Local 138 and Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (Re) (1962), 63 C.L.L.C.&para;16,261 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>, issued before <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital>, the Board did not require the support of a majority of all of the employees in the affected bargaining unit, but only a majority of the employees voting at the membership meeting. In that case, there were 100 employees in the bargaining unit, but only thirty attended the membership meeting and voted on the transfer of jurisdiction resolution. Similarly, in <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Navco Food Services (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="80" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1971&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caaab18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Navco Food Services (Re)">Navco Food Services (Re), &lsqb;1971&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Feb. 80</ref>
                        </cite>, which was decided after <ital>Astgen v. Smith</ital>, only twenty of the eighty-one members of the bargaining unit attended the merger meeting and voted on the resolution. In both cases, the Board issued a successorship declaration.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> did not substantially change this interpretation. Where the predecessor union established that it had sought membership approval by circulating proper notice, a majority of the attending members at a meeting had voted in favour of the reorganization, and all of these procedures had followed the union constitution, then the Board would issue a successorship declaration.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Children&apos;s Aid Society of Metropolitan Toronto (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="24" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1980&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caaab28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Children&apos;s Aid Society of Metropolitan Toronto (Re)">Children&apos;s Aid Society of Metropolitan Toronto (Re), &lsqb;1980&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Jan. 24</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Peerless Plastics Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="848" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caaab38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Peerless Plastics Ltd. (Re)">Peerless Plastics Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept. 848</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Peel County Bd. of Education (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="623" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1973&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caaab48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Peel County Bd. of Education (Re)">Peel County Bd. of Education (Re), &lsqb;1973&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 623</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Where the Board was not satisfied that the merger had employee support, it would decline to issue a declaration.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Standard Tube Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="375" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1976&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caaab58cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Standard Tube Canada Ltd. (Re)">Standard Tube Canada Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1976&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. June 375</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Other jurisdictions adopted and applied a similar criteria for employee support.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="17" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8cad1c08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re)">British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; 1 Can. L.R.B.R. 17 (B.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Teamsters and United Brewery, Flour, Cereal, Soft Drink and Distillery Workers, Locals 250, 284, 287, 288, 285, 245 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="479" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1974&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8cad1c18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Teamsters and United Brewery, Flour, Cereal, Soft Drink and Distillery Workers, Locals 250, 284, 287, 288, 285, 245 (Re)">Teamsters and United Brewery, Flour, Cereal, Soft Drink and Distillery Workers, Locals 250, 284, 287, 288, 285, 245 (Re), &lsqb;1974&rsqb; 1 Can. L.R.B.R. 479 (Alta.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">However, by the 1980s, a divergence was developing in the Ontario Board case law on the determination of employee support in union successorship proceedings. According to one approach, the Board&apos;s role under the successorship provision was merely declaratory.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Trans Nations Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1298" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1981&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caf8d08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Trans Nations Inc. (Re)">Trans Nations Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1981&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept. 1298</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="637" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1977&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caf8d18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re)">Zehrs Markets Division of Zehrmart Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1977&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Oct. 637</ref>
                          </cite>. In <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Jaeger Machine Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1082" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1983&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caf8d28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Jaeger Machine Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">Jaeger Machine Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1983&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July 1082</ref>
                        </cite>, the Board wrote, at pp. 1086&ndash;7, that:</para.text>
                        <quote.block date.updated="0">
                          <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Essentially, the Board looks upon a merger by trade unions as an internal procedure, subject to the overriding of the Board. Section &lsqb;63 &mdash; now s. 68&rsqb; allows the Board to make a declaration that the procedures have been properly followed to effect such merger. While at one time the Board required that a merger should be approved by a majority of employees in the bargaining unit regardless of internal procedures, the Board has recognized subsequently that constitutional provisions, if any, should prevail and only if there are no provisions should the Board require unanimous approval.</para.text>
                          </quote.para>
                        </quote.block>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In other words, its inquiry should be limited to whether, as claimed, a merger, amalgamation or transfer of jurisdiction had occurred that was in compliance with the governing constitution. This case law appeared to imply that if the union constitution stipulated a merger approval process that required less than employee support &mdash; such as consent by an executive or by a delegate convention &mdash; then the Board would be satisfied with that, and a declaration should follow.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Formula Plastics Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="702" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caf8d38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Formula Plastics Inc. (Re)">Formula Plastics Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 702</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A second approach insisted that a prerequisite for a Board declaration was evidence of employee support, presumably through some form of internal vote that was opened to the full membership.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="L.M.L.Foods Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1252" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1985&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caf8d48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="L.M.L.Foods Inc. (Re)">L.M.L.Foods Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1985&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1252</ref>
                          </cite>, where, in <ital>obiter</ital> com<?WGXPPfootnoteLeafBreak folio="5-87" footnoteHyphenated="y" ?>ments on p. 1269, the Board stated that &ldquo;we think it important to observe that the wishes of affected employees are always a relevant concern on an application under section &lsqb;63 &mdash; now s. 68&rsqb;&rdquo;. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Waterloo (Regional Municipality) (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1861" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1986&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caf8d58cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Waterloo (Regional Municipality) (Re)">Waterloo (Regional Municipality) (Re), &lsqb;1986&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1861</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Brockville (City) (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="76" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1979&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8caf8d68cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Brockville (City) (Re)">Brockville (City) (Re), &lsqb;1979&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Feb. 76</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> This approach claimed to reflect the Act&apos;s purpose, which encouraged trade unions that were the &ldquo;freely <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-88" releaseDate="2022;12;31" backBlank="n" folio="5-87" nep=";;93" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;87"?>designated representatives of employees&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital>, R.S.O. 1990, c. L.2, Preamble (Repealed 1992, c. 21, s. 5).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>L.M.L. Foods Inc. Re</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="L.M.L.Foods Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1252" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1985&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cb1fe08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="L.M.L.Foods Inc. (Re)">L.M.L.Foods Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1985&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1252</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the union executive had arranged an amendment to the constitution which gave to it the authority to approve a merger without consulting the membership, if the executive passed the merger resolution unanimously. The Board disapproved of the merger on the grounds that the constitution had been improperly amended, but it would have also declined to issue a declaration because the wishes of the affected employees had not been canvassed. Thus, according to this approach, the Board&apos;s function is not strictly a declaratory one, but rather it must ensure that the labour relations policies reflected in the legislation are applied in union successorship cases.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="610" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1989&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cb1fe18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)">IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1989&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. June 610</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 615.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The diverging approaches came to a head in <ital>IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re).</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20221125">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="491" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1988&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cb46f08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re).">IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 491, affd &lsqb;1990&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 1365 sub nom. IBEW, Local 594 v. IBEW, Local 586 (Div. Ct.)</ref>
                          </cite>. The Board had earlier avoided tackling the issue head-on in <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1413" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cb46f18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc. (Re)">M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1413</ref>
                        </cite>, where two locals of the same union were being combined into one. The constitution of the parent union did not require the consent or approval of the locals or their membership to accomplish the transfer of jurisdiction, but a vote was held nevertheless. The Board noted at p. 1418, that the constitutional provisions were followed, notice had been given, and a majority membership vote in favour of the transfer was obtained. &ldquo;Thus any requirements additional to those set out in the Constitution which <ital>might</ital> be required were satisfied&rdquo;.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> In this decision, the parent union decided to merge one of its locals with another. The local that was losing its identity was opposed. Under the parent constitution, the authority to merge locals rested solely with the union president, and there was no requirement for local or membership approval. In issuing a successorship declaration, the Board&apos;s decision adopted the first approach, ruling that, as the parent constitution did not require membership approval, it was not going to insist upon it:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">We disagree with the arguments put to us to the effect that the Constitution impliedly requires the approval of Local 594&apos;s members and Local 586&apos;s members before a merger or amalgamation of the locals can be effected under article 15, section 5. We are aware of no principle of interpretation that would require, or even permit, us to imply a need for membership approval of a merger or amalgamation, when the Constitution clearly empowers the International President to decide on mergers or amalgamations of locals.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="491" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1988&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cb46f28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re).">IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 491, affd &lsqb;1990&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 1365 sub nom. IBEW, Local 594 v. IBEW, Local 586 (Div. Ct.), at p. 495 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                              </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, Local 772" p2="United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada" post.rptr="33" pre.rptr="2013" ref.id="Id8cb46f38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, Local 772 v. United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada">United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, Local 772 v. United Association of Journeymen and Apprentices of the Plumbing and Pipefitting Industry of the United States and Canada, 2013 NBCA 33, 2013 CarswellNB 213</ref>
                            </cite>.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
                <para.text date.updated="0">On judicial review, the Ontario Divisional Court ruled that the Board had properly acted within its discretion, and that it was not required to satisfy itself that the merger had employee support before issuing a declaration.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="491" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1988&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cb46f48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)">IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)., &lsqb;1988&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 491, affd &lsqb;1990&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 1365 sub nom. IBEW, Local 594 v. IBEW, Local 586 (Div. Ct.)</ref>
                          </cite>, where the same panel that heard the original case debated the issue of the diverging approaches to the requirement for employee support. Two panel members adopted the first approach, while the third preferred the reasoning in <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="L.M.L.Foods Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1252" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1985&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cb46f58cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="L.M.L.Foods Inc. (Re)">L.M.L.Foods Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1985&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1252</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">More recently, the Ontario Board has held that the approval of a union reorganization by a delegate structure was perfectly acceptable. In <ital>RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">17</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,007" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8cb6e008cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re) (1993), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;16,007 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414, 422, 440, 448, 461, 483, 488, 1000 &amp; 1688 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1" pre.rptr="22" ref.id="Id8cb6e018cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414, 422, 440, 448, 461, 483, 488, 1000 &amp; 1688 (Re)">Bay-Kingston and USWA, Locals 414, 422, 440, 448, 461, 483, 488, 1000 &amp; 1688 (Re) (1993), 22 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 1, &lsqb;1993&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 1350</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Board did not order the union to hold an employee plebiscite where its constitution and by-laws did not require one. Rather, it noted the well-established tradition of delegate conventions within union self-government, as well as the strain on union resources to conduct a vote among a large and dispersed membership. Moreover, it wrote, requiring a full membership vote &ldquo;implies holding unions to a standard of democracy far in excess of that required in Canadian society generally. The delegate structure here was no more undemocratic than any representative democracy.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">18</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,007" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8cb95108cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re) (1993), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;16,007 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>, at p. 14,053. See also <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173" p2="" post.rptr="244" pre.rptr="27" ref.id="Id8cb95118cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173">Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173 (1995), 27 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 244, &lsqb;1995&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July 954</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Board, in <ital>George &amp; Asmussen Ltd.</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="George &amp; Asmussen Ltd." p2="" post.rptr="639" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2002&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cb95128cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="George &amp; Asmussen Ltd.">George &amp; Asmussen Ltd., &lsqb;2002&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July/August 639, 86 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 50</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Unifor, Local 588G" p2="CEP, Local 588G" post.rptr="9905" pre.rptr="2014" ref.id="Id8cb95138cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Unifor, Local 588G v. CEP, Local 588G">Unifor, Local 588G v. CEP, Local 588G, 2014 CarswellOnt 9905, &lsqb;2014&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 1883 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> has provided a general three-part test as to how and when it will apply its discretion in union successorship matters. First, it will ask whether there has been an adequate canvassing of the wishes of the appropriate people. In the case of the complete transfer of jurisdiction from one union to another, the transferor union must engage in a process that involves the entire membership. However, where the successorship involves only a single bargaining unit, then the facts of the particular case will determine whether an adequate canvassing has occurred. Second, the Board will assess whether the constitutional requirements for both unions have been substantially complied with. And third, it will ask whether there are other relevant factors to be considered, such as aspirations for complete independence from the parent union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">20</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Tiercon Corp." p2="Workers United Ontario Council" post.rptr="396" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cb95148cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council">Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 396</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="120" pre.rptr="71" ref.id="Id8cb95158cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC (Re)">Ontario Provincial Conference of the International Bricklayers and Allied Craftworkers and BAC (Re) (2001), 71 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 120 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.trace="20260211" date.updated="20260211">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Board may take diverging approaches to the adjudication of successorship applications, depending on the type of transaction that grounds the putative successorship. In <ital>Imperial Precast Corp</ital>.,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">21</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="L.I.U.N.A., Local 837" p2="L.I.U.N.A., Local 506" post.rptr="5905" pre.rptr="2012" ref.id="Id1bb7de0fd9711f0a4fba71a4de76a24" title="L.I.U.N.A., Local 837 v. L.I.U.N.A., Local 506">L.I.U.N.A., Local 837 v. L.I.U.N.A., Local 506, 2012 CarswellOnt 5905 <ital>sub nom</ital> Imperial Precast Corp., 2012 CanLII 25677</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the board expressed concern about using the successorship provisions to effect a &ldquo;transfer of bargaining rights&rdquo; from a predecessor to a successor local of the same union. Despite the lack of opposition from the employer, either of the party union locals, or any evidence of impropriety, the board ordered a vote held. In <ital>UFCW, Local 1006A v. Universal Protection Service</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">22</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW, Local 1006A" p2="Universal Protection Service of Canada Corp." post.rptr="9026" pre.rptr="2024" ref.id="I630ea7f0004e11f18d8bc20fedb2bc44" title="UFCW, Local 1006A v. Universal Protection Service of Canada Corp.">UFCW, Local 1006A v. Universal Protection Service of Canada Corp., 2024 CarswellOnt 9026, 2024 CanLII 55073 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> by contrast, the board held that it was unnecessary to order a vote where two locals of the same union had merged and no affirmative reason had been given not to make a successor declaration.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.trace="20260211" date.updated="20260211">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Boad has indicated that where a long time has passed since a merger was operationalized in practice, that might weigh against holding a vote.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">23</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UFCW, Local 1006A" p2="Universal Protection Service of Canada Corp." post.rptr="9026" pre.rptr="2024" ref.id="I630ecf00004e11f18d8bc20fedb2bc44" title="UFCW, Local 1006A v. Universal Protection Service of Canada Corp.">UFCW, Local 1006A v. Universal Protection Service of Canada Corp., 2024 CarswellOnt 9026, 2024 CanLII 55073 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:39</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">British Columbia</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The British Columbia Labour Relations Board has ruled that, in a union successorship application, it will require a majority verdict by the union members. However, it will not apply this requirement in a strict or unbending fashion. In <ital>Fashion Floor</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">B.C.L.R.B. (No. B77/94), at p. 8. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="B.C. Rail Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="96" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2003&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cb95168cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="B.C. Rail Ltd. (Re)">B.C. Rail Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;2003&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 96</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Napier Personal Care Home Ltd." p2="" post.rptr="B75/96" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1996&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cbbc208cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Napier Personal Care Home Ltd.">Napier Personal Care Home Ltd., &lsqb;1996&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B75/96</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="B.C. Ferry and Marina Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="17" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8cbbc218cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="B.C. Ferry and Marina Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re)">B.C. Ferry and Marina Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; 1 Can. L.R.B.R. (2d) 17 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Board held:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">It is only required that the unions provide relevant information to members about the merger, that the members are given a reasonable opportunity to express their views, and that the majority of the members support the merger.</para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Applying this criteria in <ital>Griffin and VMREW (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Griffin and VMREW (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="60" pre.rptr="29" ref.id="Id8cbbc228cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Griffin and VMREW (Re)">Griffin and VMREW (Re) (1995), 29 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 60 (B.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="College of New Caledonia" p2="PPWC, Local 29" post.rptr="2721" pre.rptr="2009" ref.id="Id8cbbc238cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="College of New Caledonia v. PPWC, Local 29">College of New Caledonia v. PPWC, Local 29, 2009 CarswellBC 2721, &lsqb;2009&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 190</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Laurel Point Inn" p2="Hotel, Restaurant &amp; Culinary Employees &amp; Bartenders Union, Local 40" post.rptr="258" pre.rptr="109" ref.id="Id8cbbc248cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Laurel Point Inn v. Hotel, Restaurant &amp; Culinary Employees &amp; Bartenders Union, Local 40">Laurel Point Inn v. Hotel, Restaurant &amp; Culinary Employees &amp; Bartenders Union, Local 40 (2005), 109 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 258 (B.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>, and <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Various Employers and General Worker&apos;s Union, Local 1 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="356" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cbe3308cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Various Employers and General Worker&apos;s Union, Local 1 (Re)">Various Employers and General Worker&apos;s Union, Local 1 (Re), &lsqb;2000&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 356</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the British Columbia Board determined that it would look for &ldquo;five factors&rdquo;: a vote with sufficient notice, organized by a duly elected executive, available to all members, with relevant information made available to all, and a <ital>bona fide</ital> opportunity for the employees to express their views and democratically demonstrate their wishes. Based on these factors, the Board approved a union successorship where the members of the merging union had voted by a show of hands, the vote had been a single vote across the union as opposed to by individual bargaining unit, the vote criteria had been set at 50&percnt; plus one in the absence of a merger provision in the constitution, the membership turnout for the vote had been low, and the union newspaper had promoted only the views of those in support of the merger.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Also see: <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Servantage Services Corp. and CAW-Canada, Local 3000 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="B241/2007" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2007&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cbe3318cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Servantage Services Corp. and CAW-Canada, Local 3000 (Re)">Servantage Services Corp. and CAW-Canada, Local 3000 (Re), &lsqb;2007&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B241/2007</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Various Employers (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="102" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2002&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cbe3328cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Various Employers (Re)">Various Employers (Re), &lsqb;2002&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 102</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, in <ital>Wexxar Packaging Inc. and IAMAW, Lodge No. 692 (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Wexxar Packaging Inc. and IAMAW, Lodge No. 692 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="119" pre.rptr="130" ref.id="Id8cc0a408cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Wexxar Packaging Inc. and IAMAW, Lodge No. 692 (Re)">Wexxar Packaging Inc. and IAMAW, Lodge No. 692 (Re) (2006), 130 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 119 (B.C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the British Columbia Board applied the five factors from <ital>Griffin</ital> and found that the union merger process was flawed. Relevant information had been withheld from some members, and the membership lacked a <ital>bona fide</ital> opportunity to express their views. Using its discretion, the Board ordered a supervised vote of the union membership under its auspices.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The British Columbia Board has stated that, even where two unions cooperate on a merger or successorship, it will want to be satisfied that the employees affected have had an adequate opportunity to express their views and demonstrate their support, based on labour relations common sense.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="General Workers Union of Canada, Local 1" p2="Mulligan" post.rptr="3787" pre.rptr="2002" ref.id="Id8cc0a418cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="General Workers Union of Canada, Local 1 v. Mulligan">General Workers Union of Canada, Local 1 v. Mulligan, 2002 CarswellBC 3787, &lsqb;2002&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 102</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> For example, a healthy membership turnout for a merger vote, which followed a timely and sufficient notice that provided for a real opportunity for the members to democratically demonstrate their wishes, satisfies the requirements of the Act.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Assn. of Britco Export Packers Ltd. and UFCW, Local 1518, Re" p2="Assn. of Britco Export Packers Ltd. and UFCW, Local 1518, Re" post.rptr="3721" pre.rptr="2014" ref.id="Id8cc0a428cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Assn. of Britco Export Packers Ltd. and UFCW, Local 1518, Re">Assn. of Britco Export Packers Ltd. and UFCW, Local 1518, Re, 2014 CarswellBC 3721, &lsqb;2014&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 212</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>Vancouver (City)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Vancouver (City)" p2="Vancouver (City)" post.rptr="B36/96" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1996&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc0a438cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Vancouver (City)">Vancouver (City), &lsqb;1996&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B36/96</ref>
                          </cite>. See also <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Vancouver Island Housing Association for the Physically Disabled (1976)" p2="Vancouver Island Housing Association for the Physically Disabled (1976)" post.rptr="B449/95" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1995&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc0a448cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Vancouver Island Housing Association for the Physically Disabled (1976)">Vancouver Island Housing Association for the Physically Disabled (1976), &lsqb;1995&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B449/95</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the employees affected by the proposed successorship application were neither given adequate information about the proposal, nor were they provided with a genuine choice in the balloting. Consequently, the British Columbia Board declared the successorship agreement between the two unions to be ineffectual.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The requirement for majority support applies equally to merging locals within the same parent union. Without a genuine expression of majority support, an internal merger will fail. The British Columbia Labour Relations Board, in <ital>United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1998</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1998" p2="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1998" post.rptr="77" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc0a458cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1998">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1998, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 77</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> ruled that it would not treat its standard requirement for a vote any differently where the merging locals belong to the same parent union. It stated: &ldquo;It is difficult to conceive of any distinction in principle that would require a vote where there is a merger between separate organizations, but no vote in a merger between other entities where there is an overarching relationship between the locals through the parent-local connection.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1998" p2="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1998" post.rptr="77" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc31508cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1998">United Brotherhood of Carpenters and Joiners of America, Local 1998, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B.D. No. 77</ref>
                        </cite>, at para. 82.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In holding a successorship vote, a union is entitled to insist that its own internal procedures be followed. In <ital>McBride Manor Society</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="McBride Manor Society" p2="McBride Manor Society" post.rptr="B19/96" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1996&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc31518cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="McBride Manor Society">McBride Manor Society, &lsqb;1996&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B19/96</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> local members had organized a merger vote on their own, which the union disallowed and replaced with an authorized vote. The British Columbia Board dismissed the challenge by the local members to the union&apos;s actions. In the same case, local members had also objected to the preference stated by the local president for one contending union over another. The Board held that a union officer is entitled to express her or his views in a successorship or merger vote, provided that the expression of the preference does not amount to coercion.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:40</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Federal</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="20211028">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In the federal jurisdiction, the Canada Labour Relations Board has stated that it requires as a matter of policy that any successorship application under the <ital>Canada Labour Code</ital> establish that a majority of the affected membership has approved the merger.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW and Unitel Communications Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="59" pre.rptr="86" ref.id="Id8cc31528cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW and Unitel Communications Inc. (Re)">IBEW and Unitel Communications Inc. (Re) (1991), 86 di 59 (C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Federal Public Service Labour Relations and Employment Board has taken a similar approach. In <ital>Parliamentary Protective Service</ital>, the Public Service Board, having previously approved the merger of several bargaining units of employees working for Parliament, was satisfied that the merger and succession steps taken by the successor union met the statutory requirements, and a vote of the membership was not necessary.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="House of Commons Security Services Employees Association" p2="Parliamentary Protective Service" post.rptr="1540" pre.rptr="2020" ref.id="I5e03f430370e11ec80e0c0b599422da1" title="House of Commons Security Services Employees Association v. Parliamentary Protective Service">House of Commons Security Services Employees Association v. Parliamentary Protective Service, 2020 CarswellNat 1540, 2020 FPSLREB 46 (Can. F.P.S.L.R.E.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>. At para. 13, the Board wrote: &ldquo;The Board has confirmed to its satisfaction that the assemblies held by SSEA and SPSEA were regularly constituted, that the number of people necessary for a quorum for each group was present at each general assembly, and that the members of both bargaining units had been duly informed about the general assembly and about the vote that would take place on merging the two associations. In light of these facts and of the unanimous vote in both general assemblies in favour of creating the UOPPS, the Board believes that the UOPPS&apos;s representation of the new unit constituted by the merger of the SSEA and the SPSEA reflects the will of the majority of members of these units. As a result, the Board considers that holding a vote will not be necessary.&rdquo;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:41</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Discretion to Order Vote</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A related issue to the criteria for employee support is the discretionary power that most labour boards in Canadian common law jurisdictions have to order a representative vote in proceedings involving successorship declarations.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Canada Labour Code</ital>, R.S.C. 1985, c. L-2, s. 43(3); <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, R.S.A. 2000, c. L-1, s. 49(2); <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, R.S.B.C. 1996, c. 244, s. 37(2); <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital>, R.S.N.L. 1990, c. L-1, s. 94(2); <ital>Industrial Relations Act</ital>, R.S.N.B. 1973, c. I-4, s. 58(2); <ital>Trade Union Act</ital>, R.S.N.S. 1989, c. 475, s. 32(2); <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, S.O. 1995, c. 1, Sch. A, s. 68(2); <ital>Labour Act</ital>, R.S.P.E.I. 1988, c. L-1, s. 40(2).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Section 49(2) of the Alberta <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital> is a representative provision: &ldquo;Before issuing a declaration under subsection (1), the Board may &hellip; conduct any votes that it considers appropriate.&rdquo; In practice, however, it is a discretionary power that labour boards have been adverse to exercise in union successorship applications.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">An early expression of labour board reluctance to apply this discretionary authority is found in <ital>Lambton Health Unit (Re)</ital>, where the Ontario Board stated that it would not order a representative vote in the absence of evidence that the affected employees were misled or deceived by the merger proceedings.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Lambton Health Unit (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="543" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1975&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc58608cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Lambton Health Unit (Re)">Lambton Health Unit (Re), &lsqb;1975&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July 543</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The British Columbia Board stated a similar policy, although in affirmative language: &ldquo;&hellip; Board policy is not to conduct a representation vote itself when its investigations disclose that the union&apos;s internal procedures have given the affected employees an adequate opportunity to express their views and to reach a majority verdict on the transfer&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="17" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8cc58618cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re)">British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; 1 Can. L.R.B.R. 17 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 20.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The board rationale for this cautious use of their representative vote power in union successorship matters flows from the general tribunal approach that mergers by trade unions are essentially an internal procedure, that the union membership is the best judge of what is necessary to effect a merger with another union, and that a board should be reluctant to second-guess a judgment of the members in these matters.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,007" pre.rptr="94" ref.id="Id8cc58628cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">RWDSU, Local 414 and Great Atlantic &amp; Pacific Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re) (1993), 94 C.L.L.C. &para;16,007 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Jaeger Machine Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1082" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1983&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc58638cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Jaeger Machine Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">Jaeger Machine Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1983&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July 1082</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Accordingly, labour boards have refused to order representa<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-94" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;95" folio="5-93" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;93" ?>tive votes in union successorship proceedings where there had been no misunderstanding and no interference with the members&apos; true wishes,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Jaeger Machine Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1082" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1983&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc7f708cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Jaeger Machine Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re)">Jaeger Machine Co. of Canada Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1983&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July 1082</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Food Corp. Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="636" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1983&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc7f718cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Food Corp. Ltd. (Re)">Food Corp. Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1983&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 636</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Emery Industries Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="316" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1980&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc7f728cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Emery Industries Ltd. (Re)">Emery Industries Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1980&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar. 316</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Peel County Bd. of Education (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="623" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1973&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cc7f738cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Peel County Bd. of Education (Re)">Peel County Bd. of Education (Re), &lsqb;1973&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 623</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where some employees had expressed second thoughts on the wisdom of a merger after the internal vote,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Bethesda Service Employees Assn. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="631" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1985&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cca6808cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Bethesda Service Employees Assn. (Re)">Bethesda Service Employees Assn. (Re), &lsqb;1985&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 631</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where the trade union was found not to be a bargaining agent and not to hold bargaining rights at the time of the merger,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Tiercon Corp." p2="Workers United Ontario Council" post.rptr="396" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cca6818cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council">Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 396</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Waterloo (City) and OPEIU (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="28" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2000&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cca6828cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Waterloo (City) and OPEIU (Re)">Waterloo (City) and OPEIU (Re), &lsqb;2000&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Jan/Feb 28, &lsqb;2000&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 231</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where a significant minority of the union members signed a petition to oppose the merger,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Laurel Point Inn" p2="Hotel, Restaurant &amp; Culinary Employees &amp; Bartenders Union, Local 40" post.rptr="258" pre.rptr="109" ref.id="Id8cca6838cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Laurel Point Inn v. Hotel, Restaurant &amp; Culinary Employees &amp; Bartenders Union, Local 40">Laurel Point Inn v. Hotel, Restaurant &amp; Culinary Employees &amp; Bartenders Union, Local 40 (2005), 109 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 258 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> or where the necessary constitutional steps to effect a merger had not been taken.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1252" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1985&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cca6848cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re)">L.M.L. Foods Inc. (Re), &lsqb;1985&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1252. At p. 1269</ref>
                        </cite>, the Board wrote: &ldquo;Unless the relevant constitution so provided, a representation vote could not repair any inadequacy in the steps taken by the predecessor or the successor to obtain the necessary authorization.&rdquo;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)</ital>, the Ontario<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">&lsqb;Footnote omitted.&rsqb;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Board declined to order a representative vote even where there was no internal vote among the membership to canvass their support.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="491" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1988&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cca6858cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)">IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 491, affd &lsqb;1990&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 1365 sub nom. IBEW, Local 594 v. IBEW, Local 586 (Div. Ct.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> On judicial review the Ontario Divisional Court ruled that, had the Board ordered a representative vote, it &ldquo;would have been tantamount to an unwarranted interference in the internal workings&rdquo; of the union. It also held that the Board&apos;s decision not to order a vote did not deny anyone&apos;s rights to belong to the union of one&apos;s choice, and was therefore not an infringement of the Charter of Rights.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="491" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1988&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cccd908cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)">IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 491, affd &lsqb;1990&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 1365 sub nom. IBEW, Local 594 v. IBEW, Local 586 (Div. Ct.), at p. 1365 (Div. Ct.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> In <ital>Cadillac Fairview Corporation Limited and CAW, Local 414 (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Cadillac Fairview Corporation Limited and CAW, Local 414 Re" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-044" pre.rptr="2001" ref.id="Id8cccd918cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Cadillac Fairview Corporation Limited and CAW, Local 414 Re">Cadillac Fairview Corporation Limited and CAW, Local 414 Re (2001), 2001 C.L.L.C. &para;220-044, &lsqb;2001&rsqb; OLRB Rep. January/February 23</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Ontario Board denied a request by an employer to order a representation vote where the choices would be the new union, the predecessor union, or no union at all. The Board held that, since the predecessor union was not interested in continuing to represent the bargaining unit, this option was not viable. As well, the Board refused to create a situation where union representation could be extinguished during the life of a collective agreement. It stated that:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; If there is a proper, internal democratic process which results in the approval of a merger and successorship by the affected union members (as occurred at the union&apos;s conventions during 1999), there are no allegations of deception or confusion, and the identity of the union in its new iteration is clear, then there would appear to be no good labour relations reason why a representation vote should be held of the individuals in each affected bargaining unit.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="Cadillac Fairview Corporation Limited and CAW, Local 414 Re" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-044" pre.rptr="2001" ref.id="Id8ccf4a08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Cadillac Fairview Corporation Limited and CAW, Local 414 Re">Cadillac Fairview Corporation Limited and CAW, Local 414 Re (2001), 2001 C.L.L.C. &para;220-044, &lsqb;2001&rsqb; OLRB Rep. January/February 23</ref>
                            </cite>, at para. 20.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Similarly, the Ontario Board has accepted the principle that a union may transfer its bargaining unit rights to another union by agreement, outside of the &ldquo;open period&rdquo; in the legislation, provided that majority support through a proper representation vote in the affected bargaining units has been demonstrated. In this case,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CAW-Canada and S.E.I.U., Local 220 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="137" pre.rptr="76" ref.id="Id8ccf4a18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CAW-Canada and S.E.I.U., Local 220 (Re)">CAW-Canada and S.E.I.U., Local 220 (Re) (2001), 76 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 137</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Board supervised the vote following a specific request of the two unions involved.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Canada Industrial Relations Board has ordered a representative vote in circumstances where there was considerable confusion as to which union was the proper successor. The Board&apos;s decision in <ital>CBRT &amp; GW and Canadian National Railway Co. (Re)</ital> affirms the statutory principle that a board has the authority to supersede a union&apos;s constitution where it is not satisfied that the effected employees&apos; true wishes on a merger proposal have been expressed through the internal procedures.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CBRT &amp; GW and Canadian National Railway Co. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="140" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1980&rsqb; 3" ref.id="Id8ccf4a28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CBRT &amp; GW and Canadian National Railway Co. (Re)">CBRT &amp; GW and Canadian National Railway Co. (Re), &lsqb;1980&rsqb; 3 Can. L.R.B.R. 140 (Can.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Similarly, in British Columbia, the Labour Relations Board ordered a secret ballot to be held where the incumbent and the successor unions had failed to adequately involve the affected employees in the decision-making process for a proposed change of the bargaining agent.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">17</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Prince George Regional Community Care Society" p2="" post.rptr="B130/96" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1996&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ccf4a38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Prince George Regional Community Care Society">Prince George Regional Community Care Society, &lsqb;1996&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B130/96</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Where a union constitution required a secret ballot for any merger vote, the Alberta Labour Relations Board has said in <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-96" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;97" folio="5-95" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;95" ?>
                  <ital>UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">18</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-069" pre.rptr="97" ref.id="Id8ccf4a48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)">UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re) (1997), 97 C.L.L.C. &para;220-069, 38 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 10 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> that this means:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; that the voter secretly marks his choice and, so long as the voter does not reveal that information to others, it is not capable of being ascertained by subsequent examination of the ballots, nor can the voter be required to state that information.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-069" pre.rptr="97" ref.id="Id8cd1bb08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)">UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re) (1997), 97 C.L.L.C. &para;220-069, 38 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 10 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                            </cite>, C.L.R.B.R., at p. 30.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Alberta Board went on to hold that the lack of a screen at the voting tables to conceal the ballot choice did not amount to an invalid vote: &ldquo;&lsqb;a&rsqb; mere possibility of abuse is not enough to cause us to overturn a vote like this one&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">20</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-069" pre.rptr="97" ref.id="Id8cd1bb18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)">UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re) (1997), 97 C.L.L.C. &para;220-069, 38 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 10 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, p. 31.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Nor was the Board concerned that the merger ballot was a three-way run-off vote, rather than a yes-or-no proposal: &ldquo;&lsqb;i&rsqb;f the language of the constitution permits more than one rational interpretation or approach, we prefer to allow the union the freedom to choose among them&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">21</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-069" pre.rptr="97" ref.id="Id8cd1bb28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)">UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re) (1997), 97 C.L.L.C. &para;220-069, 38 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 10 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>, p. 33.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The most considered discussion on a board&apos;s discretion to order a vote in a union successorship situation was by the Alberta Labour Relations Board in <ital>Staff Nurses Assn. of Alberta and Rivercrest Lodge Nursing Home Ltd.</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">22</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Staff Nurses Assn. of Alberta and Rivercrest Lodge Nursing Home Ltd.</ital> (1995), Alta. L.R.B.R. 83.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> In this case, a small union of twelve members had attempted to merge with a much larger union. While there was a 6-0 membership vote in favour of the merger, the process was flawed by the lack of proper notice of the meeting and its purpose. The Board endorsed a substantial compliance approach to ordering a vote. Where defects in the merger process are only minor, the Board stated that it would likely approve the transfer of bargaining rights on the basis of the union-conducted vote. However, if the procedural defects in a merger process are so serious as to affect the integrity of the consummation, and the Board is also satisfied that a board-conducted vote would cure all deficiencies and no third-party rights (such as those of a parent union) are involved, it ruled that it would be prepared to apply its discretion under s. 47(2) of the Code to order a vote, rather than dismiss the application. Here, the Alberta Board weighed several factors in light of the purposes of the Code &mdash; including the fact that the union involved was small and unsophisticated, the procedural flaws were innocent, the initial vote had indicated general employee support for the merger, and dismissing the application would only prolong workplace instability &mdash; and ordered a board-conducted vote. Given the fundamental effect that the merger would have upon the smaller union as an organization, the Alberta Board required that the merger vote be approved by two-thirds of those voting.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">F</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Substantial Completion; Opposition and the Motive for Merger</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.A Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Merger or amalgamation</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:42</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Substantial Completion</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In addition to liberally applying their discretion as to whether a union has complied with its constitutional requirements to affect a merger, labour boards have also shown flexibility in the degree of completeness they will require of the merger process before they will issue a successorship declaration. Where all that is left to finalize a merger are matters of a technical nature, the Ontario Board has stated that it will not delay issuing a declaration.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">This &ldquo;substantial completion&rdquo; rule has been long-standing.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton" p2="Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton" post.rptr="&para;16,261" pre.rptr="63" ref.id="Id8cd42c08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton">Hydro-Electric Commission of the City of Hamilton (1962), 63 C.L.L.C. &para;16,261 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Board has indicated that it will seek to determine the procedural importance of an incomplete step, and whether the remaining step or steps belong, in essence, to the substance of the merger or simply to its mechanics. For example, in <ital>Brockville (City) (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Brockville (City) (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="76" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1979&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cd42c18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Brockville (City) (Re)">Brockville (City) (Re), &lsqb;1979&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Feb. 76</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> two locals of the same parent union were seeking a declaration that one local was the successor of the other. A number of steps had yet to be completed, including the fact that the local executives and bank accounts had not been combined, the business affairs of the two locals were conducted separately, and local membership meetings still voted on their business separately, although they held their meetings together. The Board was satisfied that the required internal approval had been obtained and the necessary constitutional procedures had taken place. &ldquo;Once these matters of substance have been established, then a declaration may issue under section &lsqb;63 &mdash; now s. 68&rsqb; even though certain <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-98" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;99" folio="5-97" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;97" ?>mechanics of the transaction still remain to be done.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Brockville (City) (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="76" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1979&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cd42c28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Brockville (City) (Re)">Brockville (City) (Re), &lsqb;1979&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Feb. 76</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 80.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Since, for all intents and purposes, the merger had been completed, the Board held that for it to delay making a declaration until these mechanics were completed would introduce &ldquo;an element of unnecessary formality&rdquo; in its authority under the merger provision in the legislation.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In later rulings, the Board has declared a successorship in circumstances where the predecessor union had not transferred its assets to the successor,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Peerless Plastics Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="848" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cd69d08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Peerless Plastics Ltd. (Re)">Peerless Plastics Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Sept. 848</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> where the name, constitution and by-laws of the successor union had not been approved until six months after the application had been filed with the Board,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Coca-Cola Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="658" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cd69d18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Coca-Cola Ltd. (Re)">Coca-Cola Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 658</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and where the president of the parent union that was merging two of its locals had yet to sign the amended by-law approving the merger.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="491" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1988&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cd69d28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)">IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 491, affd &lsqb;1990&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 1365 sub nom. IBEW, Local 594 v. IBEW, Local 586 (Div. Ct.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> In this last decision, the Board accepted a commitment from the parent union that the uncompleted steps would be accomplished, and expressed a concern about making the merger process unnecessarily technical:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">In our view, it is abundantly clear from the documentary evidence before us that the International President had every intention to extend Local 586&apos;s jurisdiction in anticipation of this merger or amalgamation. We cannot, in these circumstances, attach any particular importance to whether he actually signed a document approving the amendment of the Bylaws. The Constitution permitted the amendment and the political will to amend was obviously present. It would be unduly formalistic for this Board to question the amendment of the Bylaws on the basis of the absence of evidence as to the affixing of the International President&apos;s signature.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="491" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1988&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cd90e08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re)">IBEW, Local 594 and J.S.H. Mueller Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 491, affd &lsqb;1990&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 1365 sub nom. IBEW, Local 594 v. IBEW, Local 586 (Div. Ct.), at p. 495 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                            </cite>.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.A Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Merger or amalgamation</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:43</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Opposition and the Motive for Merger</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Ontario Board has taken the position that its role in a successorship declaration proceeding is limited to ensuring that a claimed merger, amalgamation or transfer of jurisdiction has occurred. Thus, while the Board will receive arguments relating to the constitutional process followed by the unions involved in a reorganization, it has stated that it is beyond its authority under s. 68 to determine the effect of the new affiliation on the bargaining relationship between the employer and the union involved, or on the relationship that the affected union members may have with their new union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." p2="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." post.rptr="1413" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cd90e18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc.">M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc., &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1413</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Deseronto Public Utilities Commission" p2="" post.rptr="248" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1977&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cd90e28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Deseronto Public Utilities Commission">Deseronto Public Utilities Commission, &lsqb;1977&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Apr. 248</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In <ital>Deseronto Public Utilities Commission (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." p2="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." post.rptr="1413" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cd90e38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc.">M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc., &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1413</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the employer had expressed concerns before the Board that the union merger might have a detrimental industrial relations impact. After reviewing its jurisdiction under the successorship provision, the Board stated that:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">Issues such as those raised by the respondent are not relevant to the legal question as to whether or not a purported merger, amalgamation or transfer of jurisdiction has in fact been properly carried out. Further, we are of the view that once the Board is satisfied that a merger, amalgamation or transfer of jurisdiction has been effected, it would be contrary to the purpose and intent of section &lsqb;63 &mdash; now s. 68&rsqb; for the Board to refrain from making a declaration to that effect on the basis of the respondent&apos;s concerns. This is not to say that certain aspects of the bargaining relationship between the parties may not undergo change. However, the law does recognize that union mergers, amalgamations and transfers of jurisdiction may occur such that a successor union may replace or substitute for a predecessor union in a bargaining relationship with an employer. Necessarily following from this is the fact that an employer may find itself in a bargaining relationship with a union different from that it is accustomed to dealing with, and that this &ldquo;new&rdquo; union may possibly adopt policies and procedures different from that of its predecessor.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." p2="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." post.rptr="1413" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cd90e48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc.">M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc., &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1413</ref>
                              </cite>, at p. 249. See also <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173" p2="" post.rptr="244" pre.rptr="27" ref.id="Id8cdb7f08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173">Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173 (1995), 27 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 244, &lsqb;1995&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July 954</ref>
                            </cite>, where the Ontario Board pointed to the extremely restricted role that employers have in a union successorship application under s. 68.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Notwithstanding the Board&apos;s policy, employers and dissident employees have raised industrial relations concerns from time to time about a union merger at board proceedings, and they have requested that a successorship declaration not be issued because of its projected impact upon bargaining, or on internal bargaining unit relations. In various cases before the Ontario Board, it has been argued that a s. 68 declaration should be <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-100" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2021;6;30" nep=";;101" folio="5-99" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="2" sp=";;99" ?>refused because this had been the second merger in six years,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="National Sewer Pipe Ltd." p2="National Sewer Pipe Ltd." post.rptr="781" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1967&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cdb7f18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="National Sewer Pipe Ltd.">National Sewer Pipe Ltd., &lsqb;1967&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Nov. 781</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> because of a concern that a merger may result in higher wages or a province-wide master agreement,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." p2="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." post.rptr="1413" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cdb7f28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc.">M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc., &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1413</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Deseronto Public Utilities Commission" p2="" post.rptr="248" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1977&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cddf008cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Deseronto Public Utilities Commission">Deseronto Public Utilities Commission, &lsqb;1977&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Apr. 248</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> because a prior merger between two parent unions was constitutionally tainted,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Emery Industries Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="316" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1980&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cddf018cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Emery Industries Ltd. (Re)">Emery Industries Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1980&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Mar. 316</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> because there were concerns about the quality of representation by the predecessor union,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc." p2="" post.rptr="1413" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cddf028cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc.">M.L.S. Cable Installations Inc., &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1413</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> because the reinforced union local might then apply to consolidate the various bargaining units into a single unit,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173" p2="" post.rptr="244" pre.rptr="27" ref.id="Id8cddf038cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173">Famous Players Inc. and IATSE, Local 173 (1995), 27 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 244, &lsqb;1995&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July 954</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and because a group of skilled employees wanted to be carved out of the bargaining unit that was having its bargaining rights transferred.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="CUPE and Hamilton-Wentworth Roman Catholic Separate School Board Professional Staff Assn. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="49" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1990&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cddf048cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="CUPE and Hamilton-Wentworth Roman Catholic Separate School Board Professional Staff Assn. (Re)">CUPE and Hamilton-Wentworth Roman Catholic Separate School Board Professional Staff Assn. (Re), &lsqb;1990&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Jan. 49</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> In each case, the Board turned back the arguments and issued a successorship declaration. As long as the unions involved in the reorganization have taken the proper steps to form themselves into a new union, the views of the employer or any concerned employees on the impact of the re-affiliation are legally irrelevant to the statutory process.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Alberta Labour Relations Board has granted standing to employers to participate as a party in tribunal proceedings involving a union merger application.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para; 220-069" pre.rptr="97" ref.id="Id8cddf058cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re)">UMA and Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees (Re) (1997), 97 C.L.L.C. &para; 220-069, 38 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 10 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, it has also put limitations on the ability of an employer to interfere with the merger plans of a union. In <ital>Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees v. Banff Centre</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees" p2="Banff Centre" post.rptr="1245" pre.rptr="1997" ref.id="Id8ce06108cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees v. Banff Centre">Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees v. Banff Centre, 1997 CarswellAlta 1245</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Board considered a letter that the employer had written to its unionized employees while the incumbent union was attempting to complete a merger with another union. It found that the natural effect of the employer&apos;s letter was intended to sow dissension in the union membership, and to incite resistance to the merger proposal. The Alberta Board held that the:</para.text>
                <quote.block date.updated="0">
                  <quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">&hellip; efforts by a union to amend its constitution and to dissolve the organization through merger into another labour organization are internal workings of the union that are protected by &lsqb;s.146, the unfair labour practice provisions of the Act&rsqb;. Indeed, it is difficult to imagine anything that is more profoundly a matter of union &ldquo;administration&rdquo; than a decision whether the union will continue to exist as an independent entity.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                                <ref p1="Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees" p2="Banff Centre" post.rptr="1245" pre.rptr="1997" ref.id="Id8ce06118cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees v. Banff Centre">Assn. of Banff Centre Support Employees v. Banff Centre, 1997 CarswellAlta 1245</ref>
                            </cite>, p. 7.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </quote.para>
                </quote.block>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The British Columbia Labour Relations Board has indicated that the statutory right to seek a representative vote to determine whether union members have had an adequate opportunity to express their views concerning a proposed union merger belongs to the union members. Accordingly, an employer has no legislated right to represent the interests of its employees, and it will not be granted standing by a labour relations board to challenge the validity of a merger on such grounds.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Laurel Point Inn" p2="Hotel, Restaurant &amp; Culinary Employees &amp; Bartenders Union, Local 40" post.rptr="258" pre.rptr="109" ref.id="Id8ce06128cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Laurel Point Inn v. Hotel, Restaurant &amp; Culinary Employees &amp; Bartenders Union, Local 40">Laurel Point Inn v. Hotel, Restaurant &amp; Culinary Employees &amp; Bartenders Union, Local 40 (2005), 109 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 258 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Where a union local representing a number of different bargaining units votes as a whole to join a new union, an individual bargaining unit that voted against the merger may nevertheless be compelled to accept the outcome of the vote. In <ital>Pacific Press Ltd. v. CEP</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <ital>Pacific Press Ltd. v. CEP</ital>, (1994), B.C.L.R.B. No. B276/94. See also <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Griffin and VMREU (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="60" pre.rptr="29" ref.id="Id8ce2d208cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Griffin and VMREU (Re)">Griffin and VMREU (Re) (1995), 29 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 60 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the British Columbia Labour Relations Board did hold that a bargaining unit which is a member of a larger local could, in the right circumstances, be severed from a union merger without creating significant problems. However, where the merger vote had been conducted with the explicit understanding that the local would remain intact whatever the outcome of the ballot, the Board ruled that to sever the dissenting bargaining unit would create considerable prejudice.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">When a union local, that is part of a joint certification, wishes to merge with another union, the British Columbia Labour Relations Board has permitted the successorship application to proceed. In <ital>British Columbia Transit and CAW-Canada, Local 111 (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="British Columbia Transit and CAW-Canada, Local 111 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;220-016" pre.rptr="99" ref.id="Id8ce2d218cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="British Columbia Transit and CAW-Canada, Local 111 (Re)">British Columbia Transit and CAW-Canada, Local 111 (Re) (1998), 99 C.L.L.C. &para;220-016 (B.C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> it rejected the arguments of dissident members that the application was, in reality, an attempt to carve out a bargaining unit from an existing unit, or, alternatively, an out-of-time raid. The Board stated that simple opposition to a merger did not transform a successorship application into a raid. Unlike a raid application, which is governed by a distinct <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-102" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2022;12;31" nep=";;103" folio="5-101" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;101" ?>part of the <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, a successorship application involves an inquiry into the good faith of the merger, the wishes of the affected members, and the appropriateness of the bargaining unit.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block date.trace="20221125" date.updated="20221125" toc.include="y">
          <designator date.updated="0">G</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Timeliness of a Successorship Declaration; Trade Union Status</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.A Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Merger or amalgamation</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:44</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Timeliness of a Successorship Declaration</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A request by a union for a successorship declaration arising out of a union reorganization is, unlike a raid situation, not subject to statutory time-limits. In other words, a successor union can seek a board declaration at any time after the constitutional consummation of the merger or transfer. The British Columbia Labour Relations Board has held that the consensual nature of a union reorganization places it in an entirely different statutory light than a raid, primarily because of their differential impact upon industrial relations stability.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="17" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8ce2d228cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re)">British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; 1 Can. L.R.B.R. 17 (B.C.)</ref>
                          </cite>. For a discussion on the distinction between a raid and a merger in labour legislation, see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="British Columbia Transit and CAW-Canada, Local 111 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para; 220-016" pre.rptr="99" ref.id="Id8ce2d238cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="British Columbia Transit and CAW-Canada, Local 111 (Re)">British Columbia Transit and CAW-Canada, Local 111 (Re) (1998), 99 C.L.L.C. &para; 220-016 (B.C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The Canada Labour Relations Board has explained the reason for the lack of a time-limit in terms of both industrial relations purposes and practicality. In <ital>National Bank of Canada, Sillery, Quebec and Retail Clerks&apos; Union, Local 503 (Re)</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="National Bank of Canada, Sillery, Quebec and Retail Clerks&apos; Union, Local 503 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="202" pre.rptr="2" ref.id="Id8ce54308cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="National Bank of Canada, Sillery, Quebec and Retail Clerks&apos; Union, Local 503 (Re)">National Bank of Canada, Sillery, Quebec and Retail Clerks&apos; Union, Local 503 (Re) (1982), 2 C.L.R.B.R. (N.S.) 202</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the Board stated that the purpose of s. 43 of the <ital>Canada Labour Code</ital> is &ldquo;to recognize that a union, which is the bargaining agent, can undergo change, such as a merger or amalgamation or a transfer of jurisdiction, just like the business that may be sold during the life of a collective agreement. In this situation, the industrial stability resulting from the binding nature of the collective agreement is not threatened.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="National Bank of Canada, Sillery, Quebec and Retail Clerks&apos; Union, Local 503 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="202" pre.rptr="2" ref.id="Id8ce54318cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="National Bank of Canada, Sillery, Quebec and Retail Clerks&apos; Union, Local 503 (Re)">National Bank of Canada, Sillery, Quebec and Retail Clerks&apos; Union, Local 503 (Re) (1982), 2 C.L.R.B.R. (N.S.) 202</ref>
                        </cite>, at p. 211.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The Canada Board went on to rule that it would be just as unrealistic to expect a union merger or transfer to be completed within specific time-limits as it would be to impose such time-limits on a business sale under the Code. The Ontario Labour Relations Board has also stated that an application for a successorship declaration after the consummation of a union merger or transfer will not be restricted by time-limits.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="National Sewer Pipe Ltd." p2="" post.rptr="781" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1967&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ce54328cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="National Sewer Pipe Ltd.">National Sewer Pipe Ltd., &lsqb;1967&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Nov. 781</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">However, timeliness can directly affect the consequences of a union successorship application. In <ital>Siemens Building Technologies Inc.</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Siemens Building Technologies Inc." p2="" post.rptr="672" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1999&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ce54338cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Siemens Building Technologies Inc.">Siemens Building Technologies Inc., &lsqb;1999&rsqb; Alta. L.R.B.R. 672</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> a construction union had not utilized its bargaining rights in southern Alberta for 14 years. When its successor union applied for a province-wide bargaining certificate, the Alberta Labour Relations Board ruled that this length of time was an extreme delay. Consequently, while the merger itself could go forward, the successor union was not granted bargaining rights for that part of the province.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">The legislative provisions that govern union mergers and successorships do not create a new institutional or legal identity such as to extend statutory limitation periods. In <ital>Fullowka v. Slezak</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Fullowka" p2="Slezak" post.rptr="21" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2002&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ce7b408cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Fullowka v. Slezak">Fullowka v. Slezak, &lsqb;2002&rsqb; N.W.T.J. No. 21 (S.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Northwest Territories Supreme Court ruled that a wrongful death action against a union local was time-barred, and the limitations clock did not re-start merely because the defendant local had subsequently merged with another union.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.trace="20211028" date.updated="20211028" date.revised="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.C Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:44.50</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Bankruptcy and Union Successorship</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">In the event of a corporate bankruptcy, and a substantial lapse of time between the prior operations of a business and the possibilities of the business being reopened, the Newfoundland and Labrador Labour Relations Board held in <ital>Canada Fluorspar</ital> that a union&apos;s claim for successorship union rights was dependent on whether the recommencement of the company operations were closely enough related to the prior operations.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20221125">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="UA, Local 740 and Canada Fluorspar (NL) Inc., Re" post.rptr="349" pre.rptr="2020" ref.id="I0519f000696611ed811ed82e72705593" title="UA, Local 740 and Canada Fluorspar (NL) Inc., Re">UA, Local 740 and Canada Fluorspar (NL) Inc., Re, 2020 CarswellNfld 349, 2020 NLLRB 10 (N.L. L.R.B.), reversed 2022 CarswellNfld 113, 2022 NLCA 21</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.A Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Merger or amalgamation</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">5:45</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Trade Union Status</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">For a union to be granted a successorship declaration, it must be able to establish that both it and the predecessor union would qualify as a &ldquo;trade union&rdquo; under the governing labour <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-104" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2022;12;31" nep=";;105" folio="5-103" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;103" ?>legislation.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="20221125">
                        <ital>Canada Labour Code</ital>, R.S.C. 1985, c. L-2, s. 43; Alta., <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, R.S.A. 2000, c. L-1, s. 49; B.C., <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital>, R.S.B.C. 1996, c. 244, s. 37; Man., <ital>The Labour Relations Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. 1987, c. L10, s. 55; Nlfd., <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital>, R.S.N.L. 1990, c. L-1, s. 94; N.B., <ital>Industrial Relations Act</ital>, R.S.N.B. 1973, c. I-4, s. 58; N.S., <ital>Trade Union Act</ital>, R.S.N.S. 1989, c. 475, s. 32; Ont., <ital>Labour Relations Act, 1995</ital>, S.O. 1995, c. 1, s. 68; P.E.I., <ital>Labour Act</ital>, R.S.P.E.I. 1988, c. L-1, s. 40; Sask., <ital>The Saskatchewan Employment Act</ital>, S.S. 2013, c. S-15.1, s. 6-21.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> If a predecessor union did not possess a written constitution at the time of the merger, it would fail to meet one of the board prerequisites to be a trade union, and a successorship declaration would not be issued.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Tiercon Corp." p2="Workers United Ontario Council" post.rptr="396" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2011&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ce7b418cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council">Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council, &lsqb;2011&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 396</ref>
                          </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Dufferin-Peel Roman Catholic Separate School Board" p2="Dufferin-Peel Roman Catholic Separate School Board" post.rptr="821" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1976&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ce7b428cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Dufferin-Peel Roman Catholic Separate School Board">Dufferin-Peel Roman Catholic Separate School Board, &lsqb;1976&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Dec. 821</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Similarly, if the applicant claiming to be the successor union did not have a constitution, it too would not meet the trade union definition, and no declaration would be issued.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Elgin Motors Co. Ltd." p2="Elgin Motors Co. Ltd." post.rptr="256" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1973&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8ce7b438cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Elgin Motors Co. Ltd.">Elgin Motors Co. Ltd., &lsqb;1973&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 256</ref>
                          </cite>. For an example of where a labour board was satisfied that the successor union met the prerequisites, including the existence of a constitution, see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="17" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1978&rsqb; 1" ref.id="Id8ce7b448cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re)">British Columbia Ferry and Marine Workers&apos; Union and BCGEU (Re), &lsqb;1978&rsqb; 1 Can. L.R.B.R. 17 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">A related question that sometimes arises is the change, if any, to the legal status of a local in the aftermath of a merger or transfer. Labour boards have ruled that a local can be a trade union for the purposes of labour legislation, and a transfer of jurisdictional rights from a parent to a local is within the scope of the statutory successorship provisions.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">See, for example, <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="National Bank of Canada, Sillery, Quebec and Retail Clerks&apos; Union, Local 503 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="202" pre.rptr="2" ref.id="Id8cea2508cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="National Bank of Canada, Sillery, Quebec and Retail Clerks&apos; Union, Local 503 (Re)">National Bank of Canada, Sillery, Quebec and Retail Clerks&apos; Union, Local 503 (Re) (1982), 2 C.L.R.B.R. (N.S.) 202</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> When two parent unions merge, it does not cause the local to lose its pre-existing status as a trade union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Toronto Printing Pressmen &amp; Assistants&apos; Union, Local 10 and Hartley Gibson Co. Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1517" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1986&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cea2518cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Toronto Printing Pressmen &amp; Assistants&apos; Union, Local 10 and Hartley Gibson Co. Ltd. (Re)">Toronto Printing Pressmen &amp; Assistants&apos; Union, Local 10 and Hartley Gibson Co. Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1986&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1517</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Where a local that had been granted trade union status breaks away from a parent union, the Ontario Board has ruled that the local retains its status, even though the parent had, over the years, signed the collective agreement. The Board found in this case that &ldquo;disaffiliation did not constitute a fundamental change in the organization sufficient to affect its status as a trade union within the meaning of the Act and its identity as an organization has continued unchanged apart from its formal name&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Coca-Cola Ltd." p2="" post.rptr="658" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cea2528cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Coca-Cola Ltd.">Coca-Cola Ltd., &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 658</ref>
                          </cite>, at p. 666. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Rehab Express and Canadian Office and Professional Employees Union, Local 550 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="697" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2005&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cec9608cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Rehab Express and Canadian Office and Professional Employees Union, Local 550 (Re)">Rehab Express and Canadian Office and Professional Employees Union, Local 550 (Re), &lsqb;2005&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. July/August 697</ref>
                          </cite>, and <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canadian Office and Professional Employees Union, Local 343 and Ontario (Environmental Commissioner) (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="47423" pre.rptr="2004" ref.id="Id8cec9618cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canadian Office and Professional Employees Union, Local 343 and Ontario (Environmental Commissioner) (Re)">Canadian Office and Professional Employees Union, Local 343 and Ontario (Environmental Commissioner) (Re), 2004 CanLII 47423 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The inactivity of a local after it has merged with another local under the same parent may not necessarily affect its status as a trade union, as long as it continues to meet the constitutional and board policy requirements of a trade union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Knob Hills Farms and UFCW, Local 206 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="149" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1989&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cec9628cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Knob Hills Farms and UFCW, Local 206 (Re)">Knob Hills Farms and UFCW, Local 206 (Re), &lsqb;1989&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. Feb. 149</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Can a successor union acquire the bargaining rights of a predecessor union that held only a voluntarily recognized collective agreement through the governing labour relations legislation? The British Columbia Labour Relations Board had ruled, in <ital>Rissling Contractors Ltd.</ital> in 1998, that the successorship provisions in the <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital> had restricted its authority to inquire into a purported successorship only when the transfer involved a <ital>certified</ital> bargaining agent.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Rissling Contractors Ltd. and Construction &amp; Specialized Workers&apos; Union, Local 1611 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="221" pre.rptr="47" ref.id="Id8cec9638cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Rissling Contractors Ltd. and Construction &amp; Specialized Workers&apos; Union, Local 1611 (Re)">Rissling Contractors Ltd. and Construction &amp; Specialized Workers&apos; Union, Local 1611 (Re) (1998), 47 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 221 (B.C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>. Also see <ital>German Canadian Benevolent Society of B.C.</ital> (1994), B.C.L.R.B. No. B163/94, upholding B72/94.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Subsequently, the <ital>Labour Relations Code</ital> was amended to expressly permit voluntarily recognized bargaining units to be eligible for a successorship process with another union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">R.S.B.C. 1996, c. 244, s. 37(1).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Although a breakaway provincial union council may acquire trade union status after its secession from a parent, that does not, by itself, entitle the new formation to become the bargaining agent successor of the parent. In <ital>IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU" p2="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU" post.rptr="161" pre.rptr="64" ref.id="Id8cef0708cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU">IUBAC, Locals 6, 7 &amp; 25 and BACU (2000), 64 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 161 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                          </cite>. Also see <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="George and Asmussen Ltd." p2="George and Asmussen Ltd." post.rptr="50" pre.rptr="89" ref.id="Id8cef0718cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="George and Asmussen Ltd.">George and Asmussen Ltd. (2002), 89 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 50 (O.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the Ontario construction labour relations legislation had created a single, province-wide craft-based bargaining unit, with bargaining rights vested in the international parent. The provincial council, with the support of most, but not all, of the locals, broke away from the parent and sought successorship status. However, the Ontario Labour Relations Board ruled that, as long as the international parent remained a party to the provincial collective agreement, the bargaining rights could not be transferred under the Act to the new formation.</para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart backFolio="5-106" backBlank="n" releaseDate="2022;12;31" nep=";;107" folio="5-105" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;105" ?>
                <para.text date.updated="0">Labour relations boards will be vigilant when examining the trade union legal credentials of a &ldquo;homegrown&rdquo; employee association seeking to supplant an established union in a workplace. This is primarily to guard against employer-dominated employee associations. However, a board will not require perfection. Thus, when a home-grown association has established that it has substantially complied with the legal procedures to become a trade union, and can demonstrate that it is a viable bargaining agent, then, despite some procedural irregularities in its formation, a labour board will not stand in the way of ordering a representation vote.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Teachers&apos; Federation Employees&apos; Union and IWA-Canada, Local Union Number 1-3567 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="16" pre.rptr="107" ref.id="Id8cef0728cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Teachers&apos; Federation Employees&apos; Union and IWA-Canada, Local Union Number 1-3567 (Re)">Teachers&apos; Federation Employees&apos; Union and IWA-Canada, Local Union Number 1-3567 (Re) (2004), 107 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 16 sub nom. BCTF and IWA-Canada, Local 1-3567, application for reconsideration dismissed 107 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 23 (B.C.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Similarly, a labour relations board will not impose a higher onus on a homegrown association to prove that it has met the statutory requirements for trade union status because its application for a bargaining unit certificate arose out of a raid. Nor will it conduct a more searching inquiry into the status of a homegrown association simply because the applicant association was leaving one CLC-affiliated union and intended to eventually join another CLC-affiliated union.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="241" pre.rptr="105" ref.id="Id8cef0738cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re)">Canada Bread Co. and UFCW, Local 1518 (Re) (2004), 105 C.L.R.B.R. (2d) 241 (B.C.L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">One of the legal consequences of a merger between a general union and a craft union would be to bestow upon the new parent union the particular craft union status held by one of the merging unions. Thus, the new parent union would be able to claim the requisite craft union status, which it did not possess before, in circumstances where only a craft union is statutorily entitled to represent a specific category of workers. The Ontario Labour Relations Board has stated that: &ldquo;The bargaining right and privileges possessed by the successor union are no greater than, or less than the rights and privileges formerly possessed by the predecessor union.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="GreenField Ethanol Inc." p2="CEP, Local 2003" post.rptr="687" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2009&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cf17808cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="GreenField Ethanol Inc. v. CEP, Local 2003">GreenField Ethanol Inc. v. CEP, Local 2003, &lsqb;2009&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 687</ref>
                        </cite>, at para. 23.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote>
                </para.text>
              </para>
              <para date.updated="0">
                <para.text date.updated="0">What rights does a successor union acquire over such matters as grievances that were advanced to arbitration by the predecessor union? In <ital>Kensington Place Retirement Residence and United Brotherhood of Retail, Food, Industrial and Service Trades International Union (Aberia), Re</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                            <ref p1="Kensington Place Retirement Residence and United Brotherhood of Retail, Food, Industrial and Service Trades International Union (Aberia), Re" p2="" post.rptr="124" pre.rptr="230" ref.id="Id8cf17818cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Kensington Place Retirement Residence and United Brotherhood of Retail, Food, Industrial and Service Trades International Union (Aberia), Re">Kensington Place Retirement Residence and United Brotherhood of Retail, Food, Industrial and Service Trades International Union (Aberia), Re (2013), 230 L.A.C. (4th) 124, 2013 CarswellOnt 1555 (Ont. Arb.)</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> the arbitrator held that the predecessor union continued to have carriage of a dismissal grievance, but granted the successor union intervener status in the hearings, ruling that it had a &ldquo;direct and substantial&rdquo; interest in the matter because it will be responsible for implementing the arbitration award.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
  </analytical.level.body>
</analytical.level>
<analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
    <metadata field="classification">
      <value>LAB.I.2.a Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Trade unions&mdash;Legal status</value>
      <value>LAB.I.5.e.i.A Labour and employment law&mdash;Labour law&mdash;Bargaining rights&mdash;Successor rights&mdash;Successor union&mdash;Merger or amalgamation</value>
    </metadata>
  </metadata.block>
  <front date.updated="0">
    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
      <designator date.updated="0">VI</designator>
      <name date.updated="0">Change of Name</name>
    </outline.name.block>
  </front>
  <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
    <section.block>
      <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
        <section.front date.updated="0">
          <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
            <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
            <designator date.updated="0">5:46</designator>
            <name date.updated="0">Generally</name>
          </outline.name.block>
        </section.front>
        <section.body date.updated="0">
          <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
            <para.text date.updated="0">When a labour board certifies an entity as bargaining agent, bargaining rights and other rights under the act are thereby granted to a particular organization. That organization is identified by name. Unlike the case for businesses, there is no centralized register that lists and regulates the official names of unions and their locals. When a union changes its name, as a result of a merger, change of affiliation, internal reorganization or otherwise, what impact will that have on any bargaining rights it may have had?</para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="1">
            <para.text date.updated="0">
              <trace.deleted/>
              <footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">2.5 <ital>Trade Union Act</ital>, R.S.N.S. 1989, c. 475, s. 28(1)(a).</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
              <trace>Three</trace> Canadian jurisdictions <trace.deleted/>
              <trace>&ndash;</trace> Newfoundland<trace>,</trace>
              <footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <ital>Labour Relations Act</ital>, R.S.N.L. 1990, c. L-1, s. 95.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
              <trace.deleted/> Saskatchewan<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="20221125">
                      <ital>The Saskatchewan Employment Act</ital>, S.S. 2013, c. S-15.1<trace>, s 6-21.</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>
                    </para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
              <trace> and Nova Scotia2.5</trace> &mdash; contain explicit provisions in their respective statutes that enable bargaining rights to continue when a union changes its name<trace> or affiliation</trace>
              <trace.deleted/>. However, while other jurisdictions do not have similar statutory provisions, it would appear that the mere fact that a trade union changed its name<trace.deleted/> would not <trace.deleted/> threat<trace>en</trace>
              <trace.deleted/> its<trace> status as a union eligible to hold bargaining</trace>
              <trace.deleted/> rights<trace>, nor its existing bargaining relations</trace>
              <trace.deleted/>.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="20211028">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="United Food and Commercial Workers (UFCW) Canada Local 832" p2="Staff of the Non-Public Funds, Canadian Forces" post.rptr="4847" pre.rptr="2020" ref.id="I5e0812e0370e11ec80e0c0b599422da1" title="United Food and Commercial Workers (UFCW) Canada Local 832 v. Staff of the Non-Public Funds, Canadian Forces">United Food and Commercial Workers (UFCW) Canada Local 832 v. Staff of the Non-Public Funds, Canadian Forces, 2020 CarswellNat 4847, 2020 FPSLREB 92 (Can. F.P.S.L.R.E.B.)</ref>
                      </cite>; <trace>
                        <cite type="case">
                          <ref p1="Universal Workers Union, L.I.U.N.A., Local 183" p2="Coughlan Homes Inc." pre.rptr="2000" post.rptr="12437" title="Universal Workers Union, L.I.U.N.A., Local 183 v. Coughlan Homes Inc.">Universal Workers Union, L.I.U.N.A., Local 183 v. Coughlan Homes Inc., 2000 CanLII 12437 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                      </cite>; </trace>
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. and IWA, Local 1-207 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para; 16,038" pre.rptr="88" ref.id="Id8cf17828cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. and IWA, Local 1-207 (Re)">Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. and IWA, Local 1-207 (Re) (1988), 88 C.L.L.C. &para; 16,038 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Food Corp. Ltd." p2="" post.rptr="636" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1983&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cf17838cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Food Corp. Ltd.">Food Corp. Ltd., &lsqb;1983&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 636</ref>
                    </cite>. For Quebec, see the <ital>Professional Syndicates Act</ital>, R.S.Q., c. S-40, s. 10.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
              <trace> The Ontario Labour Relations Board has made clear that a successorship application need not be sought and will not be granted where a union&rsquo;s identity continues unchanged under a different name.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">3.5 <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                          <ref p1="Tiercon Corp." p2="Workers United Ontario Council" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2010&rsqb;" post.rptr="495" title="Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council">Tiercon Corp. v. Workers United Ontario Council, &lsqb;2010&rsqb; O.L.R.D. No. 495, 2010 CanLII 74590 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                      </cite> at para. 127.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote>3.5 </trace>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="1">
            <para.text date.updated="0">
              <trace>However, where an organization claims union status or bargaining rights previously allocated to an organization bearing a different name, labour boards must be vigilant that the organization&rsquo;s identity continues unchanged.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">39</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">3.6 As the Ontario Labour Relations Board stated in <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                          <ref p1="BFCSD" p2="Coca-Cola Ltd." post.rptr="411" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1976&rsqb; 1" title="BFCSD and Coca-Cola Ltd. (Re)">BFCSD and Coca-Cola Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1976&rsqb; 1 Can. L.R.B.R. 411 (Ont. L.R.B.)</ref>
                      </cite>, at p. 415 &ldquo;A change of name, especially, is an indication of a change of identity, suggesting that an organization no longer desires to be identified with its constitutional roots.&rdquo;</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote>3.6</trace>Labour boards will generally examine the new union to determine whether <trace.deleted/>
              <trace>a</trace> merger<trace>or other reorganization</trace> has resulted in any substantive or structural change sufficient to alter the legal identity of the union.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">40</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">The question cannot be resolved, however, by merely examining changes in form and structure. These changes are only signs that a fundamental change of identity may have taken place within the existing constitutional structure. Of greater importance are the circumstances underlying these changes in form and structure. It must be determined whether the underlying circumstances point to a fundamental change of identity, or merely to minor alterations in form and structure.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
              <trace>3.7</trace> In <ital>Food Corp. Ltd.</ital>,<trace.deleted/> a union local that had undergone several mergers with other locals in the same union and whose <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart releaseDate="2022;12;31" backBlank="y" folio="5-107" nep="5001" hyphenated="n" releaseNumber="5" sp=";;107" ?>parent had recently changed its name applied to the Ontario board for certification of a new bargaining unit. The membership cards it submitted to the Board contained the previous name of the local. However, despite changes to the local&apos;s name, size and responsibilities, the Board concluded that there had been no substantive change to its legal identity. It also noted that there had been no actual evidence that any of the employees who signed membership cards had been confused as to which union they were joining. In a subsequent case on similar facts, the Board found that there had been a &ldquo;constitutional continuity&rdquo; indicative of a continuation of identity. It went on to state that: &ldquo;&lsqb;T&rsqb;here is no reason in law why &lsqb;a union&rsqb; cannot carry on business under a name other than that legal name, so long as it does not do so for any improper purpose.&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Toronto Printing Pressmen &amp; Assistants&apos; Union, Local 10 and Hartley Gibson Co. Ltd. (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="1517" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1986&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cf65a08cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Toronto Printing Pressmen &amp; Assistants&apos; Union, Local 10 and Hartley Gibson Co. Ltd. (Re)">Toronto Printing Pressmen &amp; Assistants&apos; Union, Local 10 and Hartley Gibson Co. Ltd. (Re), &lsqb;1986&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. 1517</ref>
                      </cite>, at p. 1524. See also <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Coca-Cola Ltd." p2="" post.rptr="658" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1987&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cf65a18cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Coca-Cola Ltd.">Coca-Cola Ltd., &lsqb;1987&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 658</ref>
                      </cite>; <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Consumers Distributing Co. Ltd." p2="" post.rptr="509" pre.rptr="&lsqb;1981&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cf65a28cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Consumers Distributing Co. Ltd.">Consumers Distributing Co. Ltd., &lsqb;1981&rsqb; O.L.R.B. Rep. May 509</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote> Likewise, the Alberta Labour Relations Board has ruled that where the constitutional identity of the union local has remained the same, despite constitutional changes in the parent&apos;s identity, a change of name will not trigger the need for a successorship declaration.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <trace>
                        <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                          <ref p1="Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. and IWA, Local 1-207 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="&para;16,038" pre.rptr="88" ref.id="Id8cf65a38cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. and IWA, Local 1-207 (Re)">Zeidler Forest Industries Ltd. and IWA, Local 1-207 (Re) (1988), &lsqb;1988&rsqb; Alta. L.R.B.R. 182, 88 C.L.L.C. &para;16,038 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                        </cite>
                    </trace>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="0">
            <para.text date.updated="0">A union local that has dissolved its legal and institutional relationship with its former parent union may seek to change its name on the bargaining unit certification and on any other legal documents, but it may not rely upon the name change to disturb outstanding obligations under the collective agreement or under any judicial or tribunal awards.<footnote date.updated="0">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re)" p2="" post.rptr="B95/2009" pre.rptr="&lsqb;2009&rsqb;" ref.id="Id8cf65a48cd711eba591da50b3d8f767" title="Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re)">Limo Jet Gold Express Ltd. and PSAC, Local 05/21081 (Re), &lsqb;2009&rsqb; B.C.L.R.B. No. B95/2009</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
            <para.text date.updated="0">The Alberta Labour Relations Board has allowed a union with certifications in multiple provinces to change the name on its certifications to the name of a local, and to operate using that name in Alberta, notwithstanding that the local has no separate legal existence.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">8 <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="International Union of Operating Engineers, Local Union No. 955" p2="Steel River SICIM Pipeline Ltd." pre.rptr="2025" post.rptr="111" title="International Union of Operating Engineers, Local Union No. 955 v. Steel River SICIM Pipeline Ltd.">International Union of Operating Engineers, Local Union No. 955 v. Steel River SICIM Pipeline Ltd., 2025 ALRB 111, 2025 CarswellAlta 2098 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                    </cite>.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
          <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
            <para.text date.updated="0">Where a union files for certification using the name of an existing union already operating in another province but claims to be &ldquo;a completely separate and stand-alone union,&rdquo; the new union will be required to meet all the qualifications for certification <ital>de novo</ital>.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">
                      <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                        <ref p1="Canada West Construction Union" p2="Morgan Earthworks Ltd." pre.rptr="2017" post.rptr="13550" title="Canada West Construction Union v. Morgan Earthworks Ltd.">Canada West Construction Union v. Morgan Earthworks Ltd., 2017 CanLII 13550, 2017 CarswellAlta 441 (Alta. L.R.B.)</ref>
                      </cite>
                    </para.text>
                  </para>
                </footnote.body>
              </footnote>
            </para.text>
          </para>
        </section.body>
      </section>
    </section.block>
  </analytical.level.body>
</analytical.level>
</chapter.body>
</chapter>